\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.742479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00047b9f12ba7c87e8e3e3574e7d572fc8c72adf78d35d44bb742f3fd3b83671","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theorem_size_bias_bound of arXiv:2606.03442, 'Stein's method in network analysis'] Let~$X$ be a non-negative random variable with finite variance~$\sigma^2$, and let~$X^{(s)}$ be constructed on the
 same probability space as~$X$, having the size bias distribution of~$X$;
 write $\nu^2 := \mathbb{E}[(X^{(s)}-X)^2]$. Let~$\mathscr{F}$ be any sub-sigma field with respect to the probability
 space on which $X$ and $X^{(s)}$ are defined.
 Then,
    \begin{align*}
        d_{{\mathrm{BW}}}\bigl(&\mathcal{L}(\sigma^{-1}  (X- \mathbb{E}[X])),N(0,1)\bigr) \\
        &\ \le\  \sigma^{-2} \mathbb{E}[X] \bigg( 4 (\mathbb{V}[\mathbb{E}[X^{(s)}-X \, \vert \, \mathscr{F} ] ]  )^{1/2} +
        \frac{7\nu}{\sigma} \bigg( \frac{\nu}{2} + (\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{V}[X \, \vert \, \mathscr{F}]]^{1/2} \bigg) \bigg).
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.972683+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_000dcfcfe2b773b132a238e3b19d1560d4a6d4cf5e1cf0e539fcb1a2c71aa1f0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:defBmu of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For every $\tau\in\mathcal{T}_*$, we define 
    \begin{equs}
        \text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau)\eqdef\Big\{(\tau_1,\tau_2)\in\mathcal{T}^2 :\exists\,\underline{\tau}_i\in\tau_i\in\mathcal{T}\,,\;\exists\,n\in N(\underline{\tau}_1)\,,\;\underline{\tau}_2\graft_n\underline{\tau}_1\in\tau\Big\}
    \end{equs}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.746507+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0012cfcaeba746404eb54ca74a2e99f61b548ed6243eff4742cbc2e695a3afe9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:vector-q-mass-identity-paper of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] For every \(q>0\) and every \(n\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{\lvert u \rvert=n}L_u^q\right]=\rho(q)^n,
\end{equation}
where the identity is understood in the extended sense, and where
\(
\rho(q)^0=1
\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00134c863a800ef3f1cb361cb8d1eb07233b3f1d5e7423ef729ed0c4c187e4e8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:fokker_superpo of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and let ${\boldsymbol\mu}:[0,\infty)\to \mathcal{M}_{+}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ (with ${\boldsymbol\mu}_0 = \nu$) be such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto {\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big],  \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto {\boldsymbol\sigma} {\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d , \overline{\boldsymbol\mu}),
\end{equation}
with $\overline{\boldsymbol\mu}$ as in Notation (not:measure_dt_dx of the paper).

Then:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] If $X$ is a solution to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, with $[X_t]={\boldsymbol\mu}_t $ for $t\geq0$, then ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a weak solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$.
\item[(ii)] [Superposition] Under the additional assumption that 
\begin{equation}
\Big[ t \mapsto \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \Big( |{\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)| + \| {\boldsymbol\sigma}{\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t) \| \Big) {\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d x)\Big], \  \big[ t \mapsto {\boldsymbol\mu}_{t} (\mathbb{R}^d) \big]  \in L^{\infty}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) ,
\end{equation}

if ${\boldsymbol\mu}$ is a (weak) solution to NL-FP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$, 
 then there exists a solution $X$ to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ such that $ [X_t] = {\boldsymbol\mu}_t $.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.649058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00152b42f7a290b779d3bd2c718792f3c4b04f8e34b6e3e40ed935b852c66f97","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem LastOne of arXiv:2411.13926, 'Random walks on random walks:\\ non-perturbative results in high dimensions'] Let $Y_k^{(1)} \in I^{(1)}$ be the random lift of $y_k \in I^{(k)}$. Then 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}(Y_k^{(1)}=x) = P^{RW}(X \in x \;|\;  D_k), \quad x \in  I^{(1)}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:38:13.640000+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_001ce081dc4b4c3104d2b1d30094d7f00f7af1373371f97f40152667c87e3f24","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2609.00690, 'Boundary behavior and optimal pointwise H\"older exponent of the total local time of $(1+\'] Let $V^\lambda$ be the bounded nonnegative distributional solution to \eqref{V lambda} which vanishes at infinity. Then we have
	\begin{equation}
		V^\lambda(x)=\bigg[\frac{\beta}{\sqrt{\beta+2}}|x|+\bigg(\frac{2}{\lambda\sqrt{\beta+2}}\bigg)^{\frac{\beta}{2+\beta}}\bigg]^{-\frac{2}{\beta}},\quad x\in\mathbb R,\ \lambda>0.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.578871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0022a926c4304dacb5149ae7b865212c443887973d2ae2cf8f9008f89b5ba249","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 54 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$. For every $\beta\geq 0$, and every $x,y\in \mathbb Z^d$,
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf P_{\beta}^{\emptyset,\emptyset}[x,y\in \mathbf{C}_{\n_1+\n_2}(0)]\leq \langle \sigma_0\sigma_x\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_x\sigma_y\rangle_{\beta}\langle \sigma_y\sigma_0\rangle_{\beta}+(x\Leftrightarrow y),
\end{equation}
where $(x\Leftrightarrow y)$ denotes the same term with $x$ and $y$ inverted. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.754251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0024a41e4cd1d2784b7c8ade47609a23af03f731fad5df1377483b88df436266","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:intro-lbm-spectrum of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For every $0<\gamma<2$, almost surely, $A_h$ has compact resolvent, and its
