{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.162672+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00338a3661f62d473270a2b3e03727db93be3f8107c82d4dd975922bfd05fde9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 (Theorem 2.3 of \cite{zhang2016central}) of arXiv:2604.17302, 'Elephant random walk with attributed steps and extractions of random sizes'] Suppose $\{Z_{n}\}$ converges to the constant $\theta^{*}$ almost surely, and that Assumptions~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_1} and \eqref{gen_assump_dist_3} are satisfied. Suppose $\rho>1/2$, and that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\delta_{i}=o\left(\sqrt{n}\right) \text{ almost surely}.
\end{equation}
Then 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{n}\left(Z_{n}-\theta^{*}\right)\convd N(0,\Sigma),
\end{equation}
where, with $\Gamma$ is the matrix mentioned in Assumption~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_3}, we set
\begin{equation}
\Sigma=\int_{0}^{\infty}\exp\left\{\left(J_{G}(\theta^{*})+I_{d}/2\right)u\right\}\Gamma\left[\exp\left\{\left(J_{G}(\theta^{*})+I_{d}/2\right)u\right\}\right]^{t}du.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.770915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0051a1adbe08c96111922dea142fa5738df798885969717d0b48a352e228a7b7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 43 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same parameters as in Definition \ref{Def.ParametersBulk}. Fix $m \in \mathbb{N}$, $t_1, \dots, t_m \in [0, \infty)$ with $t_1 < t_2 < \cdots < t_m$, and set $\mathcal{T} = \{t_1, \dots, t_m\}$. We also define for $t \in \mathcal{T}$ the quantity $T_{t} = T_t(N) =  \lfloor t N^{2/3} \rfloor$ and the lattice $\Lambda_t(N) = a_t(N) \cdot \mathbb{Z} + b_t(N)$, where 
\begin{equation}
a_t(N) = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3}, \mbox{ and } b_t(N) = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3} \cdot \left( - h_1 N - p_1 T_{t} \right).
\end{equation}

We let $\mathbb{P}_N$ be the Pfaffian Schur process from Definition \ref{Def.SchurProcess}. Here, we assume that $M = M(N)$ is sufficiently large so that $M \geq T_{t_m}$. If $(\lambda^0, \dots, \lambda^M)$ have law $\mathbb{P}_N$, we define the random variables
\begin{equation}
X_i^{j,N} = \sigma_1^{-1} N^{-1/3} \cdot \left( \lambda_i^{T_{t_j}} - h_1 N - p_1 T_{t_j}  - i\right) \mbox{ for } i \in \mathbb{N} \mbox{ and } j \in \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.030740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00584744a55aaa43f7ba0aff456f824b5012486af4d00fd5faea87c3c9a6812d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma proptfund1 of arXiv:2609.03909, 'Conic Pseudo-Finslerian Mechanical Systems'] Let  $\mathcal{U} \subset TM \setminus 0$ be an open subset invariant under positive scalar multiplication and let$ \mathcal L$ be a Lagrangian, of class $C^2$ on $\mathcal U$, which is positively $2$-homogeneous. Then its fundamental tensor satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] $g_v$ is positively homogeneous of degree 0, that is, $g_{\lambda v}=g_{v}$ for any $\lambda >0;$
    \item[(ii)] $g_v(v,v)=2\mathcal L(v);$
    \item[(iii)] $g_v(v,w)=\ell_v(w)=d\mathcal L_v(w)$ for any $w\in T_{\pi(v)}M.$
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.751027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0058f4388ea941ef1e2987ae1fd8773987e009ee40a549d6f07a03749f33eda6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 24 of arXiv:2608.18834, 'The Parabolic Anderson Model's Total Mass at Small Times: Geometry, Fluctuations, and Reno'] Suppose that Assumption \ref{Assumption} holds, and
let $\ka>0$ be fixed.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\rho>0$, then
\begin{align}

\lim_{\th\to0}\mbf E\big[\tfrac{\ka^2}{2}\be^\th_1(B)\big]=\frac{\ka^2\mf b}{(1+\rho)\rho}.
\end{align}
\item If $\rho=0$, then
there exist bounded and continuous functions $\msf b_i:[0,\infty)\to\mbb R$, $(i=1,2)$,
such that for every $\th>0$, one has
\begin{align}

\mbf E\big[\tfrac{\ka^2}{2}\be^\th_1(B)\big]
=
\begin{cases}
\ka^2\mf b\,\ell(\th)+\ka^2\msf b_1(\th)&H_0<1,\\
\ka^2\mf b\,(1+\th)\log(1+1/\th)+\ka^2\msf b_2(\th)&H_0=1,
\end{cases}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}

\ell(\th)=\frac{1}{2\Ga(H_0)\Ga(1-H_0)}\int_0^{2\pi}\left|\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}\right|^{2H_0-1}
\Ga_0\Big(\th\big(|\sin\theta|+|\cos\theta|\big)\Big)\d\theta,
\end{align}
and $\Gamma_0$ denotes the zero-parameter upper incomplete Gamma function, i.e.,
\[\Gamma_0(\th)=\int_{\th}^\infty \mr e^{-z}z^{-1}\d z,\qquad \th>0.\]
\item 
If $\rho<0$,
then there exists a bounded and continuous function $\msf z:(0,\infty)\to\mbb R$ such that
for every $\th>0$,
\begin{align}

\mbf E\big[\tfrac{\ka^2}{2}\be^\th_1(B)\big]=\ka^2\mf d\,\th^{\rho}+\,\ka^2\msf z(\th).
\end{align}
Moreover,
$\msf z$ satisfies the limit
\begin{align}

\lim_{\th\to0}\msf z(\th)=
\begin{cases}
\frac{\mf b}{(1+\rho)\rho}&H_0>1/2,\\
0&H_0=1/2.
\end{cases}
\end{align}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.365832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00608d9ab72e3b613f1bd7f2e58870d8815373539b9e80d1c1c06a64da634b28","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:zmombd of arXiv:2606.02472, 'Correlated uniform attachment trees'] Let $\ell, j, M, n, u$ be such that $u< M+1 \leq e^\ell<e^j \leq n$, and recall the definitions at~(equation eq:Ukey of the paper) and~(equation eq:Dkey of the paper).
There is a universal constant $C_\star$   such that the random variable
