{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.728970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01fe24192af1168b31fca1715d4e1a2c44e6528698294a9125b5a3a545e6e871","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Propolemiko of arXiv:2412.09290, 'Asymptotics of Harish-Chandra transform and infinitesimal freeness'] Let $l > 1$ be a real number. Let $A$ be a random Hermitian matrix of size $N$ and
  assume that for every $r \in \mathbb{N}$ one has
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{N \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{N^l} \log \mathbb{E} [H \text{} C
    (x_1, \ldots, x_r, 0^{N - r} ; \lambda_1 (A), \ldots, \lambda_N (A))] =
    \sum_{i = 1}^r \Psi (x_i), 
  \end{equation}
  where $\Psi$ is a smooth function in a complex neighborhood of $0$ and the above
  convergence is uniform in a complex neighborhood of $0^r$. Then the random measure $N^{- 1} \sum_{i = 1}^N \delta \left( N^{- l}
  \lambda_i (A) \right)$ converges, as $N \rightarrow \infty$, in probability, in the
  sense of moments to the Dirac measure $\delta \left( \Psi' (0) \right)$. In more detail, this means that any moment of the former measure converges to the corresponding moment of the latter measure in probability.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.345689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_020118fb3d71d125cb2ff5d1a033403880f6d25135fb99f202461d08fed3a404","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eqItoWeakFor of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Suppose that $X_0 \in L_{\mathcal{F}_0}^2(\Omega ; H)$,  $ \phi(\cdot) \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^2\left(0, T ; \mathcal{V}^*\right)$, and $\Phi(\cdot) \in$ $L_{\mathbb{F}}^2 ( 0, T ; H) $.
