{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.740575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04c716477a4189afbc0e055227980b28fe90470f8f5fddba72a6a6ccf27a4308","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:flowblowdown of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\Phi$ be a smooth flow on a closed $3$-manifold $M$. A \textbf{blowup} of $(M, \Phi)$ along a closed orbit $\gamma$ of $\Phi$ is a pair $(M^\circ,\Phi^\circ)$, where $M^\circ$ is a compact $3$-manifold with torus boundary and $\Phi^\circ$ is a flow on $M^\circ$ tangent to the boundary, together with a map $\pi:M^\circ \to M$ such that 
        \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exist smooth local coordinates $(r, \theta, \phi)$ around $\partial M^\circ$ (where $r$ parametrizes the normal direction, $\phi$ parametrizes the longitudinal direction, $\theta$ parametrizes the meridional direction) and cylindrical coordinates $(r,\theta,\phi)$ in a neighborhood of $\gamma$ (where $\gamma$ lies along $\{r=0\}$), such that $\pi$ has the form 
        $$\pi(r,\theta,\phi) = (f(r),\theta,\phi)$$
        for some smooth function $f:[0,1]\to [0,1]$. In particular, $\pi$ sends $\partial M^\circ$ to $\gamma$, collapsing leaves of a linear foliation on $\partial M$ to points.
        \item $\pi$ restricts to a smooth orbit equivalence between $(\interior M^\circ, \Phi^\circ\vert_{\interior M^\circ})$ and $(M, \Phi\vert_{M\setminus \gamma})$.
        \end{enumerate}
    We say that $(M, \Phi)$ is a \textbf{blowdown} of $(M^\circ, \Phi^\circ)$ along the slope $\phi=const$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.284653+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04cf05b2c9b962c450b1580853725ecf52658a81477648cd9daf03a52789ca59","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme corr:plusdefonctions of arXiv:2603.21634, 'Individual-based stochastic model with unbounded growth, birth and death rates: a tightnes'] We work under the assumptions of Theorem~(theo:convergence of the paper) and consider a subsequence of the sequence $\left(\left(\mu^{K}_{t},R^{K}_{t}\right)_{t \in [0,T]}\right)_{K \in \mathbb{N}^{*}}$ that converges in law towards $(\mu^{*}_{t},R^{*}_{t})_{t \in [0,T]}$ in $\mathbb{D}([0,T], (\mathcal{M}_{\omega}(\mathbb{R}^{*}_{+}),v) \times [0,R_{\max}])$. Let $\varphi : (t,x) \in \mathbb{R}^{+} \times \mathbb{R}^{*}_{+} \mapsto \varphi_{t}(x)$ be continuous and such that
\begin{align*}
\exists C>0, \forall x>0, \quad \sup_{t \in [0,T]} |\varphi_{t}(x)| \leq C(1+x+ \omega(x)).
\end{align*}
Then, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left( \sup_{t \in [0,T]} \left| \langle \mu^{*}_{t}, \varphi_{t} \rangle \right|\right) <+ \infty.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:00.620129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04dfd14cf7bd55429b216ad97dc66fb5c17eee61ddb4927403cfc201ccd4e196","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2511.00764, 'Further Developments on Stochastic Dominance for Convex\\ Combinations of Infinite-Mean Ra'] {\rm \citep{CHSZ25}}\ \ Let $\bm X=(X_1, \ldots, X_n)$ be a vector of iid random variables with a common distribution function $F$. If $F\in\mathcal{H}$, then ${\rm ({\red SD})}$ holds, i.e.,
$$
   \sum_{i=1}^n\eta_i X_i  \le_{\rm st} \sum_{i=1}^n\theta_i X_i  \eqno {\rm (SD)}
$$
for $\bm \theta, \bm \eta\in \R_+^n$ such that $\bm\theta \preceq_{\rm m} \bm \eta$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.681811+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04e9a25fcce1a12ca3a7103e2b7408f84f34d1b3386623ee883ab7ff94fd2e89","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn d:sharp-dperc of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] For a graph $\Psi = {\Psi(\cdot)}$ defined on the input process \( \mathcal{P} \), the {\em critical probability $q_c$ for  percolation} is defined as  
\begin{equation}
q_c := \sup\Bigl\{q \in [0,1] :  \forall p \in \mathbb{N} , \, 
\text{the function $\tilde{\mathfrak{a}}_{p,q}(r)$ is fast decreasing as $r\to\infty$}\Bigr\},
\end{equation}
where for $r > 0$
\begin{equation} \tilde{\mathfrak{a}}_{p,q}(r):=\sup_{n \geq 1} \sup_{x_1,\ldots,x_p\in W_n}
\mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p,q}\bigl( x_1 \stackrel{(\mathcal{P}_n\cap B_{r}(x))_{*q}}{\longleftrightarrow} \mathcal{P}_n \cap B_r(x_1)^c \bigr), \,
\end{equation}
where the underlying graph is $\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n)$ and recall that
\(
\{x \stackrel{{{\cal X }'}_{*q}}{\longleftrightarrow} \mathcal{Y}\} = \{x \stackrel{\perc{\Psi({\cal X })}{{\cal X }'}{q}}{\longleftrightarrow} \mathcal{Y} \}
\)
means that there exists an open path in~\(\Psi({\cal X })\) from~\(x\in{\cal X }'\subset{\cal X }\) to
\(\mathcal{Y}\subset {\cal X }\)  through vertices in ${\cal X }'_{*q}$, the thinned subset of ${\cal X }'$ where vertices in~\({\cal X }'\) are
independently open/retained with probability~\(q\), while all other vertices in ${\cal X }$ are
always open, and where \(\mathbb{P}_{\bk x1p,q}\) incorporates this additional percolation randomness on top of Palm distribution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:17:33.988483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04ed228deb693f79798768102c7ba33be5cbdbcf7c5ce80c05b167a01543bd7c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2609.03386, 'Twisted Gradient Flow Approach to Vegetation-Rainfall-Bushfire Interactions Model'] For each pair $(\tilde{z},\tilde{g}) \in W^{1,2}(0,T;L^2(\Omega)) \times L^2(0,T;L^2(\Omega))$ we consider the following Cauchy problem 
$\text{(TGF)}{}_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}$:=\{(\ref{Equation-2-35})--(\ref{Equation-2-37})\} of a twisted gradient flow with a forcing term on the time-dependent 
twisted spaces $\{L^2(\delta,\bar{z}(t))\,;\,0 \le t \le T\}$:

\begin{gather}

v'(t)+v^*(t)=\tilde{g}(t) \quad \text{in} \quad L^2(\delta,\tilde{z}(t)),\quad \text{a.e.}~t \in (0,T),\\

v^*(t) \in \partial_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\tilde{z}(t)$}}} \Phi_\nu (v(t)),\quad \text{a.e.}~t \in (0,T),\\

v(0)=v_0 \quad \text{in} \quad L^2(\Omega),
\end{gather}

Then, the problem $\text{(TGF)}{}_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}$ admits a unique solution 

\begin{gather}

v_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}(v_0,\tilde{z},\tilde{g}) \in W^{1,2}(0,T\,;L^2(\Omega)) \cap L^\infty (0,T\,;H^1(\Omega))\\

\text{with} \quad v_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}^*(v_0,\tilde{z},\tilde{g}) \in L^2(0,T\,;L^2(\Omega)).
\end{gather}

\indent
Moreover, the unique solution $v_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}(v_0,\tilde{z},\tilde{g})$ is also the unique strong solution to 
$\text{(AP1)}{}_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}$ on $[0,T]$, and has the regularity 

\begin{equation*}
v_{\text{{\tiny $\delta,\!\nu$}}}(v_0,\tilde z,\tilde g) \in L^2(0,T\,;H^2(\Omega)).
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.286605+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_05002bb4e2e13e5ef5826c6f9e8b99f7c4eea3266a5d9038048fec2d55cb3c60","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:annealed cond of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Let $n\ge2$. Let $\bm{u}=(u_1,\ldots,u_{n})$,
		$\bm{q}=(q_1,\ldots,q_n)$ be strictly increasing
		in~$(0,1)$, and $\zeta\in\mathrm{Prefix}_{n+1}(\bm{u};\bm{q})$.
		Define the \emph{prefix free energy}
		\begin{equation}
			\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\;:=\;
			\inf_{\zeta'\,\in\,\mathrm{Prefix}_{n+1}(\bm{u};\bm{q})}
			\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta'),
		\end{equation}
		and the \emph{$n$-step complexity functional at level $f$}
		\begin{equation}
			\mathcal{C}^{(n)}_f(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\;:=\;
			u_n\,\bigl(\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})-f\bigr).
		\end{equation}

		We assume:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[\emph{(i)}]
			\emph{(Tail optimality)} The infimum $\mathcal{P}^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})$ is
			attained at $\zeta$.
			\item[\emph{(ii)}]
			\emph{(Stationarity)} $(\bm{u};\bm{q})$ is a critical point of
			$\mathcal{C}^{(n)}_f$.
		\end{enumerate}
		Let $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, let
		$(\mathbf{m}^{(1)},\ldots,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})\in\mathrm{SUSY}_{n-1}(\zeta,\varepsilon)\cap\mathrm{LawMatch}_{n-1}(\zeta,\varepsilon)$. Let
		$\mathcal{N}_\varepsilon(f,\zeta,q_n,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})$ be the number of critical points $\mathbf{m}$
		of $F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}$ with
		$(\mathbf{m}^{(1)},\ldots,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)},\mathbf{m})\in\mathrm{SUSY}_n(\zeta,\varepsilon)$ and
		$\tfrac{1}{N}F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m})\in(f-\varepsilon,f+\varepsilon)$. Set
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathrm{Skel}_{n-1}=\left(\nabla F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(1)}),F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(1)}),\ldots,\nabla F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)}), F_{\mathrm{TAP},\zeta}(\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})\right).
		\end{equation*} Then
		\begin{multline}
			\lim_{\varepsilon\to0}\lim_{N\to\infty}
			\frac{1}{N}\log\mathbb{E}\Bigl[
			\mathcal{N}_\varepsilon(f,\zeta,q_n,\mathbf{m}^{(n-1)})
			\;\Big|\;
			\mathrm{Skel}_{n-1}=(0,N\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta),\ldots,0,N\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta))
			\Bigr]
			=
			\mathcal{C}_f^{(n)}(\bm{u};\bm{q})
			\\=
			\zeta([0,q_n))\,(\mathcal{P}\mathrm{arisi}(\zeta)-f).
		\end{multline} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.448176+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_050763d87704ef01b00910151a25cdd19e6ae8298587845584414d57658077d5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conjecture 1 (Matrix Spencer) of arXiv:2606.16005, '\bfseries An Algebraic Matrix Spencer Theorem'] There exists a universal constant $C$, such that for any choice of $n$ symmetric matrices $(A_{i})_{i=1}^n \in \mathbb R^{n\times n}$ satisfying $\|A_i\|_{\op}\le 1$ there is a coloring $x\in\{\pm1\}^{n}$, such that
