{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.752198+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_057f1966d56e7dc0e0cd1ec01ec2a218fc66e5708b29cbbbcfd7e73b2ce43041","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 34 of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] Under the assumptions of Theorem \ref{distribution of outliers}, for each $1\le i\le r$ and $1\le \mu \le s$, we have 
\begin{align}
\wt\lambda_{\alpha(i)}-\theta_i^a&=-\frac{1}{\sigma_i^am'_{2c}(\theta_i^a)}\pa{\frac{d_i^a}{d_i^a+1}}^2\cdot\theta_i^a\vv_i^*\pa{G(\theta_i^a)-\Pi(\theta_i^a)}\vv_i+\OO_{\prec}(n^{-1}),\\
\wt\lambda_{\beta(\mu)}-\theta_\mu^b&=-\frac{1}{\sigma_\mu^bm'_{1c}(\theta_\mu^b)}\pbb{\frac{d_\mu^b}{d_\mu^b+1}}^2\cdot\theta_\mu^b \uu_\mu^*\pa{G(\theta_\mu^b)-\Pi(\theta_\mu^b)}\uu_\mu+\OO_{\prec}(n^{-1}).
\end{align}
where $\vv_i:=((\vv_i^a)^T, 0)^T\in\RR^{p+n}$ and $\uu_\mu:= (0^T, (\vv_\mu^b)^T)^T\in\RR^{p+n}$ are the canonical extensions of the population eigenvectors $\vv_i^a$ and $\vv_\mu^b$ to $\RR^{p+n}$, respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:25:12.186957+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0595dd57ed51139236d74c7617de8064c3d70306299331593796445f96b976aa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 20 of arXiv:2510.20492, 'Heterochromatic two-arm probabilities for metric graph Gaussian free fields'] For any $d\ge 3$, $v\in \widetilde{\mathbb{Z}}^d$ and $N\ge 1$, 
