{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.167758+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09bcb46d3dc5a82ab65609cc2fd4843dfc89e8386d6fe3a37441c667a355aec5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2604.17973, 'Schauder estimates and classical solutions of the Dirichlet problem for stochastic parabol'] Let $\alpha\in(0,1)$, let $h\in C^{1+\alpha/2}([0,T];L_\omega^\gamma)$ satisfy
\[
h(0)=0,
\qquad
h'(0)=0,
\]
and let $v$ be the solution of
\begin{equation}
\partial_t v(t,y)=\partial_y^2 v(t,y),
\qquad
v(0,y)=0,
\qquad
v(t,0)=h(t).
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
|\partial_t v|_{(\alpha,\alpha/2);[0,T]\times\R_+}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
+|\partial_y^2 v|_{(\alpha,\alpha/2);[0,T]\times\R_+}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
\le C\,[h']_{\alpha/2;[0,T]}^{L_\omega^\gamma},
\end{equation}
where $C=C(\alpha,\gamma,T)$. 

Moreover, if $v_1$ and $v_2$ correspond to boundary data $h_1$ and $h_2$, respectively, and if $h_1'(0)=h_2'(0)=0$, then
\begin{equation}
\sup_{(t,y)\in[0,T]\times\R_+}
\E\bigl|\partial_y^2 v_1(t,y)-\partial_y^2 v_2(t,y)\bigr|^\gamma
\le C\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\E\bigl|h_1'(t)-h_2'(t)\bigr|^\gamma.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.431878+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09c494e7ac9b0698c51a01626d97be4a52868cbc094c2906ef275c488eeb0e76","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 3. dfn: resistance metrics of arXiv:2412.08236, 'Aging and sub-aging for Bouchaud trap models on resistance metric spaces'] [{Resistance metric, [Kigami_01_Analysis]}]

  A metric $R$ on a non-empty set $F$ is called a \textit{resistance metric}
  if and only if,
  for any non-empty finite subset $V \subseteq F$,
  there exists an electrical network $G$ with the vertex set $V$ 
  such that the effective resistance on $G$ coincides with $R|_{V \times V}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.765649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09ca28f46011b7fa5352fd949dda8bccf7737b138b05df293f83f78fbf167e09","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:lcp-cluster-response of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] Let $x\in\Omega_{\rm str}^{\partial}$ and let $\Lambda>0$ have multiplicity
$r$ for $A_x$, with real $L^2(M_x^\partial)$-orthonormal eigenbasis
$(\phi_i)_{i=1}^r$.  Put
$\sigma_{ij}^x:=\Theta_{M_x^\partial}(\phi_i,\phi_j)$ as in
(equation eq:lcp-centered-cluster-products of the paper).  For $f\inH_{\mathrm{reg}}$, the first variation along
$M_{x,\tau}^{\partial,f}
:=e^{\kappa\tau f}M_x^\partial$ is
\begin{equation}
 \left(\inner{B_f^\Lambda(M_x^\partial)\phi_j}{\phi_i}
              _{L^2(M_x^\partial)}\right)_{i,j=1}^r
 =-\kappa\Lambda
 \left(\int_{\mathbb T}f\phi_i\phi_j\,dM_x^\partial\right)_{i,j=1}^r
 =-\kappa\Lambda
 \left(\inner{U\sigma_{ij}^x}{f}_{H}
 \right)_{i,j=1}^r.

\end{equation}
The last expression defines the unique continuous linear extension
$f\mapstoB_f^\Lambda(M_x^\partial)$ from
$H_{\mathrm{reg}}$ to $H$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.352435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09d2a4ae5516d4dea2355a0331f3a9d63efe357488a439bc10a5fd5f3b25cb6a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For each $\alpha>0$ and $d\in \mathbb{N}$, the \textbf{$d$-level Poisson-avoiding metric} with boundary parameter $\alpha$ is a random function
$$
H_d: \{(u, s; v, t) \in (\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 : s < t\} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}
$$
defined as follows. First, let $\Pi$ be a Poisson process on $\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Pi|_{(x, a) \times \mathbb{R}}$ has intensity $\alpha$ if $x = 0$, and intensity $1$ if $x > 0$. From $\Pi$, we construct a random weighted directed graph where:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item $(u, t)$ is connected to $(v, t)$ by an edge of weight $1$ if $(u, v)$ is a directed edge in $\Lambda_d$.
    \item $(u, s)$ is connected to $(u, t)$ by an edge of weight $0$ if $(u, r) \notin \Pi$ for all $r \in (s, t]$.
\end{itemize}
Let $H_d:(\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},$ be directed graph distance in this metric.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.856236+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09d93def40e91c40bcf7bed05d3ec389e09642a3217b95607812f589e413f92c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 of arXiv:2605.20115, 'Moment bounds on correctors for the degenerate random conductance model'] Suppose that $u:\Z^d\to \R$ and $f:\Z^d\to \R^d$ are related on $\Z^d$ via
\begin{equation}
-\nabla^*\cdot A\nabla u=\nabla^*\cdot f.
\end{equation}
There exists $\beta>1$ depending only on $\Lambda$ such that for any $p\in[1,\beta]$ and any ball $B$, we have almost surely
\begin{equation}
\fint_{x\in B}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u\Big)^{p}\,\lesssim \,\fint_{x\in 2B}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A^{-1} f\cdot f\Big)^{p}+\Big(\fint_{x\in 2B}\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u \Big)^{p}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, the following elliptic regularity estimates hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
(Global quenched Meyers' estimates) For $p\in[1,\beta]$,
\begin{equation}
\int_{x\in \ZZ^d}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A\nabla u\cdot\nabla u\Big)^{p}\,\lesssim  \,\int_{x\in \ZZ^d}\Big(\fint_{B_{\di}(x)}A^{-1} f\cdot f\Big)^{p}.
\end{equation}
\item
(Large-scale hole-filling estimates) For $\rd(x)\le r\le R$, if $f\equiv0$ in $B_R(x)$, then 
\begin{equation}
\int_{ B_r (x) }A\nabla u\cdot \nabla u\,\lesssim \, \Big(\frac rR\Big)^{\frac d{\beta'}}\int_{B_{R} (x) } A\nabla u\cdot \nabla u.
\end{equation}
Here we recall that $\beta':=\frac\beta{\beta-1}$ is the H\"older conjugate of $\beta$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.535200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09e81a85064621168e4bf555ae9395be30f07eb491f45d3a9db14a57a203bf76","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2603.28170, 'Stabilization time of finite configurations with a second class particle in discrete TASEP'] For $p\in(0,1)\setminus\{1/2\}$ fixed, we have that
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-\max(p,1-p)n}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow\mathcal N(0,p(1-p))
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$
where the limit is Gaussian with mean $0$ and variance $p(1-p)$.

For $p=1/2$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-n/2}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow\frac{\chi_3}2
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$ where $\chi_3$ is the chi distribution with parameter $3$.

For $p=1/2+\lambda/(2\sqrt n)$ with $\lambda\in\R$ fixed, we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-n/2}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow M_1^\lambda-\frac12B_1^\lambda
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$ where $B_t^\lambda$ is a Brownian motion with drift $\lambda$
and $M_t^\lambda=\max_{s\in[0,t]}B_s^\lambda$ is its running maximum process. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.082335+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09e91ac5409a178552b5e07b105d70705b73861aa4d806bb40db522b2a63a51c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition PropDiscreteModel of arXiv:2411.12522, 'Canonical insurance models: stochastic equations and comparison theorems'] The  two following conditions are equivalent:
\begin{itemize}
	\item[(a)]  $\Lambda$ has pure jump paths with jumps only at integer times{.}
	\item[(b)] $\mathbb{E}^i_s[N^{ij}(\mathrm{d} t)] = 0$ for $n \leq s \leq t < n+1$, $i,j \in \mathcal{Z}$, $i \neq j$,  $n\in\mathbb{N}_0$.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, for $\Omega_d := \big\{(t_k,z_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}_0} \in \Omega: t_k \in \mathbb{N}_0 \cup \{\infty\}, \, k \in \mathbb{N}_0\big\}$ the conditions imply that $ \mathbb{P}[\Omega_d]=1$ as well as
\begin{align*}
	\Delta \Lambda^{ij} (n+1)(\omega)=	\mathbb{P}_{n}^i[Z(n+1)=j](\omega), \quad  \omega \in \Omega_d,
\end{align*}
 for $ i,j \in \mathcal{Z}$, $i \neq j$, $n \in \mathbb{N}_0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.343846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09ea7c676615d5c55da3f9c09d4805a53c721d6478c7d6d88a9384d4ff471fdf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:disi of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume hypotheses and notation of Theorem~(thm:main of the paper).
For any $x\in H$ and $0<s\leq t$
it follows that
\begin{equation}
\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\mu_\ast)\right)^2\leq \int_{H}
\int_{H}\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_s\delta_z,P^*_s\delta_y)\right)^2 P^*_{t-s}\delta_x(\mathrm{d} z)\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} y).
\end{equation}
In particular, it follows that
\begin{equation}
\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,\mu_\ast)\right)^2=\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\mu_\ast)\right)^2
\leq \int_{H} \left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\delta_y)\right)^2\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} y).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.657111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09f07a11f59505bbb35532a29dad4053b69ec43826932165452b3f394b45f2c3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 19 (Occupation moment measures) of arXiv:2607.24561, 'Range convergence and sharp one-arm asymptotics\\for the critical percolation cluster in h'] For every $k\ge1$ and $\phi_1,\dots,\phi_k\in\Ccg$,
\begin{equation}
\bN\Bigl[\prod_{i=1}^k\mu(\phi_i)\Bigr]
=\gamma^{\,k-1}\sum_{T\in\T_k}\int_{(\R^d)^k}
I_T(z_1,\dots,z_k)\prod_{i=1}^k\phi_i(z_i)\,dz_i ,
\end{equation}
the integral converging absolutely. In particular the identity holds
for all $\phi_i\in\Ccg$, not only nonnegative ones.
Equivalently, as locally finite measures off the diagonals,
\[\bN\left[\mu(dz_1)\cdots\mu(dz_k)\right]
=\gamma^{k-1}\sum_{T\in\T_k}I_T(\vec z)\,d\vec z.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.225079+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09fcb3c84cc8144b9603ef2c2d00a8efa0ab5985a4ef3553b6b92985fac6b81c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition localstab of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $(M^n , [\hat{h}])$ be the conformal infinity of an asymptotically hyperbolic manifold $(X^{n+1},g^+ )$ such that $\Lambda_{\gamma} (M^n ,[\hat{h}]) < \Lambda_{\gamma} (\mathbb{S}^n ,[h_{0}])$. Then, there exist constants $C>0$, $\delta>0$ and $\theta \geq 0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        I_{\gamma} [u,\hat{h}]  - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \geq C d_{\delta}(u,\mathcal{M}_1)^{2+\theta} \, ,
    \end{align*}
    for all $u \in \mathcal{B}(u_*,\delta)$. If $u_*$ is non-degenerate or integrable, then we may take $\theta =0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.989951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09fcba722a4b43aa8dda6f2b4b04c6aa24b1ef84e53e5eca037d93ce2b2e15a2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2604.00882, 'On some extensions of generalized counting processes'] The probability generating function $
	{G}_\nu(u, t) = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} u^k {p}_k^\nu(t)$, $|u| \leq 1$, solves the following fractional differential equation
	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\partial^{2\nu} G_\nu(u, t)}{\partial t^{2\nu}} + 2\lambda \frac{\partial^\nu G_\nu(u, t)}{\partial t^\nu} = \Lambda(u - 1)G_\nu(u, t), 
	\end{equation}
	subject to the initial condition $G(u, 0) = 1$, and is given as
	\begin{equation}
		G_\nu(u, t) = \tilde \ell_\nu (\Lambda(u - 1), t), \,\,u < 1,
	\end{equation}
	where the expression for $\tilde \ell_\nu$ is given by \eqref{2.21}.
	In particular, for the case $\Lambda=\lambda^2$, \eqref{pgfN} becomes
	\begin{equation}

		{G}_\nu(u, t) = \frac{\sqrt{u} + 1}{2\sqrt{u}} E_{\nu, 1}(-\lambda(1 - \sqrt{u})t^\nu) + \frac{\sqrt{u} - 1}{2\sqrt{u}} E_{\nu, 1}(-\lambda(1 + \sqrt{u})t^\nu).
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.653171+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a0729758206e3a3427d85133f257513fcca7d44a7c4056d03426dfbfab052b5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 (Regulated-base product-form representation) of arXiv:2607.24533, 'Colored Markov-Modulated Brownian Motion for Preemptive Workload Stacks'] {Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{assump:intensity}, \ref{assump:stability}, and~\ref{assump:first-moment} hold for the regulated-base model. Then the model admits a stationary probability law.} On the face
$\Theta_{1,c_2,\dots,c_n}$, the stationary density row vector is
\begin{equation}

\vect{\pi}^{\mathrm R}(\bm{x})
=
\vect{\nu}^{\mathrm R}(x_1)
\prod_{r=1}^{n-1}
\Lop{c_r}{c_{r+1}}(x_{c_{r+1}}),
\qquad x_1>0,
\end{equation}
where $c_1=1$ and $\vect{\nu}^{\mathrm R}$ has the form
\eqref{eq:regulated-base-density}, with the direction of
$\vect g_{\mathrm{reg}}$ fixed by the reflecting boundary condition
\eqref{eq:regulated-base-boundary-g}. Because color $1$ is only one component
of the full stack, $\vect{\nu}^{\mathrm{R}}$ is not normalized to integrate
to $1$ on its own. Its scale is fixed jointly with the excursion mass by the
global normalization below. {Thus, $\vect\nu^{\mathrm R}$ is the density on the base face and an anchor for the full stack, not the marginal stationary density of $X_1$ after higher-color faces have been included.}

With
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak{M}^{\mathrm R}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\vect{0} & \vect{M}^{(2,3)} & \cdots & \vect{M}^{(2,C)}\\
\vect{0} & \vect{0} & \cdots & \vect{M}^{(3,C)}\\
\vdots & \ddots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\vect{0} & \cdots & \vect{0} & \vect{0}
\end{bmatrix},
\end{align*}
$\vect{m}^{\mathrm R}
=
[\vect{M}^{(1,2)},\dots,\vect{M}^{(1,C)}]$,
$\e^{\mathrm R}$ the column vector obtained by stacking $C-1$
copies of $\e$, and $\I^{\mathrm R}$ the identity matrix of the same dimension
as $\mathfrak M^{\mathrm R}$, the
normalization condition is
\begin{equation}

\int_0^\infty
\vect{\nu}^{\mathrm R}(x)\,\dd x
\left[
\e
+
\vect{m}^{\mathrm R}
(\I^{\mathrm R}-\mathfrak{M}^{\mathrm R})^{-1}
\e^{\mathrm R}
\right]
=
1.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.906489+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a10cb924d0528476ffc6fd1ac0da1cae15c439da37723889066f6388e19316d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 12 of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] If $h_k\in\mathscr D$ for all $k$, then, for $0\le m<r$,
