{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.752111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0bea5acc1415760d42b4797a2c82e30e770bba1c5fe27b504f4d06f18751e677","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:sol_lin_fp of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$ and $\nu\in \mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. A measurable flow ${\boldsymbol\mu}:[s,T)\to \mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ is a (weak) solution to FP$(b,\sigma;s,\nu)$ if
\begin{equation}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(y) {\boldsymbol\mu}_t (d y)    = \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \varphi(y) \nu (d y) + \int_{s}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}  \Big[\big\langle b(r , y) , \nabla \varphi(y)\big\rangle + \frac{1}{2} \text{tr}\big( \sigma\sigma^\top(r , y ) \nabla^2 \varphi(y) \big)   \Big]  {\boldsymbol\mu}_r (d y) d r, \qquad t\in [s,T),

\end{equation}

for any $\varphi\in C^{\infty}_0(\mathbb{R}^d)$. 

Furthermore, if ${\boldsymbol\mu}_t$ is absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure, namely
