{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:15.263978+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0eff0d9b2e6a272ec32360f5db14a86bf4c74073cbd956c5a425201aef9558b9","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[10 2 of arXiv:2607.12303, 'Marginal Stationary Distributions and Convergence Rates of Higher Order Markov Chains'] {\rm
An $(m-1)$the order Markov chain with transition tensor $\cal P$ is siad to be {\it ergodic},\footnote{We note that in the first order case, an ergodic chain is also called an irreducible chain and a regular chain is equivalent to an irreducible and aperiodic chain. However, for higher order chains, irreducibility and ergodicity differ (see, for example, \cite{HanWanXu22}).} if given any $i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_m \in S$, there exists $k \ge 1$, which may depend on $i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_m$, such that $p^{(k)}_{i_1i_2\ldots i_m}>0$.  

It is called  {\it regular} if there exists some fixed $h \ge 1$ such that $p^{(h)}_{i_1i_2\ldots i_m} > 0$ for every $i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_m \in S$.
 }","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.699874+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f01796dfcef30555ec635e334fa550e31eb1ed75506f47969f2f22125bf64f3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem incball.1 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] If $k$ is large enough (depending only on $\gamma$), then, given $n\geq 0$, $i \in \mathbb{N}_{\geq 2}$ and~$x \in \mathbb{X}_k$ such that $\overline{{\rm B}^-[x;i+1] }\subseteq {\rm D}_n$, there exists $y \in \mathbb{X}_{k+(i-2)k^\gamma}$ satisfying $\overline{{\rm B}(y;k+ik^\gamma -3{\rm e}^{-k^\gamma})} \subseteq {\rm D}_n$ such that ${\rm B}^-[x;\max\{i-2,1\}] \subseteq {\rm B}^-[y;i-1]$, and, for all $t > 0$,
\begin{equation}
\widehat{N}^{\rm{in}}_t[y;i] \geq \widehat{N}_{t}[x;i]	 \geq \widehat{N}^{\rm{out}}_t[y;i],
\end{equation}  
 where 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{N}^{\rm{in}}_t[y;i]:=	k^\gamma N_{t}(y; k+(i-1)k^\gamma +{\rm e}^{-k^\gamma},k+ik^\gamma -5{\rm e}^{-k^\gamma})
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{N}^{\rm{out}}_t[y;i]:= k^\gamma N_{t}(y; k+(i-1)k^\gamma -{\rm e}^{-k^\gamma},k+ik^\gamma -3{\rm e}^{-k^\gamma}).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

