{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.295555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0f39b8a3f216348a7ddb7b3a98159552c935a30e7bbba06704b333c61f84626a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defWellPoseHadmard of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Consider the equation

\begin{equation}
F(u) = v, \qquad u\in U,\; v \in V.
\end{equation}

The equation (equation defWellPoseHadmard-eq1 of the paper) is called well-posed in the sense of Hadamard if the following three conditions are satisfied:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
    \item For any $ v \in V $, there exists a solution $ u \in U$ such that $ F(u) = v $.
    \item This solution is unique.
    \item The solution $ v = F^{-1}(u) $ depends continuously on $ u $ in $U$.
\end{enumerate}
If at least one of these conditions is failed, the problem is called ill-posed.","status":"unverified"}

