{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.773766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_109d4e97a818393bec4933949f62e0d54bbe5cc29594ae8da3f442df8ed50d16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Newton-inequalities of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] Let $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$ be a non-negative and non-zero sequence with finite length $m\geq1$, and let $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ be the sequence of the elementary symmetric polynomials in the $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$, or equivalently let $e_k=e_k(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ for $0\leq k\leq m$.
	Then the sequence $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ satisfies the \emph{ultra-log-concavity} inequality, that is
	\begin{align}
	\frac{e_k^2}{\binom{m}{k}^2}\geq 
		\frac{e_{k-1}}{\binom{m}{k-1}}\cdot
		\frac{e_{k+1}}{\binom{m}{k+1}},
		\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,m-1\}.
	\end{align}
	In particular, ${\mathbf{e}}(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ is log-concave.
		\footnote{
		This is deduced from the inequality $\binom{m}{k}^2\geq\binom{m}{k-1}\binom{m}{k+1}$ for $0<k<m$, which holds by log-concavity of the binomial coefficients.
		Note that in this paper we require log-concave sequences to have no internal zeros, but we already justified that this is the case for sequences having the form ${\mathbf{e}}({\boldsymbol{\uptheta}})$ with ${\boldsymbol{\uptheta}}$ a summable weight sequence.
		} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.858011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a5d81861634706283d77e8de44525859b7891f4338d6100b8396a736cfc6ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem lem:gen_ibp of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] For all $l \in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $F \in C_b^1(L)$ the following two equations hold
    \begin{align}
        \langle \!\langle F(\beta), \bar{l}_a\delta_0(\beta_a)\rangle \!\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \Big\langle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\langle F(\beta), \int_{[0,1]} \bar{l}_x\delta_0(\beta_x) dx \Big\rangle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|.
    \end{align}
	Here, the LHSs are interpreted as discussed in Theorem (thm:ibpf of the paper). Moreover, for all elements $l\in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $G \in (C_b^1)_{H_0^1\cap H^2}$, the two equations
    \begin{align}
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho^a \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|
    \end{align}
    hold. Using these results, [RZZ12], Theorem 3.1(iii), directly implies that $\rho^a,\, \rho \in \mathrm{BV}(L, H_0^1)$, $V(\rho^a)=\var{\sigma^{(a)}}(L)$ and $V(\rho)=\| \nu \|(L)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.040120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a823ade8a28c59db2d133402cde3b91f98a0bb6a93b69242077133547a40d9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Assume that there exists a constant $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $\sigma(\beta) = 0$. 
