{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:44:27.980512+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12a2a74efdbfdd213bfbb7c11b75e2224d83705bb07d785ece64da238d1785e3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Generalized Laplace Method) of arXiv:2607.13273, 'Asymptotics of transition densities for L\'evy processes with stochastic resetting'] Let $-\infty<a<b<\infty$, and $f\in C^2((a,b))$ with unique global maximum at $x_0\in(a,b),$ $h(x)\in C((a,b))$ and $h(x)\geq 0$ for $x\in(a,b)$. If $e^{f(x)}h(x)$ is integrable on $(a,b)$, then
     \begin{equation*}
        \int_a^b h(x)e^{rf(x)} \dx \sim_r h(x_0)\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{r|f^{\prime\prime}(x_0)|}} e^{rf(x_0)}.
    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.974942+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12a2d6cd76d492973ad3f914a51f7617ac606c68cf9e5c4369a5c0a08efd1f25","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2606.11142, 'Strong invariance principles for diffusions, Markov chains and their perturbations.'] Let $P^m_{(x,y)}$ and $P^m_{\epsilon,(x,y)}$ denote the distributions of the vectors $V = (Z_{t_1}, \dots, Z_{t_m})$ and $V^\epsilon = (Z^\epsilon_{t_1}, \dots, Z^\epsilon_{t_m})$, respectively, where $Z_{t_k} = (X_{t_k}, Y_{t_k})$ and $Z^\epsilon_{t_k} = (X^\epsilon_{t_k}, Y^\epsilon_{t_k})$. These vectors consist of random variables sampled at the nodes of either the Euler scheme and its perturbed version, or a diffusion process and its perturbed counterpart, both starting from the initial point $z_0 = (x, y)$. Then, there exists a constant $C > 0$, such that:
\begin{equation*}
\|P^m_{(x,y)} - P^m_{\epsilon,(x,y)}\|_{TV} \le C m \bar{\Delta}_{\epsilon,\gamma,q},
\end{equation*}
where $z = (z_1, \dots, z_m)$, $z_k = (x_k, y_k) \in \mathbb{R}^{2d}$, and $dz = dz_1 \dots dz_m$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:53:10.210613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12a72ea68fdfd3a362b8d41e282f179340224bc9630986494af1fb1e843c8640","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm lasso of arXiv:2608.29018, 'Sharp Restricted Isometry Thresholds for\\ Global Minima of Rank-Restricted Matrix LASSO'] Fix integers $r_{\star}\ge1$ and $k\ge2$. Suppose
that $\operatorname{rank}(X_{\star})\ler_{\star}$ and that $\mathcal{A}\in\operatorname{RIP}(\delta,k)$. Define
the threshold
\begin{equation}
\delta_{\mathrm{sharp}}(t)\coloneqq\begin{cases}
\sqrt{\dfrac{t-1}{t}}, & t\ge\dfrac{4}{3},\\[2ex]
\dfrac{t}{4-t}, & 0<t<\dfrac{4}{3}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{(Sufficiency)} If $\delta<\delta_{\mathrm{sharp}}(k/r_{\star})$, then every global
minimizer $X_{\lambda,r}$ of ((eq:matrix-lasso of the paper)) with $\lambda>0$
satisfies 
\begin{equation}
r\ger_{\star},\;\;\lambda\gtrsim\normop{\mathcal{A}^{*}(\xi)}\implies\normF{X_{\lambda,r}-X_{\star}}\lesssim\sqrt{r_{\star}}\,\lambda,
\end{equation}
where the hidden constants depend only on $\delta$ and on the ratio
$k/r_{\star}$. 
\item \emph{(Necessity)} If $\delta\ge\delta_{\mathrm{sharp}}(k/r_{\star})+1/r_{\star}$, then there
exist $X_{\star}$ and $\mathcal{A}$ with $\operatorname{rank}(X_{\star})\ler_{\star}$ and $\mathcal{A}\in\operatorname{RIP}(\delta,k)$
such that, even in the noiseless case $\xi=0$, every global minimizer
$X_{\lambda,r}$ of ((eq:matrix-lasso of the paper)) satisfies 
\begin{equation}
r\gtrsimr_{\star},\;\;\lambda>0\implies\normF{X_{\lambda,r}-X_{\star}}\ge\normF{X_{\star}},
\end{equation}
where the hidden constant depends only on the ratio $k/r_{\star}$. 
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.328772+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12a738de2aeeaa4180d2bf5e63e23e8b28eb6e61f93395c00803e4c396ce36a4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] A subgroup $\mathbb K$ of a Carnot group is said to be \emph{homogeneous} if
