{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.415291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_150d82d6ed243bc399cff3478e3599d4978af90c956f048a99d3f4834357f844","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma closedness conditions of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] The following are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(i)] The curve $\gamma_{\lambda,C^2}\in W^{2,p}(\R/\Z;\R^3)$ is a closed non-planar $p$-elastica.
        \item[(ii)] The open curve $\tilde \gamma_{\lambda,C^2}\in W^{2,p}(0,1;\R^3)$ is a  non-planar $p$-elastica with $\tilde \gamma_{\lambda,C^2}(0)=\tilde \gamma_{\lambda,C^2}(1)$ and $\tilde \gamma_{\lambda,C^2}'(0)=\tilde \gamma_{\lambda,C^2}'(1)$.
        \item[(iii)] The parameter couple $(\lambda,C^2) \in S$ satisfies $\Delta z(\lambda,C^2)=0$ and $\Delta \theta(\lambda,C^2) \in 2\pi \Q$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.663398+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15210bac876f7f9a1e85a513538e8f65da61280920647d16c77502fa68b91021","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn def:Bregdiv of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] Let $F:U\to \mathbb{R}^d$ be a continuously differentiable,  strictly convex function defined on a  {\em convex} $U\subset \mathbb{R}^d$. The convex $U$ will be assumed {\em open} in all what follows, except specific mention  (see e.g. Proposition~(prop:existence of the paper)$(a)$). The $F$-Bregman divergence of $x\!\in U$ with respect to (w.r.t.) another point $\xi\!\in U$ is defined by 
\begin{equation}
 \forall\, x\!\in U,\quad \phi_{_F}(\xi,x)= F(\xi)-F(x) -\langle \nabla F(x) | \xi- x \rangle\ge 0.
\end{equation}
Moreover $\phi_{_F}(\xi,x)=0$  if and only if $\xi=x$ since $F$ is strictly convex.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.726986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_152555605961c0dc243699e44a38d4c51331b3ec559b3886e269be9b17f6dd6e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2605.17494, 'Generalized intersection exponents and local cut points for three-dimensional Brownian loo'] Let $W_1= A_{\frac{1}{20}} \cap \mathcal{A}(0,2)$ and $W_2= A_{\frac{1}{18}} \cap \mathcal{A}(-\frac{1}{10},\frac{21}{10})$ be two wedges with $W_1 \subset W_2$. There exists a universal constant $C$ such that for any $x_1 \in W_1 \cap S_0$, $x_2 \in W_1 \cap S_2$,
    \begin{equation}
    \Pb^{x_1}(B[0, \sigma_2] \in W_2 \mid B(\sigma_2)=x_2) \ge C,    
    \end{equation}
    where $\sigma_2$ is the last time $B$ visits $S_2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.723553+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15292362d172754e5f308612d99fbcf27e816be588f9ecc14a7b5fcbfcc8c5c6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 19 of arXiv:2609.01190, 'Rough differential equations on manifolds via natural bundles'] Let $m,n\in \Nsf$ and let $M$ be an $m$-dimensional manifold. A rough velocity field $f:M\to R_n^2M$ is called \emph{admissible} if, for every $p\in M$, there exists a chart $(U,x)$ around $p$ such that the coordinate representation $(f_\tau^i)$ of $f$ in the induced chart $(\overline{U},\overline{x})$ on $R_n^2M$ satisfies 
	\begin{equation}

		\left(
			\frac{\partial f^i_{\tb}}{\partial x^j}f^j_{\ta}
		\right)(p) 
		= f_{\tab}^i(p) + f_{\tc}^i(p)
	\end{equation}
for every $i\in \llbracket 1,m \rrbracket$ and every $\alpha,\beta\in \llbracket 1,n \rrbracket$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.246384+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_152c3c03730d6739944c571ac01f6eac89d94d5b382c9be1c85a5c0e46fcbad1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] The {\em Sobolev inequality} $S_\phi(n,r_1,r_2)$ holds in $B$, if for all $x\in B$, $r\in[r_1,r_2]$ {and $u\in\cC_{c}(B_x(r))$}, we have
\begin{equation*}
\frac{m(B_x(r))^{\frac{2}{n}}}{\phi r^2}
