{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.882469+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16a9f0554104c8ce03cc6ee4088ca38a6be9b8b2ea40a09ce626d7de73a6b79a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition1 3 of arXiv:2506.11799, 'Quenched limit for diffusive biased random walks in random environment'] We define iteratively the joint regeneration times $(\mu_{k}^{(1)}, \mu_{k}^{(2)})$ as $(\mu_{0}^{(1)}, \mu_{0}^{(2)}) = (0, 0)$ and
	\begin{equation*}
		(\mu_{k+1}^{(1)}, \mu_{k+2}^{(2)}) \coloneqq \inf_{n, m}\left\{(\tau_n^{(1)}, \tau_m^{(2)}) \colon \tau_n^{(1)} \ge \mu_{k}^{(1)}, \tau_m^{(2)} \ge \mu_{k}^{(2)}, X^{(1)}_{\tau_n} \cdot v^* = X^{(2)}_{\tau_m} \cdot v^* \right\}.
	\end{equation*}
    Furthermore, we let $\mathcal{L}_k = X^{(1)}_{\mu_{k}} \cdot v^* = X^{(2)}_{\mu_k} \cdot v^*$ denote the $k$-th joint regeneration level.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.375234+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16ac35e2bf0ef29ba3ec11b986ab47a01ec4e83704e868b0d45c66b7d57ef06a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:BregEsts of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] Assume:
	\begin{itemize}
	\item $\mathsf{acc}$ is continuously differentiable. What weakenings would work? Need its derivative to be integrable. 
	\item $\mathsf{acc}$ is absolutely continuous. NB this follows from cts diff, but perhaps not from the relevant weakenings. 
	\end{itemize}
	$\mathsf{acc}$ is strictly proper iff there is a strictly convex function $\varphi$ (and values $\mathsf{acc}_v(v)$) where:
	\begin{align}\mathsf{acc}_v(x)=\mathsf{acc}_v(v)-\left[\varphi(v)-\varphi(x)-(v-x)\varphi'(x)\right]
	\end{align}
	That is, the error-score is:
	\begin{align}\mathfrak{s}_v(x)=\varphi(v)-\varphi(x)-(v-x)\varphi'(x)
		\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.855622+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16acf1a4cc156d46c1ae9beb58e39c64f82dba7b7d433b520bf15cde3764b1b1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2608.23462, '2608.23462'] Assume that Assumption~\ref{assu:frozenLipschitz} is satisfied. Then for any fixed time horizon $T>0$ and $p \geq 2$, the process $(\hat Y^{x,y}_t)_{t \in [0,T]}$ is locally $L^p$-bounded and continuous in $(x,y) \in \R^{d_1+d_2}$ with respect to $L^p$-convergence. More precisely, for any bounded set $U \subseteq \R^{d_2}$ there exists a constant $C_{p,T,U}>0$ such that
    \begin{align*}
        \sup_{(x,y) \in \R^{d_1}\times U} \hat \E \Big[ \sup_{0 \leq t \leq T} |\hat Y^{x,y}_t|^p \Big] \leq C_{p,T,U},
    \end{align*}
    and for any $(x,y) \in \R^{d_1+d_2}$ we have
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{(x',y')\to (x,y)} \hat \E \Big[ \sup_{0 \leq t \leq T} |\hat Y^{x',y'}_t-\hat Y^{x,y}_t|^p \Big] =0.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:05:52.388586+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16be1537474cf860a11af32e630fb7bb8ca8acbc7beafe24a4d77a9eebf32fbb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:oscillation of arXiv:2504.11107, 'An Invariance Principle for some\\% Reaction-Diffusion Equations with a Multiplicative Ran'] For every $a>0$ and $k\in[2\,,\infty)$,
