{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.236608+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16ca83af6cd5e5263710e87d4127ac5b3085853dfe2f45fdd7ed008ac489a1ce","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop:uniform-T-W2 of arXiv:2607.00149, '\bf Uniform-in-time Propagation-of-Chaos for\\ Stein Variational Gradient Descent'] Let Assumption~(ass:Wass of the paper) hold. Recall the exponent $\mathfrak r=\mathfrak r(d,\nu)$ in (equation eq:rdef of the paper), and let
$
    \gamma_*:=\frac{\mathfrak r}{4} \wedge \beta_{\rm init},
$
where $\beta_{\rm init}$ is the logarithmic decay rate of the probability associated with the initialization in Assumption~(ass:Wass of the paper). Then there is a $N_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ and $C \in (0,\infty)$ such that,
for all $N\ge N_0$,
\begin{equation}
    \sup_{T>0}\,
    \mathbb P\left(
        W_2(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T)
        > (\log N)^{-\mathfrak r / 4}
    \right)
    \le C\,(\log N)^{-\gamma_*}.
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\[
W_2(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T)
=
O_{\mathbb P}\bigl((\log N)^{-\mathfrak r/2}\bigr)
\]
uniformly over deterministic averaging horizons \(T>0\), in the sense that
\begin{equation}
\lim_{M\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}
\sup_{T>0}
\mathbb P\left(
W_2(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T)>M(\log N)^{-\mathfrak r/2}
\right)
=0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.245869+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16d4b9b61d39d0d6e0de5ae8c46f62ac5b3a34697b73b13d2e6e8198ac90d19e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Hypercon of arXiv:2507.02518, '{\bf Exponential Ergodicity in Relative Entropy and $L^2$-Wasserstein Distance for non-equ'] Let $\bar{\mu}$ be the invariant probability measure of $(P_t)_{t\ge0}$, and
assume that $(P_t)_{t\ge0}$ is hyperbounded, i.e.,
$\|P_{t_0}\|_{p\rightarrow q }:= \|P_{t_0}\|_{L^p(\bar{\mu})\rightarrow L^q(\bar{\mu})}<\infty $ for some   $t_0>0 $ and  $1<p<q<\infty$.
Assume moreover that $L^2$-exponential ergodicity holds, i.e.
\begin{align*}\|P_t f-\bar{\mu}(f)\|_{L^2(\bar{\mu})}\leq c\text{\rm{e}}^{-\lambda t}\|f-\bar{\mu}(f)\|_{L^2(\bar{\mu})},\ \ t\geq 0
\end{align*}
for some constants $c,\lambda>0$.
Then hypercontractivity holds, i.e.
\begin{align*}
\|P_{\tilde{t}_0}\|_{p\rightarrow q }\leq 1
\end{align*}
for some constant $\tilde{t}_0>0$ depending only on $c,\lambda,q,p,t_0$ and $\|P_{t_0}\|_{p\rightarrow q }$.
Consequently, exponential ergodicity in relative entropy holds:
\begin{align*}
\bar{\mu}(P_t f\log P_tf)\leq \tilde{c}\text{\rm{e}}^{-\tilde{\lambda} t}\bar{\mu}(f\log f),\ \ \bar{\mu}(f)=1, f>0, t\geq 0
\end{align*}
for some constants $\tilde{c}, \tilde{\lambda}>0$ depending only on $\tilde{t_0},q,p$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.091440+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_16d9fb760a4dae0de89c4e86d4cba5b4ed2b1bb18dcb64f4a947a956d1ba3ec7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem equa:convL of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] For every $y \in {E}$ with $y \neq x$, under $\mathbb{P}_{0,y}$  and $\mathbb{N}_{y}$,  a.e.  for every $M \geq 0$ we have 
