{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.181193+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_175f9ef9c5cd80297be98bbf4502dfe697cc8e35ddff7b8c05235bf566ebe550","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 2 of arXiv:2608.22636, 'Q-Learning with Stable Infinite-Dimensional \\Linear Function Approximation'] Under Assumptions~\ref{assump:mdp} and \ref{assump:stable_kernels}, the operator
$\cG=\mrm{K}\mathcal T\Phi$ maps $\Theta$ into itself and is a $\gamma$-contraction on $\Theta$.  Hence $\cG$
has a unique fixed point $\theta^*\in\Theta$.  Moreover, $\theta^*$ is $\rho$-Lipschitz and
\begin{equation}
        [\theta^*]_{\rho}
        \le
        \frac{2L_{\kappa,\rho}}{c_\wedge(1-\gamma)}.

\end{equation}
With $q^*:=\Phi\theta^*$, we have
\begin{equation}
        \theta^*=\mrm{K}\mathcal T\Phi\theta^* \quad\text{and}\quad 
        q^*=\Phi\mrm{K}\mathcal T q^* .

\end{equation}
Moreover, if $Q^*\in C(\mbb Z)$ satisfies $Q^*=\mathcal TQ^*$, then
\begin{equation}
        \norm{Q^*-q^*}
        \le
        \frac{\norm{Q^*-\Phi\mrm{K}Q^*}}{1-\gamma}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.785478+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1770f476d3bc45999476e536ef87459e18a45293a6df87031605bfaa9feb1e99","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem torus-bottleneck of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] For every $d\ge 2$ and $\beta>\beta_c(d)$, there exists $C(d)$ and $c(\beta,d)$ (going to inifinity as $\beta\uparrow \infty$) such that 
