{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.428348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e0e773f325477e435ca0b8b03a025dd476cbd70b7ef47688737e2c26b2e9fdb","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma number of solutions of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] The function $V:[0,\infty) \to \R$ given as
  \begin{equation*}
    V(x) := \frac{(p-1)^2}{p^2}  x^{\frac{2p} {p-1}} - 2\lambda \frac{p-1}{p^2}x^{\frac{p}{p-1}} + C^2 x^{-2},
  \end{equation*}
  with $\lambda\in \R$ and $C^2> 0$ is strictly unimodal, i.e.\ strictly decreasing until it reaches its minimum and then strictly increasing. 
  In particular, $g(x) = E-V(x)$ has zero, one or two real positive solutions for $E<\min V$, $E=\min V$ and $E>\min V$ respectively and is strictly increasing until it reaches the maximum and then strictly decreasing.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.316272+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e10aeb8d4b3b13c7db5e66a372538607ab0cb8e4e2f9ff8408da869f18f6d7c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2604.09248, '\bfseries Limit laws for longest edges in empty region graphs'] For any of the empty region graphs from Examples \ref{ex:Gabriel}--\ref{ex:ConvBody}, we have $\xi_t \overset{d}{\longrightarrow} \zeta$ as $t \to \infty$, where $\zeta$ is a Poisson process with intensity measure $\vol \otimes \mu \otimes\nu$. More precisely, for any set $W \in \mathcal{B}(\RR^d)$ with $\vol(W)<\infty$ and any $b \in \RR$, it holds that
    \[
    \mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{KR}} (\xi_t|_{W \times [b, \infty)\times\LL^d}, \zeta|_{W \times [b, \infty)\times\LL^d}) \le \begin{cases}
        c\log^{-(d-2)}(t), & d \ge 3,\\
        c\log^{-1/2}(t), & d=2,
    \end{cases}
    \]
for any $t \ge 2$. Here, $c\in(0,\infty)$ is a constant which only depends on $d$, $\vol(W)$, $b$, and the parameters of the particular example. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.503010+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e1192d65f3aa368240e320ad68dbed5d6a7092c3bfa7cfe14c6d957f75d202b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 28 of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] Fix~$R\geq 1$ and a configuration~$\omega$. We say that there exists a \emph{double four-petal flower domain} between~$\Lambda_{R}$ and~$\Lambda_{2R}$ if
\begin{itemize}
    \item~$\Fout$, the outer flower domain on~$\Lambda_{2R}$ is well-separated and has exactly four petals $\Pout_1,\dots,\Pout_4$;
    \item~$\Fin$, the inner flower domain on~$\Lambda_R$ is well-separated and has exactly four petals~$\Pin_1,\dots,\Pin_4$;
    \item~$\Pin_1$ is connected to~$\Pout_1$ and~$\Pin_3$ to~$\Pout_3$ in~$\omega\cap \Fin^\comple\cap\Fout^\comple$;
    \item~$\Pin_2$ is connected to~$\Pout_2$ and~$\Pin_4$ to~$\Pout_4$ in~$\omega^*\cap \Fin^\comple\cap\Fout^\comple$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.406936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e16bb4f1c48a185f4756410058f937d4192a9b7b707cfcc223bf3d49823336b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (\cite{aggarwal2020limit}, Theorem 1.1) of arXiv:2604.06532, 'Random permutations from $q$-Demazure products'] Fix $\varepsilon>0$ and let $G(u,v)$ be the entropy solution to the conservation law
  \begin{equation}

    \frac{\partial}{\partial v} G(u,v) + \frac{\partial}{\partial u} \left( \varphi(G(u,v))\right) = 0,
  \end{equation}
  on the cylinder $\mathbb{T} \times [0, \lambda]$, with initial data given by $G(u,0) = \Psi(u)$ and the flux $\varphi(\cdot)$ defined as in \cref{eq:flux_def}.
  For each $M \geq 1$, let $\nu^{(M)} = (\nu_v (u))$ be the particle configuration associated to the sampled stochastic six-vertex configuration on the cylinder $\mathfrak{C}_{M,L}$ described above.
  Then,
  \begin{equation}
    \lim_{M \to \infty} \mathbb{P} \left[ \max_{\substack{0 \leq U_1 \leq U_2 < M \\ 0 \leq V_1 \leq V_2 \leq L}} \left|\frac{1}{M^2}\sum_{j=V_1}^{V_2} \sum_{i=U_1}^{U_2} \nu_{j} (i) - \int_{V_1/M}^{V_2/M}\int_{U_1/M}^{U_2/M} G(u,v) \,du \,dv \right| > \varepsilon \right] = 0.
  \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:29:14.926974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e21f73f72c5ce236f0afdd38243a0a593ec11229a1a5a15d29101c73cc0a868","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm.main_basic of arXiv:2606.13442, 'Scaling limit of additive functionals for reversible non-gradient exclusion process: criti'] Under Hypothesis~(hyp of the paper) and under the stationary measure $\mathbb{P}_{\rho}$, for every centered local function $f$, the additive functional $(\Gamma^N_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ converges weakly in $C(\mathbb{R}_+, \mathbb{R})$ to a limit $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$, which is characterized  as follows:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item $d = 1$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a $\frac{3}{4}$-fractional Brownian motion with covariance matrix
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] = \frac{2\chi(\rho)}{3\sqrt{\pi \mathbf{D} (\rho)}}\overline{f}'(\rho)^2 \left(t^{\frac{3}{2}} + s^{\frac{3}{2}} - \vert t - s\vert^{\frac{3}{2}} \right).
			\end{align}
			\item $d = 2$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a Brownian motion with covariance
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] = \frac{\chi(\rho)\overline{f}'(\rho)^2}{2\pi\sqrt{\det[\mathbf{D}(\rho)]}} (t \wedge s).
			\end{align}
			\item $d \geqslant 3$, $(\Gamma^\infty_t(f))_{t \geqslant 0}$ is a Brownian motion with covariance
			\begin{align}
				\mathbb{C}\mathrm{ov}[\Gamma^\infty_t(f), \Gamma^\infty_s(f)] =  2 \left\Vert{f}\right\Vert^2_{-1} (t \wedge s).
			\end{align}
		\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.143007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e264387e59d6d7f796b4d40fd6621e92ff863a7db6d514ac53cdb6086a33939","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 (\cite{QuanTrig}) of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] Fix $W_1,W_2, W_3 \ge \gamma^2/2$, and let $\beta_i= \gamma+\frac{2-W_i}{\gamma}$ if $W_i\neq \frac{\gamma^2}{2}$ and $\beta_i=Q^-$ if $W_i=\frac{\gamma^2}{2}$.
    Consider a distribution $\phi$ sampled from 
    \begin{equation*}
        \prod_{\beta_i\neq Q^-} (Q-\beta_i)^{-1} \LF_{\bH}^{(\beta_1,0), (\beta_2, 1),(\beta_3, \infty)}.
    \end{equation*}
    The infinite measure $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is defined to be the law of $(\bH, \phi, 0, 1, \infty)/\mathord\sim_\gamma$. A sample from $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is called a quantum triangle with weights $W_1$, $W_2$ and $W_3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.681729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e2fd37c7970323de6ec85ea023f0b24324b8ff820494d1f0884d5829334a014","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition c:cltspindisag of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let \(\mathcal{P}\) be a simple, stationary point process on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with summable exponential mixing correlations as in Definition (def.A2 of the paper), and assume that the (reduced) Palm intensity function of the ground process \(\mathcal{P}\) is bounded at all orders, as specified in (equation bdedreduced of the paper). Let \(\Psi = \Psi(\cdot)\) be a stabilizing interaction graph on finite windows with respect to \(\mathcal{P}\) as in Definition~(d:stabilizing-graph of the paper), with finite averaged connective constant \(\bar\Delta<\infty\)  defined in Definition~(d:connective-constant-pp of the paper). If, for some $\epsilon>0$,  all sets \( B \in\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0) \)
defined in~(equation e.particular-sets of the paper), and almost every realization \(\mathcal{P} \), the maximum influence \( q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \) as in (equation defq of the paper) of the Gibbs specification~(equation eqn:gibbs of the paper) on $\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B)$ with finite spin space $\mathbb{K}$ satisfies the inequality 
\begin{equation} 
q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \le (\epsilon+\bar{\Delta})^{-1}
\end{equation}
 then the spin configuration \( \{V_x(\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n))\}_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n} \) satisfies the CLT as stated in Theorem~(spinmain of the paper), provided the variance condition there is also met. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.713277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e36b81b3ec8482f0199de8c5051f6eb3358545cff3d3bdeb69d68f9ddddf1ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition proposition_rho_has_weak_deriv of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] All limit points $\mathbb{Q}$ of the sequence $(\mathbb{Q}_{\nu_N})_{N \geq 1}$ are such that, $\mathbb{Q}$-a.s. there exists  {a} $L^2([0,T],H^1[0,1])$ function, called the weak derivative of $\rho$ and that we denote by $\nabla \rho_s(u)$, such that,  {for every $G$ smooth function, it holds that
\begin{align*}
    \int_0^T \left[\int_0^1 \rho_s(x) \nabla G_s(x) dx - F^+(\rho_t(1)) G(1) - F^-(\rho_t(0)) G(0) \right] ds  = - \int_0^T \int_0^1 \nabla \rho_s(x) G_s(x) dx ds
\end{align*}} and
