{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.645014+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20891f045230f454f76050bd1ecc1de722c672d09421c60a9d928266f0eeddf4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm cont-general of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $D\subset\mathbb C$ be a bounded, simply connected domain. Let $\phi$ be a Gaussian free field with zero boundary conditions in $D$. Then, for any $\alpha\in(0,1)$ there exists a collection $(\mu_k)_{k=1}^\infty$ of measures supported on $(C_k)_{k=1}^\infty$ such that 
            \begin{align*}
            :\! \sin(\alpha \phi ) \!: \  = \lim_{N\to \infty}\ \sum_{k=1}^{N} \xi_{k} \mu_{k},
            \end{align*}
    where $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^\infty$ are i.i.d. symmetric signs. 
    The sum converges almost surely in the Sobolev space $H^s(\mathbb C)$ for $s<-1$, under \emph{any ordering} that is independent of the collection of signs. Moreover, properties (i)-(iv) of Theorem (thm:cont-XOR of the paper) hold, along with
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]\setcounter{enumi}{4}
        \item The signs $(\xi_k)_{k=1}^\infty$, the measures $(\mu_k)_{k=1}^\infty$ and the excursions $(C_k)_{k=1}^\infty$ are measurable with respect to $\phi$.
    \end{enumerate}
    The same statement holds for\footnote{Under the same caveat as before.} the field $:\! \cos(\alpha\phi+u) \!: $ for any $u\in (-\pi/2,\pi/2)$ by adding a symmetry-breaking (positive) measure $\mu_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:57.090439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20904f38f357324ed777bbfc65421fa39886e635f44ce3c598d9e2b9a7c80f16","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defSRRWG of arXiv:2604.07227, 'Transition probabilities of step-reinforced random walks'] Let $(\xi_n)_{n\geq 2}$ be i.i.d. Bernoulli random variables with success parameter $\alpha\in [0,1]$, and let $(u_n)_{n\geq 2}$ be independent random variables where each $u_n$ is uniformly distributed on $\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}$. Let $(T_n)_{n\geq 2}$ be a sequence of measurable transformations on $G$ such that $(\xi_k)_{k\geq n}$ and $(u_{k})_{k\geq n}$ are independent of $T_n$ for each $n$. Define a walk $(S_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and its the step sequence $(X_n)_{n\geq 1}$ recursively as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
  \item Set $S_0:=e_G$ (the group identity), sample $X_1\sim \mu$, and set $S_1:=X_1$;
 \item For $n> 1$, given $X_1,X_2,\dots,X_{n-1}$: 
  \begin{itemize}
    \item If $\xi_{n}=1$, set $X_{n}:=T_n(X_{u_{n}})$;
    \item If $\xi_{n}=0$, sample $X_{n}$ independently from $\mu$.
  \end{itemize}
 Update $S_{n}:=S_{n-1}\cdot X_{n}$.
\end{enumerate}
The process $S=(S_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ is called a generalized step-reinforced random walk (SRRW) on $G$ starting from $e_G$ with reinforcement parameter $\alpha$ and step distribution $\mu$ and transformations $(T_n)_{n\geq 2}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.856027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_209081664f9dc2b6fff9f96ab735f5726461587d269c44a71e571575e735c5f3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2605.20115, 'Moment bounds on correctors for the degenerate random conductance model'] Let  $a$ satisfy \eqref{eq.mombd-a} for some $\gamma\ge d+1$.
There exist constants $C_0, C_1, C_2 \ge 1$, depending on dimension and on the effective elliptic ratio $\Lambda\coloneqq\expec{a^{d+1}}^{\frac1{ d+1 }}\expec{a^{-(d+1)}}^{\frac1{ d+1 }}\ge 1$ only, such that if  $\gamma \ge C_2$, then
\begin{equation*}
\sup_{R\ge 1} \expecb{\Big|R^{\frac d2} \fint_{B_R}\nabla (\phi,\sigma) \Big|^{\frac\gamma {C_1}}}^{\frac {C_1}{\gamma}}\lesssim_{\Lambda} (\gamma\Gamma)^{C_0},
\end{equation*}
where $\Gamma$ is defined in \eqref{eq.mombd-a}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.193238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2092d4a9b1f12a9e1075f0ebee74363735ed0b2de2261f99281aea094cd6b09e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Limit of the Markov chain: Max-Height case.}

Let us suppose that $\mathcal{L}$ puts non zero mass on $S$, the maximum of its support. Then, almost surely, $(H_n, t_n)$ goes to $(S, 0)$ in finite time:
\[\p\left(\exists n \in N : (H_n, t_n) = (S, 0)\right) = 1.\]
Moreover, we have the following behaviour for $(X_n)$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Under scheme $\tau_1$, it is constant starting from the hitting time of $\{S\}\times \R_+$ (which, from the above, is almost surely finite).
    \item Under scheme $\tau_2$, it almost surely goes to $+\infty$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.343285+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_20a243d63cbfb8d87f987dcdd15de90eb1526af46320bf7b17f0df6ba363742e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:u-bound of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume that Hypotheses~(hyp:hemicont of the paper),~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:coerc-g of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity-g of the paper), and (hyp:growth of the paper) hold true. For any $T>0$ and any $x\in H$, we have that
