If $\beta\ge0$, then we may take $\eta=1/(4\sqrt{k-1})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.559601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_22fc09a31e3769e2e4de44ec2f2e6b256c63b291f2092f5ea10a32251f3971a5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:discrete-kac-counterexample of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] Fix $\lambda>0$ and $\theta\in(0,1/2)$. Then the functions
    \begin{align}
        G_1(z)\coloneqq \frac12+\frac{1}{2\bigl(1+\theta\lambda(1-z)\bigr)},
        \qquad
        G_2(z)\coloneqq \frac{2+\theta\lambda(1-z)}
        {2+2\lambda(1-z)+\theta\lambda^2(1-z)^2}.
