\end{equation*}
Consequently, $\mathcal{U}(t)\subset(0,1)$ and $\mathcal{U}(t)$ is an interval for every $t\ge0$, where $\mathcal{U}(t):=\{x:(t,x)\in\mathcal{U}\}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.684740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23c168892e7410770f5dff4d8c5648188311660ffcae568dd68ea8c87f8a8614","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 (Norm independence of infinite radius) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] The condition $R(l)=\infty$ is independent of the norm on the finite-dimensional space $V$ and of the choice of any crossnorm family $(\alpha_n)$ satisfying
