{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.058023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292e54c9303b902c08bc52f3caea826350da6b114557f36144c2df681072345c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:brownian-fixed-gaussian-block of arXiv:2608.16119, 'Fully nonlinear parabolic equations under a fixed reference diffusion: weighted \texorpdfs'] For the fixed horizon $T$ and parameters $n\geq2$ and $\alpha\geq0$, set
$
 \mathcal H_{n,\alpha}
 :=L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;\mathcal C_n)
$
and define the operator initially on $C_c^\infty((0,T);\mathcal C_n)$ by
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}\widehat F
 :=t^{-1}D_\xi^2\operatorname{Sol}_n\widehat F.

\end{equation}
Then $\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}$ extends uniquely to a bounded operator from
$\mathcal H_{n,\alpha}$ into
$L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;L^2(\gamma_d;\mathsf{S}^d))$.
Under the Brownian pullback, this is the $n$th chaos block of the physical
source-to-Hessian map.  In logarithmic time it is represented by the causal
Volterra operator
\begin{equation*}
 (\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}\widehat F)(\ell)
 =\sqrt{n(n-1)}\int_0^\ell
   e^{-(n-1+\alpha)(\ell-\ell_0)/2}
   \widehat F(\ell_0)\,\mathrm d\ell_0.
\end{equation*}
Its exact operator norm is
\begin{equation}
 \norm{\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}}
 =\norm{\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}}_{2\to2}
 =\frac{2\sqrt{n(n-1)}}{n-1+\alpha}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.058602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293503513e06c6cd7352ccc3e1de7c5e71a11db3695037dd157ac1345e7678e9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 44 (Balanced reduction) of arXiv:2606.16636, 'Free energy of non-convex multi-species spin glasses with centered Ising spins'] Assume that $\xi$ is balanced with respect to $\lambda$ in the sense of Definition~\ref{d.balanced_comparison_structure}. Let $f$ be the limit free energy, or equivalently the Hopf formula in Theorem~\ref{t.main}. Then, for every $t\geq0$ and every $q\in\mcl Q_2^\sS$,
\begin{equation}

    u(t,q^\lambda)\leq f(t,q)\leq \sum_{s\in\sS}\lambda_s u(t,q_s).
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}

    f(t,0)=u(t,0),\qquad t\geq0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.198358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2936a77d9698a119db2a94e28afbe5705299c13ed91b3677261d0eeaeb445556","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:growth-gadget of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] The following holds uniformly over all possible $Q$ as above. Let $\mathfrak{L}_n$ be the (unique) $(H+1-n)$ level line induced by $\xi$. Then, with $\pi^\xi_Q$-probability $1 - e^{-c\log L}$, $\mathfrak{L}_n$ lies below the point $X= (0, Y + \sigma(\log L)^a + \log L)$, where
		\begin{align}
			&Y = -\frac{N_n^{1/3}(\log L)^{2a}}{8(\tau_\beta(\theta) + \tau_\beta''(\theta))\cos(\theta)^3}\,,\\
			&\sigma^2 = \frac{N_n^{2/3}(\log L)^a}{4(\tau_\beta(\theta) + \tau_\beta''(\theta))\cos(\theta)^3}\,.\nonumber
		\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.500749+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293b9e1e3e816099c71995e10c5e29e4e52a884c95ac0afdf3b4b18aeb2bf5e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:gamma of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Let $X:\mathbb{Z}^2_\ge \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\cup \{\infty\}$ be a field of independent random variables distributed as follows.
Take real parameters $\alpha$ and $\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}$.

For any $i\in \mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,i)\sim \operatorname{Gamma}^{-1}(\alpha+\gamma_i)$ if $\alpha+\gamma_i>0$, and $X(i,i)=\infty$ otherwise.

For any $i > j \in \mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,j)\sim \operatorname{Gamma}^{-1}(\gamma_i+\gamma_j)$ if $\gamma_i+\gamma_j>0$, and $X(i,j)=\infty$ otherwise. 
We extend the weights $X$ to all of $\mathbb{Z}^2$ by the symmetry condition $X(i,j)=X(j,i)$ for all $i,j$.

For any $u,v\in \mathbb{Z}^2$ with $u \le v$, define the \emph{partition function}
\[
Z_X(u;v)=\sum_{\pi}\prod_{(i,j)\in \pi}X(i,j),
\]
where the sum is over all up-right paths $\pi$ from $u$ to $v$, subject to the following condition:
\begin{equation}

\text{for any $(i,i)\in\pi$, necessarily $(i-1,i)\notin\pi$.}
\end{equation}
In words, paths may cross the diagonal at most once, moving from below the diagonal to above it.","status":"unverified"}

