{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.556878+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e907f2597724d802b1d06a47f9bc078740410e1f2421fd76308ad189fdf3792","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2608.27156, 'Traveling Waves in Equity Markets with\\Rank-Based Entry and Exit'] In the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:wave}, let $\alpha$ be the tail exponent \eqref{eq:alpha-main} of Corollary~\ref{cor:tail}, and let $0 \leq p < \alpha$. Then
\begin{equation}
    G^p \;=\; c \;+\; (1 - p)\, G^p_* \;+\; \Big\langle \frac{\tilde f}{g},\, \bar\Pi^p \Big\rangle \;-\; \big\langle \lambda_d,\, \bar\Pi^p \big\rangle,
    \qquad
    G^p_* \;:=\; \tfrac{1}{2}\, \langle \sigma^2, \bar\Pi^p \rangle,
\end{equation}
where $G^p_*$ is the portfolio's excess growth rate. With $\tilde f = 0$ this is the reduction formula of \cite[Proposition 5.5]{jourdain_capital_2015}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:23.331530+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e997b91b87cff19fd4742d59091c5a014c19a546157b85d67ff8638b2bb2134","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2506.22040, '\vspace{-3em} Khinchin inequalities for uniforms on spheres with a deficit'] Let $d \geq 2$ be a fixed dimension, let $\xi_1, \xi_2, \dots$ be independent random vectors uniform on the unit Euclidean sphere $S^{d-1}$ in $\R^d$. Let $p \geq 2$. For every $n \geq 2$, every real scalars $a_1, \dots, a_n$ with $\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^2 = 1$, we have
\begin{equation}
\E\left|\sum_{j=1}^n a_j\xi_j\right|^p \leq \E\left|\sum_{j=1}^n \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\xi_j\right|^p - \tilde{c}_{p,d}\sum_{j=1}^n \left(\frac{1}{n}-a_j^2\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where
\[ 
\tilde{c}_{p,d} = \frac{p(p-2)}{4d}\begin{cases}
\frac{(p+d-2)(p+d-4)}{d(d+2)}, & 2 \leq p \leq 4, \\ 0.385, & p > 4. \end{cases}
 \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:33:12.354327+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ea789fa526cdd820da160d3d0f7a1720d43b9be8643a6d870873bca05870ea0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 212 (budget constraint) of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] Fix parameters $\alpha,L,\iota,\epsilon$. Let $M,N\to\infty$ with $M/N\to\alpha$. Let $\omega$ denote a random variable. Let $\bG,\bXi$ be $M\times N$ matrices with i.i.d.\ standard gaussian entries, with rows $\bg^1,\ldots,\bg^M$ and $\bmeta^1,\ldots,\bmeta^M$ respectively,  such that $\bXi$ is independent of $(\bG,\omega)$. Let $\bar{\bx}\equiv \bar{\bx}(\bG)$ and $\hat{\by} \equiv \hat{\by}(\bG,\bXi)$ be $L$-Lipschitz, $\R^N$-valued functions such that 
	\begin{align*}
	&\E(\|\bar{\bx}\|^2)=\E(\|\hat{\by}\|^2)=N,\\
	&\E(\hat{\by}\,|\,\bG)=0 \textup{ almost surely.}
	\end{align*}
Suppose for some $r,s \ge 1$ we have a partition, which is permitted to depend on $(\bG,\omega)$ but not on $\bXi$:
	 \begin{align*}
    [M] = \bigsqcup_{j=1}^r B_j \textup{ with } 
    \frac{|B_j|}{M}
    = \lambda_j(\bG,\omega)\,,\\
    [N] = \bigsqcup_{j=1}^s 
    K_j \textup{ with } 
    \frac{|K_j|}{N}
    = \mu_j(\bG,\omega)\,,
    \end{align*}
such that $\min_j\lambda_j\ge\iota$ and $\min_j\mu_j\ge\iota$ hold $(\bG,\omega)$-almost surely. Then define the random variables
     \begin{align}

    \bbb_j \equiv \bbb(B_j)
        &\equiv 
        \frac{1}{|B_j|}
        \sum_{a\in B_j}
        \frac{(\bmeta^a,\by)}{N^{1/2}}
        \,,\\

    \vvv_j \equiv \vvv(B_j)
        &\equiv 
        \frac{1}{|B_j|}
        \sum_{a\in B_j}
        \frac{(\bar{\bg}^a,\by)^2}{N}
        \,,\\

    \uuu_j \equiv \uuu(B_j)
        &\equiv 
        \frac{1}{|B_j|}
        \sum_{a\in B_j}
        \frac{(\bmeta^a,\bar{\bx})
        (\bar{\bg}^a,\by)}{N}
        \,,\\

    \www_j
    \equiv \www(K_j) 
    &\equiv \frac{1}{|K_j|}
        \sum_{i\in K_j}
        (\by_i)^2\,.\end{align}
Recall the definition of the function $\Cost\equiv\Cost_0$ from \eqref{e:cost}. Then, for all sufficiently large $N$, we have
    \[
    \sum_{j=1}^r
    \frac{|B_j|}{M}
    \Cost(\bbb_j,\vvv_j,\uuu_j)
    \le
    \frac{1}{\alpha}
    \bigg( \sum_{j=1}^s
        \frac{|K_j|}{N}
        (\www_j)^{1/2}\bigg)^2
     +  \epsilon\,,
    \]
with probability at least $1-e^{-cN}$, where $c$ is a positive constant depending on the parameters $\alpha,L,\iota,\epsilon$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.578892+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ea824f011e9790956dbccde493f305ac4736c4270989fe2fac7f083bc73a597","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $k\in \Nbb$, $f\in \Sob^k(\Rbb^n,\gamma_n)$, $\kappa\in \Nbb^n$ with $|\kappa|=k$, and $t\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \Dop^\kappa \Pop_t f = \upe^{-t|\kappa|} \Pop_t \Dop^\kappa f,
\end{equation}
and consequently, for all $k\in \Nbb$,
\begin{equation}
    \left|\grad^k \Pop_t f\right| \leq \upe^{-kt}\Pop_t \left|\grad^k f\right|.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.528563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eb502e08b3e7262d2a46c4df3f92ffe097fe57e297836e0ceb4097790d3a931","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2608.18557, 'Randomly Permuted Orthogonal Products and Fast Dimension Reduction'] Let \(I\subseteq [d]\) with \(|I|=m\), let $A,B \in O(d)$, and let \(X\subseteq \mathbb S^{d-1}\). We define the \emph{sampled column energy}
	of \(A\) by
	\begin{equation}
		\kappa_I(A)
		:=
		\frac dm
		\max_{k\in[d]}
		\sum_{j\in I}A_{jk}^2
	\end{equation}
	and the \emph{maximal variance} of \(B\) on \(X\) by
	\begin{equation}
		\Theta_B(X)
		:=
		d\sup_{\substack{x\in X\\k\in[d]}}
		\sum_{j=1}^dB_{kj}^2x_j^2.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.712495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eb8f70419d2ec269041a9b6dda6f8928f0204d35fd20304cba08d32c7a7a18a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Maintheorem 3 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] There is a probability law \(\mu_H\) on
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal Y
  :=\mathbb R_+^2\times C_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R_+)^2

\end{equation}
equipped with its product topology, with local uniform convergence in each
path coordinate,
such that
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L_{\widehat{\PP}_a}
  (U_a,V_a,\overline L_a,\overline R_a)
  \Longrightarrow \mu_H.

\end{equation}
Let \((U,V,\overline L,R)\) denote the coordinate random element under
\(\mu_H\).
The left horizon \(U\) is finite almost surely, whereas \(R\) is defined on
the whole half-line.  The path \(\overline L\) is constant on
\([U,\infty)\) almost surely.  Write \(L\) for the restriction of the
constant-extended path \(\overline L\) to \([0,U]\).  Moreover,
\begin{equation}
  J_\infty
  :=\int_0^Ue^{-L(s)}\,\mathrm ds
    +\int_0^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds

\end{equation}
satisfies \(0<J_\infty<\infty\) almost surely and
\begin{equation}
  J_a\Longrightarrow J_\infty,
  \qquad
  \widehat{\EE}_a[J_a^{-1}]
  \longrightarrow
  D_H:=\EE_{\mu_H}[J_\infty^{-1}]\in(0,\infty).

\end{equation}
Finally, the persistence probability satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{T\to\infty}{T^{1-H}}{\PP\bigl(B_t\leq1\text{ for every }0\leq t\leq T\bigr)} = \mathsf C_H,

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  \mathsf C_H
  =\frac{H\,\EE[M]}{\Gamma(1/H)D_H}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:17:33.988683+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ec91966208251dd750ad8896b02bf1da6dde27b770662f1606c5d9c6451ba6b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2609.03386, 'Twisted Gradient Flow Approach to Vegetation-Rainfall-Bushfire Interactions Model'] There exists a constant $C_5>0$, which depends on the following values:

\begin{equation*}
\delta,\quad T, \quad \|v_0\|_{H^1(\Omega)},\quad \int_0^T \|\tilde{z}'(t)\|_{L^2(\Omega)}dt, 
\quad \int_0^T \|\tilde{g}(t)\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2dt,
