{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_302b1cae16b8f43f86f4a5e453dca5e0d81b7b01a6ced6249d0957f3317be615","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:MP_lin of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$ and $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. A probability measure $\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}_{s,\nu}$, on $(C_{[s,T)},\mathcal{B})$, is a solution to MP$(b,\sigma;s,\nu)$ if: 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $x_{s} \sim  \nu$ under $\mathbb{P}$;
\item[(ii)] for any $\varphi\in C_0^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, the process defined by 
\begin{equation}
M_t : =\varphi(x_{t}) - \varphi(x_{s}) - \int_{s}^t \Big(   \langle  b(r, x_r) , \nabla \varphi(x_{r})  \rangle + \frac{1}{2}\text{tr}\big(\sigma \sigma^\top (r , x_r)  \nabla^2 \varphi(x_r)  \big) \Big) dr , \qquad t\in [s, T),
\end{equation}
is a martingale w.r.t. the natural filtration of $(x_t)_{t\in [s,T)}$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.755977+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_304596eea997d542193be87ae459e296fbe4b90a802eae151f10153a5729606d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem main of arXiv:2411.02362, 'Limit theorems for random Dirichlet series: boundary case'] Assume that $\mathbb{E}[\eta]=0$ and $\sigma^2=\mathbb{E}[\eta^2]\in(0,\infty)$. Then
\begin{equation}
C\Big(\Big(\Big(\frac{1}{2\sigma^2\log 1/s\, \log \log\log 1/s}\Big)^{1/2}\sum_{k\ge 2}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}}{k^{1/2+s}}\eta_k : s\in(0, {\rm e}^{-{\rm e}})\Big)\Big)=[-1,1]\quad\text{\rm a.s.}
\end{equation}
In particular,
\begin{equation}
\limsup_{s\to 0+}\Big(\frac{1}{\log 1/s\, \log\log\log 1/s}\Big)^{1/2}\sum_{k\ge 2}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}}{k^{1/2+s}}\eta_k=(2\sigma^2)^{1/2}\quad\text{\rm a.s.}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\liminf_{s\to 0+}\Big(\frac{1}{\log 1/s\, \log\log\log 1/s}\Big)^{1/2}\sum_{k\ge 2}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}}{k^{1/2+s}}\eta_k=-(2\sigma^2)^{1/2}\quad\text{\rm a.s.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859299+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_305453b7b1830fbf51285d3650dd6e8cc77a5a6a49463504601e923bf97f7661","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ is a generalised preferential attachment tree. Then,
    \begin{enumerate} 
        \item  If \[\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{F(j)^2} = \infty \quad \text{almost surely},\] then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ almost surely does not have a persistent hub. 
        \item  Suppose that the following two conditions are satisfied: 
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item There exists a sequence $(x_{j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}_0}$ with $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{x_{j}^2} < \infty$ such that,
				\begin{equation}            
             		F(j) \geq x_{j} \quad \text{almost surely.}
             	\end{equation} 
            \item There exists a $\lambda > 0$ such that 
                \begin{equation} 
                \sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \prod_{j=0}^{i}\E{\frac{F(j)}{F(j) + \lambda}} < \infty.
                \end{equation}
        \end{enumerate}
        Then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ has a unique persistent hub almost surely. 
        \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.212779+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_305640b53589c286d03ba76d75dfe74625394168d38a938b66fa1fcaac8b0ed9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma random_walk_range of arXiv:2512.06640, 'Existence and sharpness of the phase transition for the frog model on transitive graphs'] Let $G$ be a quasi-transitive graph that is of superlinear polynomial growth.
Consider a continuous-time simple random walk on $G$. 
There exist positive constants $c_0 = c_0(G)$ and $c_1 = c_1(G)$ such that the following is true.
Let $x \in V$ and $a > 1$.
For any $H \subseteq B_x(a)$ with $|H| \leq \frac{3}{4}|B_{x}(a)|$  and any $y \in B_x(a)$, if we define $\widetilde{\mathrm{R}}^{\text{H}}_y(t) := \mathrm{R}_y(t) \cap (B_x(a) \setminus H)$, \footnote{Here we omit the dependency of $\mathrm{R}_{y}^{i}(t)$ on the particle label since this is true for any random walk starting at $y$. We also omit the dependency on $y$ when it is clear from the context.} then

\vspace{2mm}

\begin{align*}
    \mathbb P_y \left( |\widetilde{\mathrm{R}}^{\text{H}}(a^2)| \geq c_1 \cdot \theta(a) \right) \geq c_0 \, ,
\end{align*}
where $\theta(a)$ is defined as 
\begin{align}
    \theta(a) := \begin{cases}
        a^2 & \text{if } d \geq 3, \\
        a^2 / \log a & \text{if } d = 2.
    \end{cases}
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.580235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30583a82e7a16df5af95b9ff428b642af740b551cd0b869e6111f53b2a8e523a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:IndS of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We call the following setup the \emph{independent setting}: 
		Let the underlying point process have independent increments and is thus either given by a Poisson point process, cf.~[LastPenrose2017], or by a Bernoulli site-percolated lattice. Further, for a given collection of mutually different vertex pairs \(\{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2}\},\dots,\{\boldsymbol{x}_{k_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\}\), we have 
	\begin{equation*}\tag{Ind}
\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\Big(\bigcap_{j=1}^k \{\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1}\sim \boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}\} \, \Big| \, \mathscr{V} \Big) = \prod_{i=1}^k \mathbf{p}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}), 
	\end{equation*} 
	for a suitable, symmetric function \(\mathbf{p}\). Here, \(\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\) denotes the law where the given points \(\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\) have been added to the graph in the Poisson point process case, or the law conditioned on the event that said vertices survived the site percolation, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750052+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30599e3673db3eb5efe5656bd6481e3cb73581694dd12ddcd15b8318cbb51d61","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equivalence_nonlinear_mp_weak_sol_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ be a probability measure on $(C_{\infty}, \mathcal{B} )$, such that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{P}_{\nu}(x_t \in d y) = v_t(y) dy, 
