{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.771671+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ada64d5eed6787fb9990d35b4c75edae5f2a80787844b649eca14074ccc81e3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 48 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same parameters as in Definition \ref{Def.ParametersEdge}. We further fix $m \in \mathbb{N}$, $\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_m$, such that 
\begin{equation}
\bar{\kappa} > \kappa_m \geq \kappa_{m-1} \geq \cdots \geq \kappa_1 \geq 0,
\end{equation}
and set $\mathcal{T} = \{\kappa_1, \dots, \kappa_m\}$. For $\kappa \in \mathcal{T}$, we define the lattice $\Lambda_{\kappa}(N) = a_{\kappa}(N) \cdot \mathbb{Z} + b_{\kappa}(N)$, where 
\begin{equation}
a_{\kappa}(N) = \sigmap^{-1} N^{-1/2}, \mbox{ and } b_{\kappa}(N) =  - \sigmap^{-1} N^{1/2} \cdot \hp(\kappa).
\end{equation}
We let $\mathbb{P}_N$ be the Pfaffian Schur process from Definition \ref{Def.SchurProcess}. Here, we assume that $M = M(N)$ is sufficiently large so that $M \geq \lfloor \kappa_m N \rfloor$. If $(\lambda^0, \dots, \lambda^M)$ have law $\mathbb{P}_N$, we define the random variables
\begin{equation}
Y_i^{j,N} = \sigmap^{-1} N^{-1/2} \cdot \left( \lambda_i^{\lfloor \kappa_j N \rfloor} - h_2(\kappa_j)  N  - i\right) \mbox{ for } i \in \mathbb{N} \mbox{ and } j \in \llbracket 1, m \rrbracket.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.780639+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ae9201efed13a754e9e176f57f9498640e2913eadeaccc75f4ac72eefdb2cc1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn Def of approximate Tridiagonal Entries of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] Define $\Tilde{a}_0(t):=a_1(t)=e_1^TM(t)e_1$, and for $k\geq 1$, define 
