 where $b^*$ is the unique solution for $b$ in the equation\footnote{The equation $2b\theta_1(b)=1$ simplifies to $\ln(1-b)=-\frac{3}{2}b$.  } $2b\theta_1(b)=1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:16:46.724188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3d7ad4fc15e061cf24e0e9145a40fa72ca4c373dd1dc6041caa4de41b82402cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop.localtimesBRW of arXiv:2411.19145, 'On the intersection of critical percolation clusters and other tree-like random graphs'] There exists $C>0$, such that for any $n\ge 1$, and any $x_1,\dots,x_n,z \in \mathbb Z^d$ (possibly with repetition), with $d\ge 5$, 
