{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.033996+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e0adaa29ee0badfa50b50e6729f76cdd31493660bada605789a2ed0adbee7da","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2605.03057, 'Uniform-in-time quantitative fluctuations of large scale interacting particle systems'] Assume Assumption~\ref{ass:smooth}. Let $\left(\mu_s\right)_{0\le s\le t}$ be the law flow of the McKean--Vlasov SDE~\eqref{eq:MV}.
Let $\varphi\in C_b^5\left(\R^d\right)$ and let $\left(\psi_s\right)_{0\le s\le t}$ be a classical solution to the backward equation
\begin{equation}

\partial_s\psi_s+\mathcal L_{\mu_s}\psi_s+\mathcal A_{\mu_s}\psi_s=0,\qquad s\in\left[0,t\right),\qquad \psi_t=\varphi.
\end{equation}
Then, the limiting variance in Definition \ref{def:uniform-time-limiting-variance-abs-assump} can be written as
\begin{equation}

\sigma_t^2\left(\varphi\right)
=
\Var\left(\psi_0\left(X_0\right)\right)
+\int_0^t \E\left(\norm{\sigma\left(X_s,\mu_s\right)^\top\nabla\psi_s\left(X_s\right)}^2\right) ds,
\end{equation}
where $X$ solves \eqref{eq:MV}. In particular, $\sigma_t^2\left(\varphi\right)=\lim_{N\to\infty}\sigma_{N,t}^2\left(\varphi\right)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.794677+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e0ec67ec7643e8485b7b1d0df6ab80516cced40781811abbe8011b4ea4a57e7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pr:prob:tail of arXiv:2411.09381, 'On the well-posedness of SPDEs with locally Lipschitz coefficients'] If $L_{\sigma}>0$, then
	\begin{equation}
		\mathrm{P}\left\{ |u_{N+1}(t\,,x)| \ge \mathrm{e}^N\right\}
		\le \exp\left( - \frac{N^{3/2}}{64 L_{\sigma}^2 \sqrt{t}}\right),
	\end{equation}
	uniformly for all $t>0$,  $x\in\mathbb{R}$, and
	\[
		N\ge  4 \log ( 4(\|u_0\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R})} +1))\vee
		256 t\max\left(4L_{\sigma}^4, L_b\right).
	\]
	If $\sigma\in L^\infty(\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R})$, then
	\begin{equation}
		\mathrm{P}\left\{ |u_{N+1}(t\,,x)| \ge \mathrm{e}^N \right\}
		\le \exp\left( - \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2N-4L_bt}}{32\mathrm{e}\left(\|u_0\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R})} 
		+ \|\sigma\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R})}t^{1/4}+1\right)^2}\right),
	\end{equation}
	uniformly for all $t>0$,  $x\in\mathbb{R}$, and
	\[
		N\ge \tfrac12\log32 + 2L_bt+ \tfrac12 + \log\left( \|u_0\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R})} + 
		\|\sigma\|_{L^\infty(\mathbb{R}_+\times\mathbb{R})}t^{1/4}+1\right).
	\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.962341+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e1a70eaf525713e72f4586dc253e1c4bea54876eaedccfd03af3559c276d21d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm unimodality.lastm.infinite of arXiv:2411.07103, 'On a connection between total positivity and Bernoulli stopping problems'] Consider the {$m$th  last-success} problem in an infinite sequence of independent Bernoulli trials. Suppose  $\sum_k p_k < \infty$ and  that zero payoff is awarded for never stopping. Then an optimal rule exists,  and the optimal strategy is to stop at the first success, if any, occurring on or after index
	\begin{alignat*}{2} 
		k^* \coloneqq \min\{j \in \mn: e_m(j) \le  e_{m-1}(j)\}.
	\end{alignat*}
	The optimal value of the problem is  $s_m(k^*)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:51:11.500406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e24d332e3ae62fd761a9d8163b493e36de3707d8abbee4dad34429154a84b87","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lm 6 of arXiv:2609.02576, 'Sharp estimates for the Jacobi and trigonometric Dunkl heat kernels'] If $f\in \mathcal{C}^2((0,\infty))$, then
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{L\Big( \big(\frac{\delta_{\rm D}(x)}{\delta(x)}\big)^{1/2} f(x)\Big) }{ \big(\frac{\delta_{\rm D}(x)}{\delta(x)}\big)^{1/2}  f(x)} = \frac{L^{\rm D}f(x)}{f(x)} -\rho^2+R_{k_1,k_2}(x),
	\end{equation*}
	where $L^D$ is the Dunkl Laplacian corresponding to the multiplicity $k=k_1+k_2$, and
	\begin{equation*}
		\big|R_{k_1,k_2}(x)\big| \lesssim 1,\qquad x>0.
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:19:42.624014+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3e3310be377a7db59b3fad0b8fac3ea1ed716f0ba119375c82b085c809828845","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 24 of arXiv:2609.03512, ''] Let $G$ be a graph with $g(G)=3$ and $r(G)\in\{2,3,4,5\}$. Then $\gamma(G)=\gamma_t(G)=2$ if and only if $G$ belongs to one of the following classes:
