{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.735509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41b9d8f737a1dea12817682b5a339a27ae6ae7e9cec517f2f9e013a6c792ccda","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_moving_Lambda of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Suppose $\Lambda_t:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{C}^{D N \times D N}$ is a continuous map such that $\Lambda_t$ satisfies (main_assm of the paper) throughout the evolution. 
     We define $m_t(z)$ as the unique solution to 
 \begin{equation}\nonumber
m_t(z)= \big\langle \left(\Lambda_t -z- m_t(z)\right)^{-1} \big\rangle ,
\end{equation}
such that $\im m_t(z)>0$ whenever $z\in \mathbb{C}_+$.  

Then, we define $M_t=M_t\left({z,\Lambda_t}\right)$ as
\begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            M_t(z)=
            \left(\Lambda_t-z-m_t(z)\right)^{-1},
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
noting that $m_t(z)=\left\langle M_t(z)\right\rangle$. 

The associated probability density is given by
    \begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            \rho_t(E)=\frac{1}{\pi} \lim _{\eta \downarrow 0} \operatorname{Im} m_t(E+i \eta).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
We denote the support of $\rho_t$ by $\left[E_t^{-}, E_t^{+}\right]$, where $E_t^{\pm}$ represent the spectral edges. For $z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta$, we also define $ \kappa_t\equiv \kappa_t(E):=|E_t^{+}-E|\wedge |E-E_t^{-}|$. Finally, we define the quantiles $\gamma_k\left({t}\right)$ of $\rho_t$ as in (equation eq:gammak of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.733027+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41d65f75e9a162469cbbc7f990452ef64f38edccfb501bdd6c18a08c5c994396","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Given a rooted tree $\tau$, we say that $C \subset E_+(\tau)$ is a cut of $\tau$ if any path in $E(\tau)$ with the root of $\tau$ as an endpoint contains at most one edge in $C$. We denote by $\mathbb{C}[\tau]$ the set of all cuts of $\tau$.
	We recall that for a rooted tree $\tau$, there is a natural partial ordering on $E(\tau)$ in which $e \ge e^\prime$ iff $e^\prime$ lies on the unique path from $e$ to the root.

	 We write $\tau_{\ng C}$ for the subgraph consisting of those edges $e \in E(\tau)$ such that there does not exist $\tilde{e} \in C$ with $e \ge \tilde{e}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.967101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41ee8300bc5127832dbbb8338298a0be831202c5859dfd614ddc8d88c3481703","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Verification theorem) of arXiv:2607.22416, 'A multi-player optimal stopping game based on a spider Brownian bridge'] Suppose Player 2 uses the threshold strategy $\sigma^d :=\inf\{s\geq 0: X_{s}\leq - d \sqrt{1-s} \}$ where $d \in [0,\infty)$. Under Assumption \ref{ass:Gamma} and \ref{supermartingale_assumption}, the value function $\overline{V}_{1,\sigma^d}$ defined in \eqref{Valuefunc_P1} coincides with the value function $V_{*,d}(t,x)$ given by \eqref{Expression_V_star} (or $V_{*,0}(t,x)$ given by \eqref{Expression_V_star_0} if $d=0$). Moreover, the stopping time 
