{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:37:08.954597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_452d18b8ee4cbe71c4b5bc2f486c843e1a3794bd0371ebf0b057aae4c645f9b8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:conc_current of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Let $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an ergodic diffusion with invariant measure $\mu$, let
$\overline{J}_t(U)$ be the empirical current in the sense of Definition~(def:empirical_current of the paper) with 
ergodic mean $\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] = \int j_{\textrm{s}} \cdot U \mathrm{d} x$, and let $\nu \ll \mu$
with $\mathrm{d}\nu/\mathrm{d}\mu \in L^2(\mu)$. Then for all $t > 0$ and $a > 0$,
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{P}^\nu\left(\overline{J}_t(U) - \E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] \geq a\right)
  \leq
  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\mu}\right\|_{L^2(\mu)}
  \exp\bigl[-tI^J\bigl(\E^\mu[\overline{J}_t] + a\bigr)\bigr],

\end{align}
where the Cram\'er transform
\begin{align}
  I^J(s) \equiv \sup_{k \in \mathbb{R}}\bigl[k s - \Lambda^J(k)\bigr],

\end{align}
is defined as the Legendre conjugate of the principal eigenvalue $\Lambda^J(k)$~(equation eq:Lambda_J_dirichlet of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.658564+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_452e41eff32a1b925e65ba2eaa9f897fdc15ed3bb6f7f553c338e6328acca4e5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2608.29984, 'On Cauchy Problems for Parabolic Equations with Rough Coefficients'] For any $ 0<s<\frac{1}{2}$, there exists a universal constant $\delta_{0}$ only depends on $c_{0}$ and $s$, such that: under the assumptions (H1), (H2), $(H3)_{\delta_{0},s}$, for any initial data $u_{0}(x)$ with $u_{0}(x)\in H^{s}(\R)$ and force term $f(t,x)\in L^{2}([0,T];H^{s-1}(\R))$, the divergence form equation \eqref{heateqdiv} exists a unique weak solution $u(t,x)$, which satisfies
\begin{equation}
\sup_{0\leq t \leq T}\|u(x,t)\|_{H^{s}_{x}}^{2}+\int_{0}^{T}\|\partial_{x}u\|_{H^{s}_{x}}^{2}dt\lesssim \|u_{0}(x)\|_{H^{s}(\R)}^{2}+\|f(t,x)\|_{L^{2}([0,T];H^{s-1}(\R))}^{2}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:52:51.769380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_453ac9c32192756530778f9a02fdfd5550a25e9da8d4768f8facef35f0bfe91d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi defn:higher-order-path of arXiv:2605.03151, 'Giants through Higher-Order paths in Random Simplicial Complexes'] For any pair of $(d-1)$-dimensional simplices $\sigma, \sigma' \in S_{d-1}(X)$, we say that they are \textit{adjacent} if there exists a $d$-dimensional simplex $\tau \in S_d(X)$ such that $\sigma \subseteq \tau$ and $\sigma' \subseteq \tau$.  
For any pair of $(d-1)$-dimensional simplices $\sigma_1, \sigma_2 \in S_{d-1}(X)$, we say that they are \textit{adjacent} if there exists a $d$-dimensional simplex $\tau \in S_d(X)$ such that $\sigma_1 \subseteq \tau$ and $\sigma_2 \subseteq \tau$. Also, we say that $\sigma$ and $\sigma'$ are connected through a \textit{$d$-dimensional path}, denoted by $\sigma \overset{\scriptscriptstyle d}{\longleftrightarrow} \sigma'$ if there exist distinct elements $(\sigma_i)_{i=0}^m\subseteq S_{d-1}(X)$ with $\sigma_0 = \sigma$ and $\sigma_m = \sigma'$ such that $\sigma_{i-1}$ and $\sigma_{i}$ are adjacent for all $i = 1, \dots, m$.

The smallest simplicial complex containing all $d$-dimensional simplices $\{\tau_i\}_{i=1}^m$ each of which defines the adjacency $\sigma_{i-1} \sim \sigma_i$ is called a $d$-dimensional path of length $m$ connecting $\sigma = \sigma_0$ and $\sigma' = \sigma_m$. The length of the shortest path between $\sigma,\sigma' \in S_{d-1}(X)$ is the minimum value of $m$ such that there exists a $d$-dimensional path between $\sigma,\sigma'$ of length $m$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_456ab82ae572bbaf8a42204f3db184afa7324b19eb6546e42f026f6baead51d3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:pathwise_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $d=d_0$ and let Assumptions (assump:hor of the paper), (assum:sigma_0 of the paper) and (assum:b_0 of the paper)-(a) be in force. Furthermore, assume 

