 where $\left(B_t\right)_{0\leqslant t\leqslant 1}$ is a standard Brownian motion and $\sigma^2$ 
 is given by (equation eq:definition_de_sigma of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.202357+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4572de5b73ecb3b6e06bf48130f3c919409b7f24a37e82194a01fffe05cb8612","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:gradphi-large-deviations of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] There exists a constant $C=C(p)>0$ such that the following holds for any domain $V$ that contains $\ensuremath{\mathcal B}_R(o)$, where $R = Ch^{p-1}$ for $1 < p < 2$ and $R = Ch$ for $2 < p < \infty$. Let $z$ be such that $\ensuremath{\mathcal B}_{R+1}(z) \subset V$. Then there exist absolute constants $c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3,c_4>0$ such that
    \begin{align}
		\exp\Big[-c_0 \beta h^{(p-1)\,\wedge\, 1}\Big] \leq \frac{{\widehat\pi}^{(p)}_V(\phi_o = h)}{{\widehat\pi}^{(p)}_V(\phi_o = h-1)} &\leq \exp\Big[-c_1 \beta h^{(p-1)\,\wedge\, 1}\Big]\,,\\
		\exp\Big[-c_2 \beta h^{p \,\wedge\, 2}\Big] \leq {\widehat\pi}^{(p)}_V(\phi_o = h) &\leq \exp\Big[-c_3\beta h^{p \,\wedge\, 2}\Big]\,,\\        
		{\widehat\pi}^{(p)}_V(\phi_z = h \mid \phi_o = h) &\leq \exp\Big[-c_4 \beta h^{p\,\wedge\,\frac{p}{p-1}}\Big]\,.
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.137640+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45739c54b80ae0a0611d0ecedb5c12088412678015fa0662d04fb2138b2e9d57","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2608.27569, 'Laplace and Dolbeault operators on Sasakian manifolds'] Let $(M^{2n+1},g,\xi)$ be a compact Sasakian manifold. Let  $m\in \{0,\ldots, 2n+1\}$. Then, 
\begin{itemize} 
\item If $m<n$ and $q<0$ (resp. $m>n+1$ and $q>0)$, we have $\mathcal{H}
_{\overline{\partial}}^{m}(q)=0$. 
\item If $m=n$, $q<0$ and $\mathcal{H}_{\overline{\partial}
}^{m}(q)\neq 0$ (resp. $m=n+1$ and $q>0$), then $q^2$ is an eigenvalue of the Hodge Laplacian restricted to $m$-differential forms. 
\item When $\mathcal{H}_{\overline\partial}^{m}(q)$ is nonzero for some $q>0$ and $m<n+1$,  then the first positive eigenvalue $\lambda_{1,m}$  satisfies
$$\lambda_{1,m}<q^2+2q(n-m+1),$$
and, for $1\leq b\leq n-m$, 
$${\rm min}(\lambda_{1,m+2b}, \lambda_{1,m+2b+1})<q^2+2q(n-m+1)+4(b+1)(n-m-b+1).$$
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.574189+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4576f9e6a1ac24a60d5a6a2a56cf89320f0d88628d5af92296e7b0a4a60ff30b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 37 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d>4$. There exist $M>1$ and $c>0$ such that for every $n\geq 1$,
