{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.148429+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4749d3f65382f87854d64eb276efebc74e4f57e86949085066c076a617aef743","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:variance of arXiv:2507.16188, 'The noisy voter model with general initial conditions'] For each $l=1,\ldots,n$ and $k=1,\ldots,q-1$, we have
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Var}_{\mu_G}(\Psi_l^{(k)}) = \frac{1}{\gamma_l}\sum_{v\in V}\pi_G(v)^2| \psi_l(v)|^2 \big(1-(1-\theta)h_G(v)\big)\,,
\end{equation}
with $h_G(v) = \frac{1}{d_G(v)}\sum_{w\sim v}\mathbb{P}(v \leftrightsquigarrow w)$ and $\{v\leftrightsquigarrow w\}$ the event that two independent continuous-time random walks from $v$ and $w$, which move at rate $1-\theta$ and are killed at rate $\theta$, coalesce before dying. 
In particular,
\begin{equation}
\frac{\theta}{\gamma_l}\sum_{v\in V}
\pi_G(v)^2| \psi_l(v)|^2\leq \operatorname{Var}_{\mu_G}(\Psi_l^{(k)}) \leq \frac{1}{\gamma_l}\sum_{v\in V}\pi_G(v)^2| \psi_l(v)|^2
\end{equation}
Furthermore, if we let $M:= \max_v \mathrm{d}_G(v)/ \min_v \mathrm{d}_G(v) $, then we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{\theta}{2M}\leq \frac{\theta}{M\gamma_l}\leq  n \operatorname{Var}_{\mu_G}(\Psi_l^{(k)})  \leq \frac{M}{\gamma_l}\leq \frac{M}{\theta}\,.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.570569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_474e1f158b9d2a928a8b2840ff62451bd43ab9197a3fcc88cf6a1ab62cf8dc16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2608.27307, '2608.27307'] Under Assumption~\ref{ass:basic}, the measure $\bnu$ admits the decomposition
