{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.192940+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4dfb75591d61558b5c2296d026bafd38a1161aab6ef9af88373fcafe76678984","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 9 (GFT) of arXiv:2609.03924, 'Beyond normal fluctuations in local laws for Wigner matrices'] Let $W^{(1)}=\big(w^{(1)}_{ab}\big)_{a,b=1}^N$ and $W^{(2)}=\big(w^{(2)}_{ab}\big)_{a,b=1}^N$ be two Wigner matrices satisfying Assumption~\ref{ass:momass}. Fix $\Gamma\in\N$ and assume that 
\begin{equation}
\E \big(w^{(1)}_{ab}\big)^{\gamma_1}\big(w^{(1)}_{ba}\big)^{\gamma_2} = \E \big(w^{(2)}_{ab}\big)^{\gamma_1}\big(w^{(2)}_{ba}\big)^{\gamma_2},\quad \gamma_1+\gamma_2\le \Gamma,\, \gamma_1,\gamma_2\in\N\cup\{0\},

\end{equation}
Let $\Delta^{(j)}$ be defined as in \eqref{eq:def_Delta} but with $W$ replaced by $W^{(j)}$, $j=1,2$. Then for any fixed $n_1,n_2\in\N\cup\{0\}$ and $\bm{k}\in\N^{n_1},\bm{l}\in\N^{n_2}$ it holds that
\begin{equation}
\E \left[\eta^{|\bm{k}|+|\bm{l}|}\Delta^{(1)} (\bm{k})\overline{\Delta^{(1)}(\bm{l})}\right]=\E \left[\eta^{|\bm{k}|+|\bm{l}|}\Delta^{(2)} (\bm{k})\overline{\Delta^{(2)}(\bm{l})}\right] + \mathcal{O}\left(N^{-(\Gamma-3)/2+\xi}\right),

\end{equation}
for any fixed $\xi>0$, uniformly in $z\in\mathcal{D}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.098980+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e06e595e173cbf0a5515304ae19b5b816b33533b45e83a5f05a52fe12bd594c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] The subspace $\mathcal{L}_{U,\mathcal{M}}(\mathcal{G}^{\star}) \subseteq \mathcal{L}_{U}(\mathcal{G}^{\star})$ is defined as the set of 
elements $\mu$ of $\mathcal{L}_{U}(\mathcal{G}^{\star})$ such that for $(G,o,w,(f_i)_{i \in \{1,..,I\}}) \sim \mu$, we have that $f_I(u,v)$ maps $E$ to $\{0,1\}$ and the subset $\{(u,v), f_I(u,v)=1 \}$ almost surely induces a matching on $G$ in the sense that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left( \exists u,u',v \in V, u \neq u' \text{ and } f(v,u)=f(v,u')=1\right)=0.
