{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.492602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e9a3555e7a9263cd490e325a3d887ff2002fae693144ae4bc3890d4a3395738","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 243 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] \cite{MumTata}
 Let $\Sigma$ be a compact Riemann surface of genus $\mathsf{g}$ equipped with a lagrangian sublattice $\mathcal{L} \subset H_1(\Sigma,\amsmathbb{Z})$. There exists a unique meromorphic bidifferential $\mathcal{B}^{\Sigma,\mathcal{L}}$ with the following properties.
\begin{itemize}
\item It is symmetric in its two variables.
\item It has double poles on the diagonal with biresidue $1$, \textit{i.e.} for any $p \in \Sigma$ and local coordinate $\zeta_p$ near $p$, we have
\[
\mathcal{B}^{\Sigma,\mathcal{L}}(z_1,z_2)\,\, \mathop{=}_{z_1,z_2 \rightarrow p}\,\, \frac{\dd\zeta_{p}(z_1)\dd\zeta_{p}(z_2)}{(\zeta_p(z_1) - \zeta_p(z_2))^2} + O(1).
\]
\item It is normalized on $\mathcal{L}$, \textit{i.e.} for any $\mathsf{a} \in \mathcal{L}$ we have $\oint_{z \in \mathsf{a}} \mathcal{B}^{\Sigma,\mathcal{L}}(z_0,z) = 0$.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:54:57.593747+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e9a7aa530cbd80bb3e9b11a4ca3c928d7704c5e742bb1fe356b7899ec14dcf2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem Lemma_A.2 of arXiv:2412.03370, 'TASEP with a general initial condition and a deterministically moving wall'] Let $(x(t),t\geq 0)$ be a TASEP with step initial condition and let $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Then, it holds
	\begin{equation}
	\lim_{T \to \infty} \mathbb{P}( x_{\alpha T}(T) \geq (1-2 \sqrt{\alpha})T - s c_1(\alpha) T^{1/3}) = F_{\textup{GUE}}(s), 
	\end{equation} where $c_1(\alpha) = \tfrac{(1-\sqrt{\alpha})^{2/3}}{\alpha^{1/6}}$.

	In addition, we have the following estimates on the lower and the upper tail:
	uniformly for all large times $T$ and for $\alpha$ in a closed subset of $(0,1)$, there exist constants $C,c > 0$ such that
	\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}( x_{\alpha T}(T) \leq (1-2\sqrt{\alpha})T - s c_1(\alpha) T^{1/3}) \leq C e^{-c s} \ \ \textrm{for} \ s > 0 
	\end{equation}
	and
	\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}(x_{\alpha T}(T) \geq (1-2 \sqrt{\alpha})T + s c_1(\alpha) T^{1/3}) \leq C e^{-c s^{3/2}} \ \ \textrm{for} \ 0 < s = o(T^{2/3}). 
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.785729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e9b793c685f62356aa88b45f495473018a378c3d91a1b1a53d32a6d6f48da51","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 18 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $p(z_1, z_2)$, with $p(0, 0)=1$, be a non-constant 
bivariate polynomial with real coefficients. Then $p$ admits a representation 
\begin{equation} p(z_1,z_2)=\det (I_{|n|} - K Z_n ),
\end{equation}
with $n=(n_1,n_2)=\deg p$ and $K\in {\mathbb R}^{|n|\times |n|}$ a contraction, where $|n|=n_1+n_2$ and
$Z_n=z_1I_{n_1}\oplus z_2I_{n_2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.701594+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4e9c8a2164aa97fe79441a4fe56e07419d431714d7a5b017fa1652521205cdb8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor co1 of arXiv:2411.17216, 'Large deviations of the empirical measures of a strong-Feller Markov process inside a subs'] Assume {\rm {\bf (C1)}}$\to$ {\rm {\bf (C5)}}, and let $\nu \in  \mathcal P_{\mathbf W}(\mathscr D)$.
 For any measurable $\tau$-neighborhood $\mathcal{N}$ of the q.e.d. $\pi_{\mathscr D}:=\varphi_{\mathscr D} \mu_{\mathscr D}$, there are constants $C, \delta>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P_{\nu} [L_T\notin \mathcal{N}|T<\sigma_{\mathscr D}] \leqslant C e^{-\delta T},\ \forall T>0.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.139866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ea16422e774f0c8a4870971082d07e5de13a5296e614609c9d854b0c9711e36","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:consistent of arXiv:2510.13572, 'Coalescence in Markov chains'] A probability measure $\mu$ on $\mathcal{F}_S$ is \emph{consistent} with $P\in\mathcal{P}_S$,
in which case we say that the pair $(P,\mu)$ is \emph{consistent},  if
\begin{equation}
p_{i,j} = \mu\bigl(\{f\in\mathcal{F}_S: f(i)=j\}\bigr), \qquad  i,j\in S.
\end{equation}
Let $\mathcal L_P$ denote the set of probability measures $\mu$ on $\mathcal{F}_S$ 
that are consistent with $P\in\mathcal{P}_S$.  \hfill $\blacktriangleleft$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.662489+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4ea43c391a7a1ee6ee203d1ef297bfcf594d5c63b1841df80f1db7b806d6feba","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Let $1<p<\infty$. Given a matrix weight $W\in A_p$, we have
