{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.719055+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_533c4b0c7fb3ecb27d3cb787d812ac86686bc69385ecac369d3818627123a35f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:neg_terme_biais of arXiv:2605.30999, 'Functional central limit theorems for $U$-statistics of $\beta$-mixing data'] Let $h\colon S^m\to\mathbb R$ be a canonical kernel. Suppose that $\sum_{k=1}^{+\infty} k\beta\left(k\right)$ is finite. 
Suppose that $\ens{h\pr{X_{\bm{i}}}^2,\bm{i}\in\operatorname{Inc}^m}$ is uniformly integrable.

Then 
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{n\to +\infty}\frac 1{n^{m-1/2  }}\sum_{\bm{i}\in\operatorname{Inc}^m_n}
    \abs{\E{h\pr{X_{\bm{i}}}}}=0.
\end{equation}
Let $\alpha\in\left(0,1/2\right)$. Suppose that 
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{t>0}\sup_{\bm{i}\in\operatorname{Inc}^m}t^{p\left(\alpha\right)}
    \mathbb P\pr{\abs{h\pr{X_{\bm{i}}}}>t }<+\infty,
\end{equation*}
where $p\left(\alpha\right)=\left(1/2-\alpha\right)^{-1}$. Then
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{n\to +\infty}\frac 1{n^{m-1/2-\alpha }}\sum_{\bm{i}\in\operatorname{Inc}^m_n}
    \abs{\E{h\pr{X_{\bm{i}}}}}=0.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

