{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.974716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5503e1fcfdad9fb217bc7de253b19f37c13fa0e55e4a6e2c12e7287f28ee4b44","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:defA of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For any ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} \in\text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$}$ such that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\geqslant1$, we set $k\equiv k({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\eqdef\min\{i\in[p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})]:r_i=1\}$. Moreover, to have notation for writing Leibniz rule for scale derivatives of cumulants, for ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} = \big( (\tau_{i},0,u_i) \big)_{i=1}^{p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})} \in \text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$} $ (note that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}}) = 0$) and $1 \leqslant k \leqslant p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$, we define \textcolor{white}{$\mathbf d$}
\begin{equs}
    \mathbf{d}_{k} {\boldsymbol{\tau}} \eqdef \Big( (\tau_{i}, \mathbf{1}\{i = k\},u_i) : 1 \leqslant i \leqslant p({\boldsymbol{\tau}}) \Big) \in \widehat{\mathcal{T}}\,.
\end{equs}
Note that is holds $k(\mathbf{d}_k{\boldsymbol{\tau}})=k$.

Fix ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} \in\widehat\mathcal{T}$ such that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\geqslant1$, and recall the definition of $\text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)$ given in Definition~(def:defBmu of the paper). Moreover, fix $v_1,v_2\in\{0,1,2\}$ such that $v_1+v_2=u_k$. We combine $v_1,v_2$ and $(\sigma_1,\sigma_2)\in\text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)$ in the forest ${\boldsymbol{\sigma}} = \big((\sigma_1,v_1),(\sigma_2,v_2) \big)$. 

We then define 
\begin{equs}
\text{$ {\mathrm{Ind}}$}(\text{${{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}$})\eqdef
\Bigg\{ 
\big({\boldsymbol{\sigma}}, \pi,\rho \big):
\begin{array}{c}
(\sigma_1,\sigma_2) \in \text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)\,,\\
 v_1,v_2\in\{0,1,2\}:\,v_1+v_2=u_k\,,
\\
(\pi,\rho) \in \mathcal{Q}\big([p(\text{${{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}$})]\setminus\{k\},[2]\big)
\end{array}
\Bigg\}\;.
\end{equs}
The set $\mathrm{Ind}({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$ will appear as an indexing set for a sum in our cumulant equation. 
To motivate this notation, we note that upon fixing ${\boldsymbol{\sigma}}$, we are working with a cumulant of the form (suppressing time arguments) 

\[
\kappa_{p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})}
\Big( \big(\xi^{\tau_i}_{{\eps,\mu}}: i \in [p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})] \setminus \{k\} \big), 
\xi^{\sigma_2} \graft^{\mu} \xi^{\sigma_1}
\Big)
\]
We then generate a sum over $(\pi,\rho)$ using (equation eq:relcum of the paper), each term corresponding to a product of joint cumulants indexed by the $|\rho|$ different blocks of  $\rho$. 

We fix $({\boldsymbol{\sigma}},\pi,\rho)\in\mathrm{Ind}({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$. For each $1 \leqslant q \leqslant |\rho|$, we define ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{q} \in \text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$}$ via the concatenation of ordered forests ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_q \eqdef {{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}_{\pi_q}\sqcup{{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}}_{\rho_q}$, where we set  ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_q={\boldsymbol{\sigma}}_{\rho_q}$ if $p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})=1$. 

Now, in order to define our analogue of the r.h.s. of (equation eq:flowLabelled of the paper) for the cumulant flow, we fix some representative $\underline{\boldsymbol{\tau}}\in {\boldsymbol{\tau}}$ verifying (equation eq:restrictionforest of the paper). For $j\in[2]$, we also fix some representatives $\underline{\sigma}_j\in\sigma_j$, along with $f\in\mathrm{Aut}(\underline{\tau}_k)$ and $n\in N(\underline{\sigma}_1)$ such that $\underline{\tau}_k=\underline{\sigma}_2\graft_n\underline{\sigma}_1$.

Then, following Remark~(rem:2_24 of the paper), we introduce the short-hand notations
 \begin{equs}       
    \ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2,j}\eqdef \begin{cases}  
  0 & \text{for} \;j= 1\,, \\
   \ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2}& \text{for} \;j= 2\,, \\
\end{cases}
           \quad\text{and}\quad
           t_{\underline{\sigma}_i} \equiv t_{\underline{\sigma}_i}(t_{\underline{\tau}_k},w)\eqdef \begin{cases}  
    t_{\underline{\tau}_k} & \text{for} \;j= 1\,, \\
      w & \text{for} \;j= 2\,. \\
\end{cases}
   \end{equs}

Recall that $f$ was promoted to a map
$\text{$\mathbf{f}$}:\bigotimes_{j=1}^2\text{${\mcb{C}}$}[\underline{\sigma}_j+\ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2,j}]\longrightarrow{\mcb{C}}[\underline{\tau}_k]$. We further promote","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.654450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5504f48df330a25e9a83ee3bbf6a2f13f1a54dd5e754e97d39419f22b53b02fc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 7 of arXiv:2607.24561, 'Range convergence and sharp one-arm asymptotics\\for the critical percolation cluster in h'] For $x \in \partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$, we say that $x$ is $s$-good if $\mathcal T_{s}(i;x)$ holds. Otherwise, we say that $x$ is $s$-bad. For $x \in \partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$, we call $x$ a $K$-regular point if the following two conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $x$ is $s$-good for $s\ge K$;
\item $x$ is connected to $0$ in $\mathcal Q_{z, i}^c$.
\end{enumerate}
Similarly, if $x$ is connected to $0$ in $\mathcal Q_{z, i}^c$, but it is not $K$-regular, then we say it is $K$-irregular. We write $X_{r,i}^{K-\mathrm{reg}}$ (resp.\ $X_{r,i}^{K-\mathrm{irr}}$) for the number of $K$-regular (resp.\ irregular) points on $\partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.269250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_550652387292eceda2f1b8f8338abd7db08f30c7048863b20f2259513753a567","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mydef gg1 of arXiv:2411.08430, 'The Restricted Isometry Property of Block Diagonal Matrices Generated by $\varphi$-Sub-Gau'] For an Orlicz $N$-function $\varphi$ satisfying $Q-$condition, then for $1 \leq p<\infty$ distribution-dependent Talagrand-type $\gamma$-functional 
	is defined by 
	\begin{eqnarray}
		\gamma_{\varphi,p}(T, d)=\inf\sup _{t \in T} \sum_{n\geq k}^{\infty} \varphi^{*(-1)}(2^{n}) \Delta \left(A_n(t)\right),
	\end{eqnarray}	
	where the infimum is taken over all admissible sequences and $k:=\left\lfloor\frac{\log (p)}{\log (2)} \right\rfloor$, $A_n(t)$ is the unique element of $\mathcal{T}_n$ which contains $t$, $\Delta$ is the diameter of the $A_n(t)$ with respect to the norm $\|\cdot\|_{\tau_{\varphi}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.769268+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_551206935989cbd35bee4adc1bc8558fa11a38f7e8c3b4f609b15d23df640367","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 28 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Assume the same notation as in Definition \ref{Def.AvoidingLawBer}. Fix $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $M \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \cup \infty$, $T_0, T_1 \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $T_0 < T_1$, and $\vec{x}\,^b, \vec{y}\,^b, \vec{x}\,^t, \vec{y}\,^t \in \mathfrak{W}_k$. We assume that 
\begin{equation}
x_i^t \geq x^b_i \geq x_i^t - M \mbox{, and }  y_i^t \geq y_i^b \geq y_i^t - M \mbox{ for } i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket,
\end{equation}
and also that $\Omega_{\ice}(T_0, T_1, \vec{x}\,^b, \vec{y}\,^b)$ and $\Omega_{\ice}(T_0, T_1, \vec{x}\,^t, \vec{y}\,^t)$ are both non-empty. Then, there exists a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$, which supports two $\llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$-indexed geometric line ensembles $\mathfrak{Q}^t$ and $\mathfrak{Q}^b$ on $\llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$ such that the law of $\mathfrak{Q}^{t}$ {\big (}resp. $\mathfrak{Q}^b${\big )} under $\mathbb{P}$ is given by $\mathbb{P}_{\ice,\operatorname{Geom}}^{T_0, T_1, \vec{x}\,^t, \vec{y}\,^t}$ {\big (}resp. $\mathbb{P}_{\ice,\operatorname{Geom}}^{T_0, T_1, \vec{x}\,^b, \vec{y}\,^b}${\big )} and such that $\mathbb{P}$-almost surely 
$$ Q_i^t(r) \geq Q^b_i(r) \geq Q^t_i(r) - M \mbox{ for all $i \in \llbracket 1, k \rrbracket$ and $r \in \llbracket T_0, T_1 \rrbracket$.} $$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.422482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_55162a84a78b2c0e6c29ff5a59b1d7bf8fb3c676d29b1fef0ac380a6bcf90b51","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition moment cumulant of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] In an operator-valued free probability space $(\mathcal{A},\mathcal{B},\Delta)$, we define the corresponding (operator-valued) free cumulants $(\kappa_{n}^{\mathcal{B}})_{n\in\mathbb{N}},\kappa_{n}^{\mathcal{B}}:\mathcal{A}^{n}\rightarrow\mathcal{B}$ by the moment cumulant formula :
        \begin{equation}
            \Delta(a_{1}\cdots a_{n})=\sum_{\pi\in NC(n)}\kappa_{\pi}^{\mathcal{B}}(a_{1},\cdots,a_{n}),
        \end{equation}
        where arguments of $\kappa_{\pi}^{\mathcal{B}}$ are distributed according to the blocks of $\pi$, but the cumulants are nested inside each other according to the nesting of the blocks of $\pi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.442714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5516b09f12d9caa3de4ddc0243f19fc97f0d302dfd128f59b8af83f53eaa061e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary eq:cor-K-sum of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] For any given real numbers  $t_1,\dots,t_s>0$,    $\tau_1,\dots,\tau_q$ and  $a_{q+1}, \ldots,a_{r}\in (-a,a)$, suppose that 
\begin{equation}
    n_i\,N^{-1/3}\;\longrightarrow\;t_i, \quad i=1,\ldots,s,
\end{equation}
and  \begin{equation}
N^{1/3}(a_j-1)\;\longrightarrow\;\tau_j,
\quad j=1,\dots,q, \end{equation}
as \(N\to\infty\).   Then the  scaled cumulants 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\frac{\kappa_{\widetilde X}(n_1,\ldots, n_s)}{(n_1+1) \cdots (n_s+1)}=\widetilde{\mathcal{K}}(t_1,\dots,t_s):
=\sum_{\Gamma\in\mathcal{T}_s}
\mathcal{F}_\Gamma(t_1,\dots,t_s).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.542902+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_551707dce2f6ac8b1380dea3c5f1669ecaf4e65c41aa37f7dbd7e905e09ca69d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:matrix_local of arXiv:2603.27906, 'Marked GUE-corners process in doubly periodic dimer models'] For $(z_1,w_1)$ and $(z_2,w_2)$ in a neighbourhood of $q_{\infty}$, let
~$z_i=1+\frac{Z_i}{N^\frac{1}{2}}$,~$i=1,2$, and set
\begin{equation}
\tilde g(\ell x+i,2y+j)=\prod_{m=1}^i(1+\alpha_m^{-1}\beta_m)(1+\alpha_{m+1}\beta_m^{-1})\alpha_{i+1}^{-j},
\end{equation}
and let~$\nu$ be given in~(equation eq:period_function of the paper). Then, as~$N\to \infty$, 
\begin{multline} 
\boldsymbol{\mathcal{M}}_{i_1,j_1,i_2,j_2}\left(z_1,w_1;z_2,w_2\right)

= \nu(\ell-i_2,j_2)\frac{Z_1^{i_1}}{Z_2^{i_2}}\frac{\tilde g(\ell x_2+i_2,2y_2+j_2)}{\tilde g(\ell x_1+i_1,2y_1+j_1)}\frac{N^{\frac{i_2}{2}}}{N^{\frac{i_1}{2}}}\left(1+\mathcal{O}\left(\frac{|Z_1|+|Z_2|}{N^{\frac{1}{2}}}\right)\right),
\end{multline}
where, assuming $|Z_k|\leq N^{\frac{1}{2}}$, the implicit constant in the remainder term is independent of $Z_k, x_k, i_k, y_k, j_k$, $k=1,2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859853+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5519337c5696b00b31d152feca9e79a354207680759be66f0ce32bfd8e9c18f5","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem finite-persistence-claims of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Assume Assumption~(ass:gen-ass of the paper) is satisfied. Then, with regards to the process $(\mathscr{T}_{t})_{t \geq 0}$, we have the following claims:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  We have $\lim_{n \to \infty} \max_{u \in \mathscr{T}_{\tau_{n}}} \outdeg{u, \mathscr{T}_{\tau_{n}}} = \infty$ almost surely. 

    \item  If $\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} (X_{i} - X'_{i})$ converges almost surely, then for any $u, v \in \mathcal{U}$ we have 
    \begin{equation} 
    \Prob{\win(u,v) \cup \win(v,u)} = 1.
    \end{equation}
    \item  If $\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} (X_{i} - X'_{i})$ diverges almost surely, there exists an increasing function $\phi\colon \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ such that, for any $u \in \mathcal{U}$, $j \in \mathbb{N}$ we have 
    \begin{equation} 
    \Prob{\exists k \leq \phi(j)\colon \sum_{\ell = 1}^{k} X(uj\ell) < \sum_{\ell = j+1}^{k} X(u\ell)} \geq 1/2.
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.240567+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_551b6cbc68a324767d7260d3d1a8947997b0a0d987feb59a49da5e3ab1611f0b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 61 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] For each $j\in \N$, consider families of ASEPs on the integers $(\eta_t^{N,i,1,j})_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\eta_t^{N,i,2,j})_{t \geq 0}$ under the patched coupling with $i \in \lbr M \rbr$ and $N \in \N$. We define the $\boldsymbol{(\delta,N)}$\textbf{-patched ASEP} $(\eta^{\textup{patch},N,i}_t)_{t \geq 0}$, taking values in $\{0,1\}^{\Z/N\Z}$, as follows. First, let $j=1$, and define
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\textup{patch},N,i}_t(x) := \begin{cases} \eta_t^{N,i,1,1}(x) & \text{ if } (x,t) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\big(0,\frac{1}{2}\big) ,   \\
\eta_t^{N,i,2,1}(x) & \text{ if } (x,t) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\big(\frac{1}{2},1\big) , 
\end{cases}
\end{equation} where the points $x$ are understood modulo $N$. 

In order to define $\eta^{\textup{patch},N,i}_t$ for $t>\delta N^{3/2}$, we proceed inductively. Suppose that we have constructed $(\eta^{\textup{patch},N,i}_t)_{t \in [0,j \delta N^{3/2}]}$ for some $j\in \N$ (the base case $j=1$ is covered above). Then consider two families of ASEPs $(\eta^{N,i,1,j+1}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\eta^{N,i,2,j+1}_t)_{t \geq 0}$, started at time $j\delta N^{3/2}$, where assign for all $i\in \lbr M \rbr$
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
        \eta^{N,i,1,j+1}_{j\delta N^{3/2}}(x) &= \bar{\eta}^{N,i}_{j\delta N^{3/2}}(x) \ \forall x \colon (x,j\delta N^{3/2}) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\Big(-\frac{1}{8},\frac{5}{8}\Big) , \\
        \eta^{N,i,2,j+1}_{j\delta N^{3/2}}(x)&=  \bar{\eta}^{N,i}_{j\delta N^{3/2}}(x) \ \forall x \colon (x,j\delta N^{3/2}) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\Big(\frac{3}{8},\frac{9}{8}\Big)  .
\end{split}
\end{equation} For all other $x$, we assign independent Bernoulli-$\rho$-random variables.
Then $(\eta^{N,i,1,j+1}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ and $(\eta^{N,i,2,j+1}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ evolve according to the patched coupling between time $j\delta  N^{3/2}$ and $(j+1)\delta N^{3/2}$, independently of the processes up to time $\delta j N^{3/2}$. Set for all $s \in [j \delta N^{3/2},(j+1)\delta N^{3/2}]$,
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\textup{patch},N,i}_{s}(x) := \begin{cases} \eta_{s}^{N,i,1,j+1}(x) & \text{ if } (x,s) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\big(0,\frac{1}{2}\big) ,   \\
\eta_s^{N,i,2,j+1}(x) & \text{ if } (x,s) \in \mathcal{I}_{\delta,N}^{\rho}\big(\frac{1}{2},1\big) . 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.581669+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_551ee9daa0bcb79de89c882eaf6d183a30fd4adffc9d2184d3da5057fc9f3a14","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Polynomial Gaussian envelope) of arXiv:2609.01319, 'Matched Queries for Curvature and Density at Branching Junctions'] Fix a compact $\cQ\subset\R^D$. There are $c>0$ and constants $C_{m,\cQ}$ such that the following holds. Let $v\in\Sph^{D-1}$, $u\ge0$, and let $q_t=uv+t\xi$ for $0\le t\le1$, with $\norm{\xi}\le u/2$. For every fixed mixed-derivative order $m$ of $K_z(q)=e^{-\norm{z-q}^2/2}$ in $(z,q)$,
\begin{equation}
\abs{\partial^m K_z(q_t)}
\le C_{m,\cQ}(1+u)^{m}e^{-cu^2},
\qquad z\in\cQ.

\end{equation}
The same form holds for vector and matrix derivatives.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:40:27.002679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_552a37e8c2db00e64e19c33daecfd83141c70b7123807b90679c9f85575e475e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:tree of arXiv:2604.17253, 'Large Deviations for the Nonlinear Schrödinger Equation with Randomized Quasi-Periodic Ini'] For all $k\geq1$, we have
\[
{c}_k(t,\boldsymbol{n})
=
\sum_{\gamma^{(k)}\in\Gamma^{(k)}}
\sum_{\substack{\spadesuit^{(k)}\in{\mathrm D}^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}\\
\lambda(\spadesuit^{(k)})=\boldsymbol{n}}}
\mathfrak C^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(\spadesuit^{(k)})
\mathfrak I^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(t,\spadesuit^{(k)})
\mathfrak F^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(\spadesuit^{(k)}),
\]
where for $0\in\Gamma^{(k)}$,
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak C^{(k,0)}(\spadesuit^{(k)})
&={c}(\lambda(\spadesuit^{(k)}))g_{\lambda(\spadesuit^{(k)})},\\
\mathfrak I^{(k,0)}(t,\spadesuit^{(k)})
&=e^{-\mathrm{i} Q(\lambda(\spadesuit^{(k)})) t},\\
\mathfrak F^{(k,0)}(\spadesuit^{(k)})
&=1;
\end{align*}
for $1\in\Gamma^{(1)}$,
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak C^{(1,1)}(\spadesuit^{(1)})
&=\prod_{j=1}^3\{{c}(\boldsymbol{n}^j)g_{\boldsymbol{n}^j}\}^{\ast^{[j-1]}},\\
\mathfrak I^{(1,1)}(t,\spadesuit^{(1)})
&=\int_0^t e^{-\mathrm{i} Q(\lambda(\spadesuit^{(1)}))(t-s)}
\prod_{j=1}^{3}
\left\{e^{-\mathrm{i} Q(\boldsymbol{n}^j) s}\right\}^{\ast^{[j-1]}}
\,\mathrm{d}s,\\
\mathfrak F^{(1,1)}(\spadesuit^{(1)})
&=\mathrm{i}{\varepsilon^\alpha} ;
\end{align*}
and for $\gamma^{(k)}=(\gamma_j^{(k-1)})_{j=1}^3$,
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak C^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(\spadesuit^{(k)})
&=\prod_{j=1}^3
\left\{\mathfrak C^{(k-1,\gamma_j^{(k-1)})}(\spadesuit_j^{(k-1)})\right\}^{\ast^{[j-1]}},\\
\mathfrak I^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(t,\spadesuit^{(k)})
&=\int_0^t e^{-\mathrm{i} Q(\lambda(\spadesuit^{(k)}))(t-s)}
\prod_{j=1}^3
\left\{\mathfrak I^{(k-1,\gamma_j^{(k-1)})} (s,\spadesuit_j^{(k-1)})\right\}^{\ast^{[j-1]}}
\,\mathrm{d}s,\\
\mathfrak F^{(k,\gamma^{(k)})}(\spadesuit^{(k)})
&=\mathrm{i}{\varepsilon^\alpha} \prod_{j=1}^3
\left\{\mathfrak F^{(k-1,\gamma_j^{(k-1)})}(\spadesuit_j^{(k-1)})\right\}^{\ast^{[j-1]}}.
\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

