for all sufficiently large 
\(\lvert\xi\rvert\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.736847+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_581646594013b7f5ac21ee02ffce2b3a71f73b27a16e02d614f83e876827a48e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq:slope_ineq1 of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] We define a partial relation $\prec$ on (finite) fine slopes generated by the following inequalities:
    \begin{equation}
        s^\heartsuit \prec t^\diamondsuit
    \end{equation}
    whenever $s < t$ and $\heartsuit, \diamondsuit \in \{\natural, \flat, \sharp, \dagger\}$, and
    \begin{equation}
        s^\flat \prec s^\dagger \prec s^\sharp, \qquad \text{and} \qquad s^\dagger \prec s^\dagger.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.669357+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_58187cabbd95b428261beb034f6cbce84f900e2bbf6a0623a0ca90f69f0275aa","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 10 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] A \textbf{perversity} 
   	$\bar{p}: \mathbb{Z}_{ \geq 2} \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}$ is a function such that $\overline{p}(2)=0$ and
   	$\overline{p}(k+1)- \overline{p}(k) \in \{1,0\}$. The \textbf{complementary perversity} $\bar{q}$ of $\bar{p}$ 
   	is the one with $\overline{p}(k)+\overline{q}(k)=k-2$. Furthermore, the lower middle perversity is defined by $\bar{m}(k)=[\frac{k-2}{2}]$. Its complementary perversity is called the upper middle perversity and is defined by $\bar{n}(k)=[\frac{k-1}{2}]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.584504+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_583312d1c134b434d88b083751487e723d14aaea089c8388aeffa632a04547c0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def:hyperunif of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] A point process $\mathsf{P}\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ is  hyperuniform if \begin{align*}
       \frac{1}{n} \int (\xi(\Lambda_n)-n)^2\mathsf{P}(d\xi)\xrightarrow{n\to \infty}0.
    \end{align*}
    The process $\mathsf{P}$ is class-1-hyperuniform if  $ \int (\xi(\Lambda_n)-n)^2\mathsf{P}(d\xi)=O(1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.318170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_58422738689619b7e8bcdff0e28147f6f63688aea2bdc8d7c9c83e64369209a2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ITS-carac of arXiv:2411.14926, 'Convex combinations of random variables stochastically dominate the parent for a new class'] A random variable $X$ is {\rm InvSub}\ if and only if
