{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:37:08.954439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5db45ff6a6cffb324492836e30dfc9882c46cebc1173fa0cad992b809e906e16","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:conc_density of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Let $(X_t)_{t \geq 0}$ be an ergodic diffusion with invariant measure $\mu$, let
$\overline{\rho}_t(V)$ denote the empirical density according to Definition~(def:empirical_density of the paper) with 
ergodic (time-independent) mean $\E^\mu[\overline{\rho}_t]$, and
let $\nu \ll \mu$ with $\mathrm{d}\nu/\mathrm{d}\mu \in L^2(\mu)$. 
Then for all $t > 0$ and
$a > 0$,
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{P}^\nu\left(\overline{\rho}_t(V) - \E^\mu[\overline{\rho}_t] \geq a\right)
  \leq
  \left\|\frac{\mathrm{d}\nu}{\mathrm{d}\mu}\right\|_{L^2(\mu)}
  \exp\bigl[-t I^\rho(\E^\mu[\overline{\rho}_t] + a)\bigr],

\end{align}
where the Cram\'er transform
\begin{align}
I^\rho(s) \equiv \sup_{k \in \mathbb{R}}\bigl[k s - \Lambda^\rho(k)\bigr],

\end{align}
is defined as the Legendre conjugate of the principal eigenvalue $\Lambda^\rho(k)$~(equation eq:Lambda_rho_dirichlet of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.824147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dbcbe452a6b82ef2cbccc3218bd82135ae66d24f25a26b90e2c14949569ece6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:hilbert-strata of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $h\geq1$, $k\geq2$, and a field $K$.  Recall the open normalized
exponent-shape simplex
\[
 \mathcal S_k=
 \{x\in\mathbb R^k:0=x_1<x_2<\cdots<x_k=1\}
\]
and put
\[
 N_h(x)=\left|\{u\cdot x:u\in\mathcal U_{h,k}\}\right|.
\]
For every $n\in\mathcal R(h,k)$, the locus
\[
 \mathcal H_{h,k}(n)=\{x\in\mathcal S_k:N_h(x)=n\}
\]
is a finite disjoint union of relatively open rational polytopes, and
\begin{equation}
 \max\{\dim P:P\text{ is a cell of }\mathcal H_{h,k}(n)\}
 =k-2-r_{h,k}(n).
\end{equation}
For rational $x\in\mathcal S_k$, choose $D\geq1$ with
$Dx\in\mathbb Z^k$ and let $C_x\subset\mathbb P^{k-1}$ be the projective
monomial curve defined by the kernel of
\[
 K[X_1,\ldots,X_k]\longrightarrow K[s,t],
 \qquad X_i\longmapsto s^{D(1-x_i)}t^{Dx_i}.
\]
The kernel is independent of the choice of $D$, and
\[
 H_{C_x}(h)=N_h(x).
\]
Consequently, $r_{h,k}(n)$ is exactly the codimension in exponent-shape
space of the largest stratum with degree-$h$ Hilbert value $n$.
If $h\geq2$, $k\geq3$, and $A_q$ is uniform in $\binom{[q]}k$, then
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P(|hA_q|=n)
 =k!c_{h,k,n}q^{-r_{h,k}(n)}
  +O_{h,k}(q^{-r_{h,k}(n)-1}),
\end{equation}
and hence the same integer has the four equivalent descriptions
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 r_{h,k}(n)
 =k-\deg F^{(k)}_{h,n}
 =\operatorname{codim}_{\mathcal S_k}\mathcal H_{h,k}(n)
 =-\lim_{q\to\infty}
   \frac{\log\mathbb P(|hA_q|=n)}{\log q}.}
\end{equation}
Here the codimension of a finite polyhedral union means the codimension of
its largest-dimensional cell.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:28:53.673463+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5dd3de3ed068697b634a1d2688110319a0e5a4468b518db60f8558ab4edfe3c0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 (bound on the discrete Poisson solution) of arXiv:2605.13648, 'Sticky CIR process with potential: invariant measure and exact sampling'] \label[lemma]{lem:poisson-sup}
