{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.291313+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_659836c4f1bc4eb5a285e59e9195f942c17aceff381c5b5416322ff811cd8a2a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theo:main of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Let $X$ be a complex or real $N\times N$ i.i.d. matrix satisfying Assumption~(ass:chi of the paper). Fix (small) $c_0,\delta>0$ and an open bounded domain $\mathbf{D}\subset \Omega\subset \mathbf{C}$. Then the following holds.

\emph(i) The process of centered linear statistics $L^{(k)}(f)$ converges to a centered Gaussian process $L(x,f)$ in the sense of moments: 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{E} \prod_{i=1}^n \left(L^{(k_i)}(f_i)\right)^{p_i} \left(\overline{L^{(k_i)}(f_i)}\right)^{q_i} = \mathbf{E} \prod_{i=1}^n \left(L(x_i,f_i)\right)^{p_i}\left(\overline{L(x_i,f_i)}\right)^{q_i} + \mathcal{O}(N^{-c(\bm{p},\bm{q})})\,\,\,\text{with}\,\,\, x_i:=k_i/N,

\end{equation}
for any $N$-independent $n, p_i,q_i\in\mathbf{N}\cup\{0\}$, and for (possibly $N$-dependent) $x_i\in [c_0,1]$, for $i\in [n]$. The exponent $c(\bm{p},\bm{q})>0$ in (equation eq:main_macro of the paper) depends only on $\sum_{i=1}^n (p_i+q_i)$, and the implicit constant in $\mathcal{O}(\cdot)$ may depend only on $H^{2+\delta}$-norm of $f_i$, $i\in[n]$, and on the constants from Assumption~(ass:chi of the paper).

\emph{(ii)} The covariance structure of $\{L(x,f)\}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Cov}\left(L(x_1,f_1),(x_2,f_2)\right)=C_\beta\left((x_1,f_1),(x_2,f_2)\right),

\end{equation}
where the explicit sesquilinear form $C_\beta$ is defined below in (equation eq:C_beta_2 of the paper)--(equation eq:C_beta_1 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:57.051129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_659dde2b3ed855f04b887a7d7030c30a17d113e91bd0629fc9220f9f24741361","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma dir_E[deg-edges]_asymp of arXiv:2604.05921, 'Simplicity of random hypergraphs'] If $\forall e \in E: \delta_e^{\textnormal{tail}} = \delta^{\textnormal{tail}}\in O(1)$ and $\delta_e^{\textnormal{head}}=\delta^{\textnormal{head}}\in O(1)$ and
