{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:03.899104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_68655f3ea4905a3e7b598d5d0989c292550e0ec2e4428f636e8df5d185ea594b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2606.20169, 'THEORY OF UNCERTAIN PROBABILITY:\\ CAN WE DERIVE THE PROBABILITY DENSITY FUNCTION OF UNCER'] The uncertain distribution of an uncertain random variable is the function $\mathfrak{F}_{\mathbb{X}}:\ \mathcal{R}\rightarrow\left[0, 1\right]$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathfrak{F}_{\mathbb{X}}\left(\mathrm{x}\right)=\mathbb{P}\left(\mathfrak{x}\le\mathrm{x}\right),\mathrm{x}\in\mathcal{R}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

When $\mathfrak{F}_\mathbb{X}\left(\mathrm{x}\right)$ is displayed as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
