{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.764030+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70b57e4defef476e0995f1ba1ad53b8fc81ad709809ae94627b45b8bb47b1de8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 of arXiv:2604.12334, 'On additive averaging kernels for finite Markov chains'] For any subset $S \in \mathcal{D}$ and $P \in \mathcal{L}(\pi)$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \|A_\alpha(S) - \Pi\|_{F,\pi}^2 &= \alpha^2\Tr(P^2) - 2\alpha(1-\alpha)g(S) + 1 - 2\alpha^2\\
         = &\underbrace{2\alpha(1-\alpha) \left(\dfrac{1}{\pi(S)} \sum_{x,y \in S'} \pi(x) P(x,y) + \dfrac{1}{\pi(S')} \sum_{x,y \in S} \pi(x) P(x,y)\right)}_{\text{supermodular}}\\
         &-\underbrace{\left(\dfrac{2\alpha(1-\alpha)}{\pi(S)\pi(S')} + 6\alpha^2 - 4\alpha - 1 - \alpha^2\Tr(P^2)\right).}_{\text{supermodular}}
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.442649+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70be948bac045b482e0789a203fe9b2cda2f9152ad1668e66999e653d08a78fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2604.07478, 'Mixing Times and Cutoff for the Rook's Walk'] Let $P$ be the transition matrix of the RWBD chain. Then, $\Phi$ is an eigenfunction of $P$ with eigenvalue $\alpha$ if
         \[\alpha= 1 - \frac{n}{d(n-1)}\quad \text{and}\quad \Phi(x) = 1 \frac{nx}{d(n-1)}\]
         \begin{proof}
           Clearly $\alpha = \lambda_1$. Hence, consider $K_1(x)$
            \begin{align*}
            K_1(x) &= \sum_{j=0}^1 (-1)^j{x \choose j}{d-x \choose 1-j}(n-1)^{1-j}\\
            &= (-1)^0{x\choose 0}{d-x \choose 1}(n-1)^{1} + (-1)^1{x \choose 1}{d-x\choose 0}(n-1)^{0}\\
            &= (d-x)(n-1) -x\\
            &= d(n-1) -nx\\
            &\propto 1 -\frac{nx}{d(n-1)} = \Phi(x)
            \end{align*}
            Thus, $\Phi(x)$ is an eigenfunction with eigenvalue $\alpha$.
         \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

