{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.604881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_74bc2aa19f11284fd85ead41abfd912eca35982345abeb9f2c05675a9e173219","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary c:purejumppareto of arXiv:2604.03979, 'Stationary Distributions in Monotone Markov Models: Theory and Applications'] Let $h \equiv x_0$, $\nu = \delta_0$, and $\mu =
    \mathrm{Exp}(\theta)$ for some $\theta > 0$. In this setting, for the income
    level $Y = e^X$, the tail of the unique stationary distribution is Pareto:
    for $y > y_0 \coloneq e^{x_0}$,

    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb P(Y > y)
        = q \left(\frac{y}{y_0}\right)^{-\alpha}
        \quad \text{with} \quad
