    \end{equation*}
    In particular, $\rho$ belongs to $C([0,T],\mathscr{M}_{ac})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.552331+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_755e9694e4c7cb3ac17210f4301d3a4be75885796acec5fbb65d997cf5bd48a2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 27 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] For all $k\in \naturals$, we denote $\mathfrak{X}^{k}((\S^{d-1})^n)$ the set of vector fields admitting a derivative of order $k$, endowed with the norm 
