    \end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:37:08.956044+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78bcf05e630a729cc344e376704c504ffff0ee24869d83b022c089b87fc2c5b3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:tmin of arXiv:2609.01581, 'Concentration of additive functionals of Stratonovich-type'] Under the assumptions of Theorem~(thm:ci of the paper), we fix a desired accuracy (or tolerance)
$\varepsilon>0$ at a confidence level $1-\alpha$, with $\alpha\in(0,1)$, and define
\begin{align}
  t_{\textrm{min},A}(\varepsilon,\alpha)
  \equiv
  \frac{1}{2\varepsilon^2}
  \left(\sqrt{\tilde\sigma_A^2}+ \sqrt{\tilde\sigma_A^2 + 2 c_A \varepsilon}
  \right)^{2}
  \log\left(\frac{2N_\nu}{\alpha}\right).

\end{align}
Then $r_A(\alpha,t) \leq \varepsilon$ if and only if $t \geq
t_{\textrm{min},A}(\varepsilon,\alpha)$.
In particular, for every $t \geq t_{\textrm{min},A}(\varepsilon,\alpha)$, it holds that
\begin{align}
  \mathbb{P}^\nu\left( -\varepsilon \leq \overline{A}_t - \E^\mu[\overline{A}_t]\leq \varepsilon\right)\geq 1 - \alpha.

\end{align}
Moreover, the minimal time $t_{\textrm{min},A}$ admits the simple two-sided estimate
\begin{align}
  \frac{2\tilde\sigma_A^2}{\varepsilon^2}\log\left(\frac{2N_\nu}{\alpha}\right)
  \leq
  t_{\textrm{min},A}(\varepsilon,\alpha)
  \leq
  \left(\frac{2\tilde\sigma_A^2}{\varepsilon^2}+ \frac{2 c_A}{\varepsilon}\right)
  \log\left(\frac{2N_\nu}{\alpha}\right),

\end{align}
and the corresponding one-sided guarantees of Eq.~(equation eq:ci_onesided of the paper) hold at
tolerance $\varepsilon$ for all $t \geq t_{\textrm{min},A}^{(1)}(\varepsilon,\alpha)$, where
$t_{\textrm{min},A}^{(1)}(\varepsilon,\alpha)$ is obtained
from Eq.~(equation eq:tmin of the paper) by replacing $2N_\nu$ with $N_\nu$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.321920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78bebb788e61a7991101f1e0a9ed85d33ceeecb0fdb36e18705adbafb4faccc4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 38 (Exact constant) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] Let $\sigma, \alpha > 0$. Every $X \in \SE(\sigma^2,\alpha)$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
    X \lecx \left(\sigma \vee \alpha\right)\Lap,
\end{align*}
and no smaller multiple of $\Lap$ dominates every member: $c^{\SE}(\sigma, \alpha) = \sigma \vee \alpha$. An extremal pair for that multiple exists exactly when $\alpha \le \sigma$, and $X \sim \mathrm{Unif}(\{-\sigma,\sigma\})$ with $f(x) = |x|$ is one. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.038152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78cdc01d283d77442860990c2161e49a43dcf1e503d9685c737c5dd08d35893d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 11 (Free Brownian motion) of arXiv:2606.31528, 'Well-posedness and stationary distribution of free stochastic differential equations'] A stochastic process $\{W_t\}_{t\ge 0}$ is called a \emph{free Brownian motion} if it satisfies 
\begin{itemize}
\item $W_0=0;$
\item The increment $W_t-W_s$ is freely independent from $W^*(W_r: 0\leq r\leq s)$ for all $0\leq s <t;$
\item The increment $W_t-W_s$ is a semicircular element with mean $0$ and variance $t-s$ for all $0 \leq s <t.$
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.252422+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_78d32fd47edf7d5c705cf101fea68762fe49faf8384f50ddf401e9f79017e36d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] Let $ x\in X $, $T\in\RR$, 
$n>2$, $\delta\in(0,1]$, $r_2\geq 2r_1\geq 256 S$ and constants $\phi,\Phi\geq 1$.
Assume $S_{\phi}(n,r_1,r_2)$ in $ x$.
 For all non-negative $\Delta_\omega$-subsolutions $v\geq 0$ on $[T-r_2^2,T+r_2^2]\times B_{x}(r_2)$, we have
 \begin{multline*}
