{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:33:12.352993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a57afcea96c197da349cd90d9f01a0de6da1f3e4efa3f4d8cd575599f32e59b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Dfn 199 (truncation) of arXiv:2608.29416, 'Algorithmic threshold for high-dimensional projection pursuit I: general theory'] For the spherical perceptron, let $\vX\equiv\vX^{\RomI:\RomIII}$ be the three-dimensional process defined by \eqref{e:X.decomp} and \eqref{e:vX} above. Given a (large constant) parameter $\trK$, we define a \textbf{truncated} process $\vX^\trstar$ by initializing
$\vX^\trstar(q_0,a)=\vX(q_0,a) = (0,0,0)\in\R^3$, and setting
    \[
    \vX^\trstar(q_{d+1},a)
    =\begin{cases}
    \vX(q_{d+1},a) &\textup{if $\vX^\trstar(q_d,a)\in[-\trK,\trK)^3$,}\\
    \vX^\trstar(q_d,a) &\textup{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
    \]
so that the process $\vX^\trstar$ stays frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)^3$. For the Ising perceptron, we apply the above procedure to obtain $\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII}$. We separately define $X^{\trstar,\Ising}$ to be the process $X^\Ising$ from \eqref{e:Delta.X.Ising}, frozen upon exiting $[-\trK,\trK)$. We then denote $\vX^\trstar\equiv(\vX^{\trstar,\RomI:\RomIII},X^{\trstar,\Ising})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:31:45.374295+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a5c42cea1bdf89a2cea26b16071e035daaf3f462856ea2d415b69abef1b19e7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm:schervish are str proper for estimates of arXiv:2412.06420, 'Accuracy, Estimates, and Representation Results'] {schervish2014dominating}}]
	Suppose $\mathsf{acc}$ has the form:
	\begin{equation}
		\mathsf{acc}_k(x)=\mathsf{acc}_k(k)-\int_x^k(k-t)\;\;\lambda(\dd{t})
	\end{equation}
	where $\lambda$ is finite on every bounded interval and mutually absolutely continuous with Lebesgue integral; then $\mathsf{acc}$ is proper. 

(We will here prove this for finitely supported probabilities, i.e., for any finitely supported probability $p$, $\mathrm{Exp}_p[\mathsf{acc}_V(x)]$ is maximised at $x=\mathrm{Exp}_p[V]$.)

	If $\lambda$ gives positive measure to every non-generate interval, then $\mathsf{acc}$ is \emph{strictly} proper, i.e., this maximum is unique.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.569652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a6088c76c8b0d00d2108ebb917c24d0d593d0b3e3112bfda03a54e5cf264f49","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 19 (Spherical second and fourth moments in a subspace) of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let \(\mathbb F\in\{\mathbb R,\mathbb C\}\), and put
  \[
    \beta_{\mathbb F}:=\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathbb F
    =
    \begin{cases}
      1, & \mathbb F=\mathbb R,\\
      2, & \mathbb F=\mathbb C.
    \end{cases}
  \]
  Equip \(\mathbb F^N\) with the standard Euclidean/Hermitian inner product. Let \(V\subset \mathbb F^N\) be an \(m\)-dimensional \(\mathbb F\)-linear subspace, and let \(P\) be the matrix representing the orthogonal projection onto \(V\). If \(v\) is a random unit vector in \(V\) uniformly distributed on the unit sphere of \(V\), then, with $v^\ast$ understood as $v^{\top}$ if $\mathbb F = \realR$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb E[vv^\ast]=\frac1m P.
  \end{equation}
  Moreover, for any \(N\times N\) matrices \(A\) and \(B\) that are real symmetric if \(\mathbb F = \realR\) and are Hermitian if \(\mathbb F = \compC\),
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb E\bigl[(v^\ast A v)(v^\ast B v)\bigr]
    =
    \frac{
      \tr(PAP)\tr(PBP)+\frac{2}{\beta_{\mathbb F}}\tr(PAPBP)
    }{
      m\bigl(m+\frac{2}{\beta_{\mathbb F}}\bigr)
    }.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.319654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a6d297a1bae37e6302bd5e276e060135d425414a2ac7e202f9cae3954df9ff2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:inv-NWU of arXiv:2411.14926, 'Convex combinations of random variables stochastically dominate the parent for a new class'] A random variable $X$ is super-heavy-tailed if and only if
