{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.451159+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7dd027473924d0b8ed1a71306a88fe8dc5eeede4d0d470b1d8629b5994744754","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:Q_path_expansion of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] Define  a family of  polynomials $\mathcal{Q}_n(x)$ recursively by
\begin{equation} 
\mathcal{Q}_0(x) = 1, \quad
\mathcal{Q}_1(x) = x - 1, \quad
\mathcal{Q}_n(x) = (x - 1 - \alpha)\mathcal{Q}_{n-1}(x) - \alpha \mathcal{Q}_{n-2}(x) \quad (n \ge 2),
\end{equation}
with the convention that $\mathcal{Q}_n(x) = 0$ for $n < 0$. Then for $X = (H+A)(H+A)^*$ and  for each $n \ge 0$, we have
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Q}_n(X)
= \sum_{k \ge 0} \sum_{\alpha \in \{2,3,A,A^*\}^k} \sum_{\substack{\ell_0 + \cdots + \ell_k = 2n}}
V_{\ell_0}\,\underline{\Phi_{\alpha_1} V_{\ell_1}} \cdots \underline{\Phi_{\alpha_k} V_{\ell_k}}.
\end{equation}
Here, each $\alpha_i$ denotes insertion of $\Phi_2$, $\Phi_3$, $\Phi_A$, or $\Phi_{A^*}$, and the indices satisfy $\ell_i \ge 0$ with $\sum \ell_i = 2n$.","status":"unverified"}

