{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.415373+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7e6d2b2bc8084adc3e1c222fb146e560653cb20b059780461daba794ebcb3c78","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 93 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Take two variational data satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A} and \ref{Assumption_B}, differing only by their potentials $V^{\textnormal{I}}_h$ and $V^{\textnormal{II}}_h$ on the $h$-th segment for a single $h \in [H]$. Assume furthermore that $\boldsymbol{\mu}^{\textnormal{I}}$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{Assumption_C}. Then, there exists $M > 0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, such that for any measurable function $f$ with finite $|\!|f|\!|_{\frac{1}{2}}$, we have \begin{equation}

