{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.194101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_815ced3660bd3913939fb692cf90fb3927fe26b25d715c4c638bd6c533ced52b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 19 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Users being relayed by a given building.}

Let $(\alpha, \beta)\in \Half$ be a given point such that $\mathcal{L}([\beta,  +\infty[)>0$, and consider the landscape $\tilde{\landsc} := \landsc\cup \{(\alpha, \beta)\}$. 

Then, the expected length of the zone of eventual relaying of $(\alpha, \beta)$ is equal to 
$$\e[\ell(\zone(\alpha, \beta))] =\left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
        \displaystyle\int_{0}^{\beta} -\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\primi(\beta)}{\primi(u)^2}du & \mbox{if } \beta<S \\
        +\infty & \mbox{if }\beta=S,
    \end{array}
\right.$$
where $S$ denotes the supremum of the support of $\mathcal{L}$. 
In particular, under the Palm measure, the average length of the zone of eventual relaying for a typical building is infinite. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.736161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8178415c524b119f02549f1e8690b02850adbe3673489467e717d49b9b8f6e4c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_wtM of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Define the spectral domain $\mathbf D(\tau):=\{z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta\in \mathbb{C} :|z|\le \tau^{-1}, |\eta| \ge N^{-1+\tau}\}$ for an arbitrarily small constant $\tau>0$.
For $z_1,z_2\in \mathbf D(\tau)$, we define the $D\times D$ matrices $\widehat M$ and $L$ as
\begin{align}
    \widehat M_{ab}(z_1,z_2,\Lambda):=D\langle M(z_1) E_{a} M(z_2) E_{b}\rangle ,\quad L_{ab}(z_1,z_2, H, \Lambda):= D\langle G(z_1)E_{a}G(z_2)E_{b}\rangle , 
\end{align}
for  $a,b\in \llbracket D\rrbracket$, and define the $D\times D$ matrix $K$ as

\begin{align}   K(z_1,z_2, \Lambda ):= \left[1-\widehat M(z_1,z_2,\Lambda)\right]^{-1} \widehat M(z_1,z_2,\Lambda).
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.132288+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_817c7ef6e85d718746e5cbc68537df22446162d0c4a6b25f0a396d27e2dc537c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2608.27500, 'Optimal Transport for Network Comparison: \\ A Review with Machine Learning Applications'] Let $G=(V,E)$ and $G'=(V,E')$ with $|V|=n$ be connected graphs. Then
