{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:01:15.328565+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_86325ffeebe7c74d2fc88eab63cd5f2d480e6ae24d057a3255cc0152ff3874c9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 11 of arXiv:2608.30357, 'Mean convergence for Banach space-valued random elements indexed in measure spaces'] Let $\{K_n : n \ge 1\} \subseteq \cK(\cX)$ and let $p \in [1, \infty)$. Assume that $\{X^n_s : s \in S_n, n \ge 1\}$ is $(\nu_n)$-$\CUI(p)$ w.r.t. $\{K_n\}$. Assume for any $n \ge 1$, there exists $D_n \in \cS_n \otimes \cS_n$ such that:
	\begin{enumerate}[\rm (a)]
		\item  $\lim_{n \to \infty} N(K_n, \ep)^{1 - (\frac{1}{p} \wedge \frac{1}{2})} \lt\|K_n\rt\| ((\nu_n \otimes \nu_n) (D_n))^{\frac{1}{p} \wedge \frac{1}{2}} = 0$ for all $\ep >0$,

		\item  $X^n_s$ and $X^n_t$ are diagonally negatively dependent for $(\nu_n \otimes \nu_n)$-a.e.\ $(s, t) \in (S_n \times S_n) \backslash D_n$.
	\end{enumerate}
	Then, we have 
	\begin{align}
		\lim_{n \to \infty} \bE \lt\| \int_{S_n} \lt(X^n_s - \bE X^n_s\rt) \nu_n(\od s) \rt\|^p = 0,
	\end{align}
	and in particular, $\int_{S_n} \lt(X^n_s - \bE X^n_s\rt) \nu_n(\od s)  \to 0$ in probability as $n \to \infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.597900+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_86380316a78eff60aedff66ceb9fb2965cef877bf0024aeaec449fa1fe15f430","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop P:weighted-quadrangle of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Consider a weighted directed acyclic graph $G = (V, E, M)$, and let $H = (V, E, M')$ be another one with the same vertices and edges, but different weights satisfying $M' \le M$, with equality on a set $W \subset V$. Consider vertices $x_1 \prec y_1$ and $x_2 \prec y_2$ and suppose that we have the following {\bf path-crossing} property:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Let $\tau$ be any path from $x_1$ to $y_1$ and $\pi = (\pi_0, \dots, \pi_k)$ be any path from $x_2$ to $y_2$. Then if $\pi_i \notin W$ for some $i \in \llbracket1, k-1 \rrbracket$, there are indices $i^- < i < i^+$ such that the vertices $\pi(i^-), \pi(i^+)$ are on the path $\tau$.
\end{itemize}
Then
$$
H(x_1; y_1) + G(x_2 ; y_2)  \le G(x_1; y_1) + H(x_2 ; y_2).
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.736550+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_863aa782cc896abb0ffca77d412f2e533d5c48c876fb9e612866a552409c0528","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition flow of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Given a starting time $t_0\in \mathbb{R}$ and initial values $(z_{t_0},\Lambda_{t_0})$, we define flows of $z$ and $\Lambda$ as 
\begin{equation} \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t}z_t=-\frac12 z_t- \langle M_t\rangle,\quad   \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t}\Lambda_t=-\frac12 \Lambda_t,\quad t\ge t_0, 
\end{equation}
where $M_t:= M(z_t, \Lambda_t)$ is the solution to (equation def_M of the paper) with $z$ and $\Lambda$ replaced by $z_t$ and $\Lambda_t$. Let $t_c:=\inf\{t\ge t_0: \im z_{t_c}=0\}$ be the first time $\im z_t$ vanishes. We also introduce the function $Z:\mathbb{C}\times \mathbb{C}^{DN\times DN}\to\mathbb{C}^{DN\times DN} $ as $Z(z ,\Lambda ):=z I - \Lambda$ and abbreviate that $Z_t:=Z(z_t , \Lambda_t )$. Note that $Z_t$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} t}Z_t=-\frac12 Z_t- m_t,\quad \text{where}\quad m_t:=\langle M_t\rangle .
\end{equation}

Given the initial random matrix $H_{t_0}$ satisfying (main_assm of the paper) with diagonal blocks $(H_a)_{t_0}$, $a\in [\![{D}]\!]$, we define the flow $H_t$ as a $DN\times DN$ random matrix with diagonal blocks $(H_a)_{t}$ being matrix-valued OU processes 
\begin{equation} \mathrm{d} (H_a)_t = -\frac12(H_a)_t\mathrm{d} t + \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\mathrm{d} (B_a)_t,\end{equation}
where $(B_a)_t$, $a\in [\![{D}]\!]$, are independent complex Hermitian matrix Brownian motions (i.e., $\sqrt{2} \re (B_a)_{ij}$ and $ \sqrt{2} \im (B_a)_{i j}$, $i<j$, and $(B_a)_{i i}$ are independent standard Brownian motions and $(B_a)_{j i}=(\overline{B}_a)_{i j}$). In particular, for each $t\ge t_0$, $(H_a)_t$ has the same law as  
\begin{equation}
e^{-(t-t_0)/2}\cdot  H_a^{(0)}+ \sqrt{1-e^{-(t-t_0)}}\cdot H_a^{(g)},
\end{equation}
where $H_a^{(g)}$, $a\in [\![{D}]\!]$, are i.i.d.~GUE. Then, we define the Green's function flow 
$G_t=\left(H_t+\Lambda_t-z_t\right)^{-1}.$
Finally, with $(z_{i})_t$, $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, $\Lambda_t$, $H_t$, and $M_t$, we can define 
$$ \widehat M_{(1,2),t}=\widehat M((z_{1})_t,(z_{2})_t,\Lambda_t),\quad L_{(1,2),t}=L((z_{1})_t,(z_{2})_t,H_t, \Lambda_t),\quad K_{(1,2),t}=K((z_{1})_t,(z_{2})_t,\Lambda_t)$$ 
as in (def_wtM of the paper), and define
$$L_{(1,2,3),t}=L((z_{1})_t, (z_{2})_t, (z_{3})_t, H_t, \Lambda_t),\quad K_{(1,2,3),t}=K((z_{1})_t, (z_{2})_t, (z_{3})_t, \Lambda_t)$$ 
as in (defLK3 of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:38:57.142964+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_863eb37c1e5e87ba3d5eccb338cb2846b4c99a864e5703cf947cee270f1029b5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 18 of arXiv:2604.05910, '\textbf{Well-posedness and Hurst parameter estimation for fluid equations driven by fracti'] {\em(Young integral for transport-type noise)}
        Let $Y\in C([0,T];\cB_{\alpha-1/2})\cap C^\gamma([0,T];\cB_{\alpha-\gamma-1/2})$.\\Then 
     there exists a map $\mathcal{I}: C([0,T];\cB_{\alpha-1/2})\cap C^\gamma([0,T];\cB_{\alpha-\gamma-1/2})  \to C([0,T];\cB_\alpha)\cap C^\gamma([0,T];\cB_{\alpha-\gamma}) $ such that $\mathcal{I}_0=0$, 
   \begin{align*}
\mathcal{I}_t =\int_0^t S_{t -s}Y_s dW^H_s       =\lim\limits_{\pi\in\cP([0,t]),|\pi|\to 0} \sum\limits_{[u,v]\in\pi} S_{t-u} Y_u W^H_{u,v}, 
   \end{align*}
which satisfies the following estimates for all $0\leq s \leq t \leq T$:
\begin{align}
    \Big\| \int_s^t S_{t-r}Y_r~d W^H_r -S_{t-s}Y_s W^H_{s,t} \Big\|_{\alpha-\gamma}\lesssim [W^H]_\gamma \max\{ \|Y\|_{0,\alpha-1/2}, \|Y\|_{\gamma,\alpha-1/2-\gamma}\} (t-s)^{2\gamma-1/2}
