{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.610359+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89f828b2305af8b6cbe6399275e14ad5b72fdf51977df0b04f42fa74f3b984a7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2608.19065, 'Boundedly exchangeable graphs'] An adjacency measure on $S$ is a simple measure $\xi$ on $S^2\times\{0,1\}$ such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item there is a simple measure $\nu$ on $S$ with $\xi(C\times\{0,1\}) = \nu^{\otimes 2}(C)$ for every $C \in \Bcal(S)^{\otimes 2}$,
    \item $(\theta \otimes \mathrm{id})\,\xi = \xi$, where $\theta(x,y)=(y,x)$ and $\mathrm{id}$ denotes the identity function on $\{0,1\}$.
\end{enumerate}
For $B\in\mathcal{B}(S)$, the $B$-restriction of $\xi$ is the adjacency measure $\xi\vert_{B \times B \times \{0,1\}}$, which we denote by $\xi\vert_B$ for brevity.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.598111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_89fa07b46283529bf43cf414afcab6e417430ced3600aa16041c7446ebb7701b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main_CPMRE of arXiv:2606.10257, 'Downward conditional monotonicity gives survival and extinction for contact processes in r'] Consider the CPMRE described above with increasing infection rate vector $\beta=(\beta_0,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_{k-1})$, decreasing recovery rate vector $\mu=(\mu_0,\mu_1,\dots,\mu_{k-1})$, 
