{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.898895+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8a7e31e6dbfa570511c342ae28ce73e11155d4c6efcd4fce5e1a4047c606634f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop maximum-inequality of arXiv:2411.06861, 'Quenched invariance principle for random walks in random environments admitting a cycle de'] Fix $\omega \in \Omega$. Let $u: \mathbb{Z}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be either non-negative $L^\omega$-subharmonic $(-L^\omega u \leq 0)$,  or $L^\omega$-harmonic $(L^\omega u = 0)$ on $B(x, n)$, for some $x\in \mathbb{Z}^d$. Then for $p, q\in (1, \infty]$ with $1/p+1/q <2/d$, there exist constants $\kappa = \kappa(d, p, q) \in (1/2, \infty)$ and $C_{\mathrm{Max}}= C_{\mathrm{Max}}(d, p, q)$ such that for all $1/2 \leq \sigma' < \sigma \leq 1$,
