{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.504752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8bd94b54b7e2b7be52df5e2f94fc93ea9b48895e1af7397081f25d273c76bcc6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Hilbert module {\cite[Chapter~1) of arXiv:2608.30589, 'Vector-Valued Wavelet Bases as Hilbert $\mathbb{M}_m(\mathbb{R})$-Module Bases: A Construc'] {lance1995hilbert}}]
Let $\mathcal{A}$ be a (real) $C^*$-algebra.
A \emph{pre-Hilbert $\mathcal{A}$-module} is a left $\mathcal{A}$-module $\mathcal{H}$ equipped with an $\mathcal{A}$-valued inner product $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}:\mathcal{H}\times\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{A}$ such that for all $x,y\in\mathcal{H}$ and $A\in\mathcal{A}$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]

  \item $\langle x,x\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}\ge 0$;
  \item $\langle x,x\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}=0$ implies $x=0$;
  \item $\langle x,y\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}=\langle y,x\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}^{*}$;
  \item $\langle Ax,y\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}=A\,\langle x,y\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}$.

\end{enumerate}
The induced norm is $\|x\|_{\mathcal{A}}:=\|\langle x,x\rangle_{\mathcal{A}}\|_{\mathcal{A}}^{1/2}$.
If $\mathcal{H}$ is complete for this norm, it is called a \emph{Hilbert $\mathcal{A}$-module}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.803470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8be1f761df94f17f1d0bca7dff9dbfd1abfe519c9c0f79e95f37cee68f4d9d20","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 5 Hodge n point formula of arXiv:2609.02409, 'Membranes and Maps'] Suppose that $\sum_{i=1}^5 \epsilon_i = 0$. (conj:GW:vertex of the paper) holds if and only if, for all $n\in\{1,\ldots,5\}$, we have
    \begin{equation}

        \mathsf{H}(\epsilon_1^{-1},\ldots, \epsilon_n^{-1} ; \epsilon_1,\ldots, \epsilon_5 ; u)
        =
        \mathsf{V}(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n;u)
        \prod_{j=1}^n \frac{1}{u^2}
        \prod_{i\neq j} \frac{\gamfct{-\epsilon_j^{-1}\epsilon_i}{u\epsilon_j}}{\epsilon_i} \,,
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}

        \mathsf{V}(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n;u)
        \coloneqq
        \begin{cases}
            1
            & n=1 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \dfrac{\varsigma(u \epsilon_3)\varsigma(u \epsilon_4)\varsigma(u \epsilon_5)}{\varsigma(u (\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2))}
            & n=2 \,, \\[0.95em]
            \varsigma(u \epsilon_4)^2 \varsigma(u \epsilon_5)^2
            & n=3 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \Big(\prod_{i=1}^5 \varsigma(u\epsilon_i) \Big) \, \varsigma(u\epsilon_5)^2 \sum_{i=1}^4 \kappa(u\epsilon_i)
            & n=4 \,, \\[0.75em]
            \Big(\prod_{i=1}^5 \varsigma(u \epsilon_i) \Big)^2 \Big(\big(\sum_{i=1}^5 \kappa(u \epsilon_i)\big)^2 -\frac{1}{4} \Big)
            & n=5 \,,
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    and $\varsigma(x) = 2 \sinh \tfrac{x}{2}$, $\kappa(x) = \frac{d}{dx} \log{\varsigma(x)} = \tfrac{1}{2} \coth \tfrac{x}{2}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

