{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:26:02.364624+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_900b5f75c36b1f7e58599bda48346697af8ec033ecdaaf678440ad18ad2f7d87","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:adapted of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Let $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ be a cooriented $C^0$-foliation on a manifold with fences and windows $M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$. We say that $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ is \textbf{adapted} to the boundary of $M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ if it is transverse to $\partial M_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$, and there exist pairwise disjoint standard neighborhoods and coordinates of the fences and windows such that
    \begin{itemize}
        \item In $N(\mathcal{A}_i)$, $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ is tangent to $\partial_y$, positively transverse to $\partial_z$ (i.e., horizontal), and it is
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Positively transverse to $\partial_x$ in $S^1 \times I \times [1,2)$
            \item Negatively transverse to $\partial_x$ in $S^1 \times I \times (-1,0]$,
        \end{itemize}

        \item If $\mathcal{W}_j$ is a square window, then in $N(\mathcal{W}_j)$, $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ is the standard horizontal foliation by $(x,y)$-planes. In particular, it is smooth there. If $\mathcal{W}_j$ is a toroidal window, we only require that $\mathcal{F}_{\mathord{\scalebox{0.45}{$\square$}}}$ is tangent to $\partial_y$ in $N(\mathcal{W}_j)$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.726664+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9012331254797dc9ed72632716c118ad7c36d365fdfe183e7c5e38d169fd2611","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor polynomial-log of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] Let \(P\in\mathbb R[x]\) have degree \(d\ge2\).  There exists \(A_0=A_0(P)>0\) such that, if for all sufficiently large \(n\),
