{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.767317+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9078e31ec731a4262ee0ffc26989c92395afb94e9dc92feea937c339e8d4abcc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 of arXiv:2603.24616, 'Pinning in non-critical half-space geometric last passage percolation'] Fix $k \in \mathbb{N}$, $\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \weyl_k$, $a,b \in \mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and two continuous functions $f: [a,b] \rightarrow (-\infty, \infty]$ and $g: [a,b] \rightarrow [-\infty,\infty)$. This means that either $f \in C([a,b])$ or $f \equiv \infty$, and similarly for $g$. In addition, we assume that $f(t) > g(t)$ for $t \in[a,b]$, $f(a) > x_1$, $f(b) > y_1$, $g(a) < x_k$, $g(b) < y_k$. With this data we let $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ denote the law of $k$ independent Brownian bridges $\{B_i\}_{i =1}^k$ with law $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{free}}^{a,b,\vec{x}, \vec{y}}$, conditioned on the event 
\begin{equation}
E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}} = \{f(t) > B_1(t) > B_2(t) > \cdots > B_k(t) > g(t) \mbox{ for all } t\in [a,b]\}.
\end{equation}
As explained in \cite[Definition 2.7]{DEA21}, we have that $E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}}$ is measurable and $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{free}}^{a,b,\vec{x}, \vec{y}}(E^{f,g}_{\mathrm{avoid}}) > 0$, so that the law $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ on $C(\llbracket 1, k \rrbracket \times [a,b])$ is well-defined. The expectation with respect to $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$ is denoted by $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}, f,g}$. When $f = \infty$ and $g = -\infty$, we drop them from the notation and simply write $\mathbb{P}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}}$ and $\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{avoid}}^{a,b,\vec{x},\vec{y}}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.413557+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_907a831c22ca32d7293b07628c3aaf51b56599b8b789a4a6e309b153ef8ed3c1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] For $p\in (1,\infty)$, the immersed curve $\gamma \in W^{2,p}(0,1;\R^n)$ is called a \textit{$p$-elastica} if there exists $\lambda \in \R$ such that 
