{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.431523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93642b5d97d980d8b28985e3b2755644e2ce811f1b587f06035c052d67bf573d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: weighted mvt of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] Let $f: [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function and let $g: [a, b] \to \mathbb{R}$ be an integrable function that does not change sign on the interval $[a, b]$. 
Then there exists at least one point $c \in (a, b)$ such that
