{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.875880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_939f99ae081c4824837a0e85ae61389caf796e711ef5c4eeff89e58d0b635542","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2608.23462, '2608.23462'] Let $\beta>0$ and assume that $(\hat Y^x_t)_{t \ge 0}$ is $2$-dissipative with constant $\beta$ and $f$ is $L_f$-Lipschitz. Under Assumption~\ref{assu:regularity}, $\Phi$ is differentiable in $x$, its derivative is jointly continuous on $\R^{d_1+d_2}$, and for all $\alpha>0$, there exists a constant $C_{\alpha,\beta} >0$ satisfying
