{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:12:20.559720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93c354a8bab3ec5deb33a1c0a1ae0b6cbab01c44aa3457a193f92ed3f439df46","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary of arXiv:2604.04882, 'On a Problem of M. Kac on Laplace Distributions'] For every $n\in\mathbb N$, $\lambda>0$, and $\theta\in(0,1/2)$, the functions
    \begin{align}
        F_1(z)\coloneqq \left(\frac12+\frac{1}{2\bigl(1+\frac{\theta\lambda}{n}(1-z)\bigr)}\right)^n,
        \qquad
        F_2(z)\coloneqq \left(
        \frac{2+\frac{\theta\lambda}{n}(1-z)}
        {2+\frac{2\lambda}{n}(1-z)+\frac{\theta\lambda^2}{n^2}(1-z)^2}
        \right)^n
    \end{align}
    are pgfs satisfying
    \begin{align*}
        \Phi_n(F_1(z),F_2(z))
        =
        \left(1+\frac{\lambda}{n}(1-z)\right)^{-n},
        \qquad
        \Phi_n(x,y)\coloneqq \bigl(x^{-1/n}+y^{-1/n}-1\bigr)^{-n},
    \end{align*}
    but neither factor belongs to the negative binomial family $\left\{\left(1+\frac{\mu}{n}(1-z)\right)^{-n}:\mu>0\right\}.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.898055+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93dacf252d0bdc38291b482e02e06f186b93c56232b0c7a78fcff51b6546bb15","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theoremi 4 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Suppose that $N\rightarrow\infty$ with $\theta>0$ and $\kappa>0$ fixed in the discrete ensemble with Gaussian weight \eqref{eq_discrete_model_Gauss}. Let $f(x)$ be an analytic function and consider the random variable
\begin{equation}

 \sum_{i = 1}^{N} f(\ell_i) - N\int f(x)\dd\mu(x).
\end{equation}
If $\sqrt{2\theta\kappa} < \pi$, then \eqref{eq_linear_Gauss_intro} converges in distribution to a Gaussian limit with explicit mean and covariance given in Proposition~\ref{CLTGAUSSIANWEIGHT}. If $\sqrt{2\theta\kappa} > \pi$, then \eqref{eq_linear_Gauss_intro} converges as $N\rightarrow\infty$ towards a sum of two independent random variables: the first one is a deterministic affine function of a discrete Gaussian random variable; the second one is Gaussian with explicit covariance given in Proposition~\ref{CLTGAUSSIANWEIGHT2}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.288474+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93ed36d88e4d39e4d7f5109c4140dbd33b9c62cffeadd02c8892320ddef1e238","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 of arXiv:2606.23099, 'Path-dependent Affine Processes'] An $\R^d$-valued stochastic process $X$ 
    is called a $d$-dimensional \emph{path-dependent process}, if it is a continuous semimartingale and there exist continuous $b : \ccC_T^d \to \R^d$ and measurable $\sigma : \ccC_T^d \to \R^{d \times m}$, such that $a(t,x) := \sigma(t,x) \sigma(t, x)^\top$ is continuous and $X$ satisfies
    \begin{align}
        X(t) & = X(0) + \int_{0}^{t} b(s,X_{[0,s]}) \, ds + \int_{0}^{t} \sigma(s,X_{[0,s]}) \, dW(s), \quad 0 \leq t \leq T,
    \end{align}
    with deterministic initial value $X(0) \in \R^d$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.094367+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_93fe484b374c12d0701783c6e1e006b2804b0eba0ab6e90e359905b42b95e61f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 36 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $\mathfrak{v}\in \Gamma(A_U)$ be a local section, and let $\LXu[v]$ be its complete lift. 
Then, if we denote by $\LB[\mathfrak{v},\mathfrak{u}]^V_A$ the vertical lift of  $\LB[\mathfrak{v},\mathfrak{u}]_A$, 
\begin{align} 
\LXu[v](\mathfrak{u}(x)) = T_x\mathfrak{u}(\rho(\mathfrak{v}(x))) + (\LB[\mathfrak{u},\mathfrak{v}]_A(x))^V_{\mathfrak{u}(x)}. 
\end{align}
Let $c$ be admissible, $\gamma=\pi\circ c$, and let $b$ be a section along $\gamma$. If $\mathfrak{v}_t$ satisfies $\mathfrak{v}_t(\gamma(t))=b(t)$, then 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{T}_c(b)(t) = \mathcal{X}_{\mathfrak{v}_t}(c(t)) + \left(\frac{\partial \mathfrak{v}_t}{\partial t}(\gamma(t))\right)^V_{c(t)}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.317186+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_94093a6dba85899bb8ff74b010a09749df816691edc9762ab4784666b742da06","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:vthd of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] {bass1992lifetimes}}]
