{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.181201+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0c134691054c08d539ed86d1e01674e3b419f46fc3836e4bc9e09211215f5b9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 116 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mf A$ be a regular HTM, $I$ and $J$ be sets, and $K = I\times  J$. Let $\sm{\mf A}{k\in K}{f(k)}$ and $x = \sum_{k\in K} f(k)$.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item [(i)] Let $\sm{\mf A}{j\in  J}{f(i,j)}$ for every $i\in I$. Then $x = \sum_{i\in I}\sum_{j\in J}f(i,j)$.
  \item [(ii)] Let $\sm{\mf A}{i\in  I}{f(i,j)}$ for every $j\in J$. Then $x = \sum_{j\in J}\sum_{i\in I}f(i,j)$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

