{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.433664+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a35a6d0dddd8f2c3ebd13e0afb919ab4550b4a667cc06529d0df0f1260c7ba41","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Define Definition 4.1 of arXiv:2601.09990, 'Remarks on the convex integration technique applied to singular stochastic partial differe'] Given initial data $u^{\text{in}} \in L^{n} (\mathbb{T}^{d})$, a scalar field $u \in L^{n} (0, \infty; L^{n} (\mathbb{T}^{d}))$ is a weak solution to (equation odd power of the paper) if 
\begin{align}
&\int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(t,x) \psi(t,x) dx - \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u^{\text{in}} (x) \psi(0,x) dx - \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(s,x) \partial_{s} \psi(s,x) dx ds  \nonumber \\
& \hspace{30mm} + \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u^{n}(s,x) \psi(s,x) dx ds = \int_{0}^{t} \int_{\mathbb{T}^{d}} u(s,x) \Delta \psi(s,x) dx ds 
