{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.121954+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a631b4de5ca00c61182beb6e205b019ccb0277644e1952555176cddc652c71c3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:offdiag-chaos of arXiv:2608.18293, 'Critical tensor covariance at the Marchenko--Pastur threshold'] Put $\mu_3=\mathbb{E} X^3$ and
\[
 \tau^2=v-\mu_3^2\ge0.
\]
If $\tau>0$, define
\[
 h_1(X)=X,
 \qquad
 h_2(X)=\frac{X^2-1-\mu_3X}{\tau};
\]
when $\tau=0$, omit $h_2$ and restrict all sums below to
$S_2=\varnothing$ (equivalently, $T_2=\varnothing$); throughout the lemma and
its later use, terms involving $S_2$ are interpreted only under this
restriction. For disjoint $S_1,S_2\subseteq[n]$, set
\[
 H_{S_1,S_2}
 =\prod_{i\in S_1}h_1(X_i)\prod_{i\in S_2}h_2(X_i).
\]
These random variables form an orthonormal family.

Let $A$ be complex symmetric with zero diagonal and
$Q=x^{\mathsf T}Ax$. For every nonempty even set $D$, write
$|D|=2s$. For $C\cap D=\varnothing$, $|C|=d-s$, define
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_{D,C}
 =\sum_{\substack{U\subset D\\|U|=s}}
 A_{C\cup U,\,C\cup(D\setminus U)},

\end{equation}
and, for $W\cap D=\varnothing$, define
\begin{equation}
 \gamma_D(W)
 =\sum_{\substack{|C|=d-s,\ C\cap D=\varnothing\\mathbb{C}\supseteq W}}
 \alpha_{D,C}.

\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 Q=\sum_{S_1\cap S_2=\varnothing}
 c_{S_1,S_2}H_{S_1,S_2},

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 c_{S_1,S_2}
 =\sum_{\substack{\varnothing\ne D\subseteq S_1,\ |D|\ {\rm even}\\
                   D\cap S_2=\varnothing}}
 \mu_3^{|S_1\setminus D|}\tau^{|S_2|}
 \gamma_D\bigl((S_1\setminus D)\cup S_2\bigr).

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.054381+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a63bb4a30703b3e4384ec14e152a5d4c5b1ac7012f1513857682984c23bba52f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma A_m_izraz of arXiv:2607.13302, 'Extending an Order-Statistic Characterization of the Exponential Distribution'] Let $F$ be a distribution function that belong to the class $\mathcal{F}$. Denote $A_m(x) = F^{m}(x)f(x)$. If the condition (equation uslov_q of the paper) is satisfied for all $0 \leq q \leq r-m$, $r > m$, then
\begin{equation}
A_m^{(r)}(0) = (-1)^{r-m}f^{r+1}(0)\genfrac{\{}{\}}{0pt}{}{r+1}{m+1}m!,
\end{equation}
where $\genfrac{\{}{\}}{0pt}{}{a}{b}$ denotes the Stirling number of the second kind, i.e. number of ways to partition a set of $a$ elements into $b$ non-empty subsets. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.440823+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a641ebcd1f9a0f915494d5f0116648d32b03f3ae32e66b2afe66d8279ff20e98","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 35 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $(\mu_n)_{n\in \N}$ be a sequence of random probability measures and let $\mu\in \mathscr{P}(\C)$ be deterministic. Suppose that:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item there exists a compact set $D$ such that $\mathrm{supp}(\mu)\subset D$ and
        \begin{equation*}
            \mathbb{P}\Bigl(\mathrm{supp}\pr{\mu_n}\subset D\Bigl)=1-o(1)\m\m\m\text{when}\m\m\m\m n\to +\infty,
        \end{equation*}
        \item for almost every $z\in \C$, $\phi_{\mu_n}(z)$ converges in probability to $\phi_{\mu}(z)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, $\mu_n$ converges weakly in probability to $\mu$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.310853+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6497e633306509f1e0c6bb5e1a7a3d8862cfdf502a7984e4b677611c020418c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.05931, 'Effective Lagrangian regularity and the uniqueness threshold for random H\"older velocity '] Let $\alpha \in (1/2,1)$. Suppose $U:\T^d \to\R^d$ is a $C^{\alpha-}(\T^d)$ multiscale finite range random velocity field; that is, $U$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{asmp:main for autonomous}, and \eqref{eq:main theorem hypothesis} holds for some $p > \frac{d}{\alpha-1/2}$.

    Then almost surely, the ODE~\eqref{eq:main ODE autonomous data} admits a unique Lipschitz flow away from the random zero level set of $U$.
    More precisely, for all $\ep>0$, there exists an almost surely finite random constant $M_\ep>0$ such that for any $\tau \in [0,1]$ and any two integral curves of $U$ avoiding $Z_\ep$ on $[0,\tau]$---$X^i: [0,\tau] \to \T^d\backslash Z_\ep$ with $\dot X^i = U(X^i)$---we have the bi-Lipschitz stability estimate
    \begin{equation}

    M_\ep^{-1} d_{\T^d}(X^1_0, X^2_0) \leq d_{\T^d}(X^1_t ,X^2_t) \leq M_\ep d_{\T^d}(X^1_0, X^2_0)\qquad \mbox{for all $t \in [0,\tau]$}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular, the solution to~\eqref{eq:main ODE autonomous data} is uniquely determined up until hitting $Z$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.795718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a653a491bcbaa7b5cfea613decc88627c33135c9a0b9b5d3ab063895834168ec","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.17509, '2608.17509'] Let \(a>1\). The limiting log-moment generating function \(\Lambda_a\) belongs to \(C^\infty(\mathbb{R})\), with
\[
\Lambda_a'(\theta)
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_0^{\infty}
\frac{e^{\theta}r_a(\xi)}
{1+(e^{\theta}-1)r_a(\xi)}
\,\mathrm{d}\xi,
\]
\[
\Lambda_a''(\theta)
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_0^{\infty}
\frac{e^{\theta}r_a(\xi)(1-r_a(\xi))}
{\left(1+(e^{\theta}-1)r_a(\xi)\right)^2}
\,\mathrm{d}\xi .
\]
Moreover, \(\Lambda_a\) is strictly increasing and strictly convex on \(\mathbb{R}\), and
\begin{equation}

\lim_{\theta\to-\infty}\Lambda_a'(\theta)=0,
\qquad
\lim_{\theta\to+\infty}\Lambda_a'(\theta)=+\infty.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the Legendre--Fenchel transform 
\(
\widetilde{J}_a(x):=\sup_{\theta\in\mathbb{R}}\{\theta x-\Lambda_a(\theta)\}
\)
is finite precisely on \([0,\infty)\), and \(J_a:=\widetilde{J}_a|_{[0,\infty)}\) is a good rate function on \([0,\infty)\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344579+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a65bba3aa74721b7a7e8ed277582dbd3df5346ec18364c844098e755c1ae6227","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqSimplefuction of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Let $ f: \Omega \rightarrow \mathcal{X} $ be an $ \mathcal{X} $-valued function.
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item The function $ f(\cdot ) $ is called an $ \mathcal{F} $-simple  function (or simple function when $ \mathcal{F} $ is clear) if 
        \begin{align}

            f (\cdot) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} \chi_{E_{j}}(\cdot) u_{j},
        \end{align}
        for some $ k \in \mathbb{N} $, $ u_{j} \in \mathcal{X} $, and $ \{E_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k} \subset \mathcal{F} $.
        Here, $ \chi_{E}(\cdot) $ is the characteristic function of $ E $.

        \item The function $ f(\cdot ) $ is said to be strongly $ \mathcal{F} $-measurable with respect to $ \mu $ (or simplify strongly measurable) if there exist simple functions $ \{ f_{k} \}_{k = 1}^{\infty} $ converging to $ f $ in $ \mathcal{X} $, $ \mu $-a.e.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:00:22.782379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a66fddfc405eb6c7bdf696048d215e249e85a964fc10321f3e73863a116f9bcd","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lm G almost always tilde G of arXiv:2411.01633, 'On the Limit of the Tridiagonal Model \\for $\beta$-Dyson Brownian Motion'] We have
