{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.187510+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6e663fc1dc17cd791710853ddbdd2ec3eeab89febb6966212377189edd30cd5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Ising-on-tree of arXiv:2603.01322, 'Gibbs conditioning, atypical consensus and splitting Gibbs measures on random regular grap'] Given $\k \in \mathbb N, \k \geq 2,$ $0\neq \beta\in \R$, $B\in \R$, and $\Gamma = \Gamma (\k, \beta, B)$. In the ferromagnetic regime $\beta>0$, we let
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item[(1a)] 
         $\ising_\k^*(\beta,B)$ denote the  Ising measure on $T^\k$ corresponding to the fixed point $\exth^*$ when $(\beta, B)$ lie in the uniqueness regime (equation regime:unique of the paper); 
          \item[(1b)] $\ising_{\k}^{\sharp}(\beta,B)$ denote the 
        Ising measure 
        on $T^\k$ corresponding to the fixed point $ \exth^\sharp$ of $ \Gamma$ 
        when 
        $\beta > \betacrit$; 
        \item[(1c)] $\ising_\k^+(\beta,B)$ as the  Ising measure on $T^\k$  corresponding to the fixed point $\exth^+$ of $\Gamma$ when $B > 0$ (positive external field) or $\beta>\beta_*$ and $B= 0$ (zero external field in the non-uniqueness regime) or $\beta\geq \beta_*$ and $B<0$ (negative external field in the non-uniqueness regime); 
\item[(1d)] 
$\ising_\k^-(\beta,B)$ 
        as the Ising measure on $T^\k$ corresponding to the fixed point $\exth^-$ of $\Gamma$ when $B < 0$ (negative external field) or $\beta>\beta_*$ and $B= 0$ (zero external field in the non-uniqueness regime) or $\beta\geq \beta_*$ and $B>0$ (positive external field in the non-uniqueness regime);
        \end{enumerate}
        In the antiferromagnetic regime $\beta<0$, we let 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item[(2)] $\ising_\k^\sharp(\beta,B)$ denote the Ising measure on $T^\k$ corresponding to the unique fixed point $\exth^\sharp$.
        \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:53:30.673899+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ea73d7aa8a48da59e3a5509a6951135fff577f7688524150d829fe8a7fb179","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor eq:magique of arXiv:2506.01746, '\bf Optimal Bregman quantization: existence and uniqueness of optimal quantizers revisited'] If $x\!\in U^n$ is a stationary quantizer at level $n\ge 1$  (i.e. has pairwise distinct components with $P$-negligible Bregman--Voronoi boundary and  satisfies the $\phi_{_F}$-master equation~(equation eq:Mastereq of the paper)), then all induced quantizations $\widehat X$ are $\mathbb{P}$-$a.s.$ equal and
\begin{equation}
e_{2}(x, P,\phi_{_F})^2 = \mathbb{E} \, F(X)-\mathbb{E}\, F\big(\widehat X^{x}\big).
\end{equation}
In particular, if $x^{(n)}$ is an optimal $F$-Bregman quantizer then
 \begin{equation}
e_{2,n}(P,\phi_{_F})^2 = \mathbb{E} \, F(X)-\mathbb{E}\, F\big(\widehat X^{x^{(n)}}\big).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.354974+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ed5607af312edf7ca4160efab993e19f43d375d887fbbfc5250e6fef2849da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2511.09280, 'Random Walk conditioned to stay above a non-flat floor: curvature effects'] Let \(\epsilon>0\). With the notations above, for any \(n\geq 1\), and non-negative measurable function \(f:\R^{n+1}\to \R_+\),
    \begin{multline}
        E\bigl(f(S) \mathds{1}_{S\geq h_n}\mathds{1}_{S_n\leq h_n(n)+\epsilon}\bigr)
        \\
        =
        e^{-n\int_{0}^1 I(h'(s))ds} E\Bigl(f(Z+h_n)\mathds{1}_{Z\geq 0}\mathds{1}_{Z_n\leq \epsilon}\exp\Bigl(-\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \tfrac{\alpha_k}{n} Z_k\Bigr) \Bigr)e^{O_n(1)},
    \end{multline}
    with \(O_n(1)\) uniform over \(f\), and
    where
    \begin{equation}
        \alpha_k \equiv \alpha_{n,k} = -\frac{h''(k/n)}{H''((H')^{-1}(h'(k/n)))} + b_{n,k},
    \end{equation}
    and the \(b_k\equiv b_{n,k}\)'s are such that \(\sup_{k} \abs{b_{k}} = o_n(1)\). Moreover, the \(b_{n,k}\)'s are independent of \(\epsilon\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.707070+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a6ef959d0b58ba8a9bfd0b87c4422ab1d27ec98195d3bfa93942e69b772b6467","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem hitting of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] There exist $C,\delta > 0$ such that, given $x \in {\rm D}_n$ and $R > {\rm d}(x,{\rm D}_n^c)$, 
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{P}_x(\tau_{\partial {\rm B}(x;\log R)} < \tau_\partial) \leq C \left(\frac{{\rm d}(x,{\rm D}_n^c)}{R}\right)^\delta.
\end{equation} In particular, given any $\varepsilon > 0$ and $x,y \in {\rm D}$ with $\| x-y\| \geq \varepsilon$, ${\rm d}(y,{\rm D}^c)>2\varepsilon$ and $\lfloor {\rm e}^n x\rfloor, \lfloor {\rm e}^n y\rfloor \in {\rm D}_n$, 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_{\lfloor {\rm e}^n x\rfloor}(\tau_{\lfloor {\rm e}^n y\rfloor} < \tau_\partial) \leq 	C_{\varepsilon,{\rm D}} \frac{\left({\rm d}(x,{\rm D}^c)\right)^\delta }{n}
\end{equation} for some constant $C_{\varepsilon,{\rm D}} > 0$ and all $n$ large enough (depending only on $\varepsilon$ and ${\rm D}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a7008846103ea50cfb4c9d7201c09238326228f44a8217691b771605bf23f000","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p_{i})$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent centered $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables.
       Let $a=(a_{1},\cdots, a_{n})$ be a  fixed vector. Then for $0<\alpha\le 1$ and $t\ge 0$
       \begin{align}
       	\mathbb{P}\Big\{\big\vert \sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\delta_{i}\zeta_{i}\big\vert\ge t   \Big\}\le 2\exp\Big(-c(\alpha)\min\Big\{\frac{t^{2}}{L(\alpha)^{2}(\sum_{i}a_{i}^{2}p_{i})}, \Big(\frac{t}{L(\alpha)\Vert a\Vert_{\infty}}\Big)^{\alpha}  \Big\}   \Big),\nonumber
       \end{align}
   where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_{i}\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)$ is a positive constant depending only on $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.780742+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a703ae1a30a2d501937f59b7a0ee85b6408bffc3c61c5e96728594d8a9dfa056","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 5 of arXiv:2603.29549, 'Multitype PCR branching processes'] The function $\bar F$ has a continuous inverse on $\Real_+^d$. The subset 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma = \big\{\bar x\in \Real_+^d: x_{d_0+1}=...=x_d=0\big\}
\end{equation}
is both backward and forward invariant with respect to $\bar F$. The restriction of the inverse to $\Gamma$ admits the formula:
\begin{equation}
\bar F^{-1}(\bar y) = \frac {f^{-1}(y)}{y}\bar y, \quad \bar y\in \Gamma.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.616281+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a708c2ae06c41d8a871302e56bf262b5eccfaa9c7464bc3d8dfc338e288e69fb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Assuming the statements of Propositions~\ref{prop-3.1}--\ref{prop-3.3}, let~$f\in\Cloc^+$ and let~$g$ be related to~$f$ as in \eqref{E:3.22a}. Then for all~$t>0$,
\begin{equation}

