{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.687846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_aa1c583c161f5e92075c7e2f273ffba7704f07c1b860ab7cf0ce92a0a57846cd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 26 (Geometric axis and gate decomposition) of arXiv:2607.26377, '\textbf{Entire Logarithmic Signatures\\ of Bounded-Variation Paths in Finite Dimensions}'] Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real normed space, let $0\neq v\in V$, and let $g,a\in\Sl(V)$ satisfy
\[
g=a\e^v a^{-1}.
\]
Equip $V$ with an auxiliary Euclidean norm, denoted by $\norm{\cdot}_{\mathrm{Euc}}$, and set
\[
\mathcal{A}=a\mathcal{A}_v
 =\set{a\e^{tv}:t\in\R},
\qquad L_g(x)=gx,
\]
and let $p$ be the metric projection of $1$ onto $\mathcal{A}$. Then, for every $x\in\Sl(V)$,
\begin{align}
 d_{\infty}(x,gx)
 &=2d_{\infty}(x,\mathcal{A})+\norm{v}_{\mathrm{Euc}},
 \\
 \mathcal{A}
 &=\set{x\in\Sl(V):d_{\infty}(x,gx)=\norm{v}_{\mathrm{Euc}}}.

\end{align}
Thus $\mathcal{A}$ is the unique minimum-displacement axis of $L_g$ and is independent of the chosen factorisation of $g$. Moreover,
\begin{equation}
[1,g]=[1,p]\cup[p,gp]\cup[gp,g],
\end{equation}
where the three pieces meet only at $p$ and $gp$, and
\begin{equation}
gp=p\e^v,
\qquad
g=p\e^v p^{-1}.
\end{equation}
Finally,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}\cap[1,g]=[p,gp].
\end{equation}
