{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.223901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab9adfdcb63d083d0ec63bb07296c77010d713d998912c354783f45b90334d13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm conv-DL-infty of arXiv:2411.16376, 'Higher order approximations in arcsine laws for subordinators'] Under the assumptions of Theorem $(Thm:potential-infty of the paper)$ with  $\alpha_0=\alpha\in[0,1]$, let us define a function $\varepsilon_2:(0,+\infty)\to\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ by
\begin{align}
    \Pi((x,+\infty))=
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
    \frac{c_0 x^{-\alpha}}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}+\varepsilon_2(x),
    \quad 0<x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Set
\begin{align}
   \varepsilon_3(s,x)
   =
   s\Pi((s-sx,+\infty))\varepsilon_1(sx),
   \quad 0<s, x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Then, for $s\in(0,+\infty)$, the finite measure 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in dx,\; 0<\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}<1\bigg),
\quad
0<x<1,
\end{align}
 admits a density expressed in the form
\begin{align}
   s\Pi(&(s-sx,+\infty))u(sx)
   \\
   =\,&\frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}x^{-1+\alpha}(1-x)^{-\alpha}
   +
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
   \sum_{k=1}^n
   \frac{c_0 s^{\alpha_k-\alpha}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}x^{-1+\alpha_k}(1-x)^{-\alpha}
   \\
   &+\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{s^{\alpha_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)}
   x^{-1+\alpha_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)
   +
   O(\varepsilon_3(s,x)),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to+\infty$,}

\end{align}
uniformly in $x\in I$ for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$. 
Therefore, for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$,
\begin{align}
	\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(&\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in I\bigg)
	\\
	=
	\,&\frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}\int_I x^{-1+\alpha}(1-x)^{-\alpha}\,dx
	\\
	&+
	 \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
	\sum_{k=1}^n
   \frac{c_0 s^{\alpha_k-\alpha}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}
   \int_I x^{-1+\alpha_k}(1-x)^{-\alpha}\,dx
   \\
   &+\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{s^{\alpha_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)}
   \int_I
   x^{-1+\alpha_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)\,dx
   +
  O\bigg(\sup_{x\in I}\varepsilon_3(s,x)\bigg),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to+\infty$}.

\end{align}

\begin{Rem}
By Remark (Rem:Pi-infty of the paper), if $0<\alpha<1$, then $\varepsilon_2(x)=o(x^{-\alpha})$, as $x\to+\infty$.	
\end{Rem} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.287634+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_abab8d4b410257a2c1f80748e11797868caaa188ac42fda1efce989d48b140f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm 5.1 of arXiv:2411.01847, 'Well-Posedness of Stochastic Chemotaxis System'] Let $(u, v, \tau_m)$ be a local mild solution of system ((main2 of the paper)), with $\tau_m$ defined by
    \begin{align*}
        \tau_m = \inf \{ t > 0 \mid \|u\|_{\Upsilon^u_t} \geq m \}.
    \end{align*}
    Then, for $p\geq 2$, the following It\^{o} formula for $\|u(t)\|_{p}^{p}$ holds $\mathbb{P}$-a.s. for all $t \in [0, \tau_m]$,
    \begin{align}
        &\|u(t)\|_{p}^{p} - \|u_0\|_{p}^{p} +p(p - 1)  \int_0^{t}\|\nabla u^{\frac{p}{2}}(s)\|_2^2 \, ds \nonumber \\
        &= p(p - 1) \chi \int_0^{t} \int_\mathcal{O} u^{p - 1}(s) \nabla u(s) \cdot \nabla G * u(s) \, dx \, ds \nonumber \\
        &\quad + p \int_0^{t} \int_\mathcal{O} u^{p - 1}(s) g(u(s)) \, dx \, ds \nonumber \\
        &\quad + p \sum_{i=1}^\infty \int_0^{t} \int_\mathcal{O} b_i\|u(s)\|_q^{r}|u(s)|^{p} \, dx \, dW^i_s \nonumber \\
        &\quad + \frac{p(p - 1)}{2} \int_0^{t} \|u(s)\|_q^{2r}\int_\mathcal{O} \sum_{i=1}^\infty b_i^2 |u(s)|^{p} \, dx \, ds.
    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.251972+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_abae8b70da380ddd6c87ec61e79ac431c2f71be0c30d837dd9378f3883cc985a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm: SSC JSQ of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] [State Space Collapse for JSQ]

    For JSQ under in heavily overloaded condition (ass:heavy_overload of the paper), the following inequality holds:
