{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.849212+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b377443f9a8b7431b51ee8bbc4da99880d2ac3ba0785471d45fb29e35512ffe9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 of arXiv:2511.10814, '\Large \bf \boldmath\ \\ Convergence of the extended Kalman filter with small and state-de'] Let $\widetilde{W}(t)$ be a $\mathbb{F}$-Brownian motion with values in $\mathbb{R}^{r}$, $n \in \mathbb{N}$, and let $A$ be a family of $\mathbb{F}$-adapted $(\K, k)$-stable processes where $\K$ is $\mathbb{F}$-adapted  and $k$ is  some family of deterministic positive and  locally bounded  functions. We suppose that $\x(t)$ is a family of $\mathbb{R}^{n}$-valued semi-martingales satisfying
				\begin{equation}
					d\x(t)=A(t)\x(t)dt + F(t)dt + D(t)d\widetilde{W}(t), 
				\end{equation}
				where $F(t)$ and $D(t)$ are  $\mathbb{F}$-adapted processes satisfying   for some $ \a \in [0,1/2) $ 
				\begin{align} 
					\x^{\top}(t)\K^{-1}(t)F(t) &\leq           ~   \a k(t)\x^{\top}(t)\K^{-1}(t)\x(t) + \mathcal{O}(k(t)), \\
					D^{\top}(t)\K^{-1}(t)D(t) &=~ \mathcal{O}(k(t)).
				\end{align}	
				If $\x^{\top}(0)\K^{-1}(0)\x(0)$ is bounded in $L^{q}$, 		
				then for any $q\geq 1$ and $\eps$ small enough,  the process\\ $\x^{\top}(t)\K^{-1}(t)\x(t)$ is  bounded in $L^{q}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.751804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b37bf48eb9ad503b57ca42109ffb0729bcc1edb1687ae5e58e8b5c8565cc3aa6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 7 of arXiv:2609.00448, 'Equivariant formality and representation theory'] (\cite[p. 172]{H}, \cite{GD}). Let $A$ be a ring and $B$ an $A$-algebra. The module of K\"ahler differentials $\Omega^1_{B/A}$ of $B$ over $A$ is the quotient of the $B$-module freely generated by the symbols $\{db| b\in B\}$ by the submodule generated by the following sets
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item(Constancy of elements of $A$) $\{da| a\in A\}$, 
		\item(Linearity of $d$) $\{d(b_1+b_2)-db_1-db_2| b_1, b_2\in B\}$, and 
		\item(Derivation) $\{d(b_1b_2)-b_1db_2-b_2db_1| b_1, b_2\in B\}$.
	\end{enumerate}
	The Grothendieck differentials $\Omega^*_{B/A}$ is defined to be the exterior $B$-algebra $\displaystyle\bigoplus_{p=0}^\infty \bigwedge\nolimits_B^p\Omega_{B/A}^1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.474460+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b38d2fc5717ed88afc0da9f26b60fbc717f9a1a00091e0cc60b406fe11cd453e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:U-inside of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[lemma]{lem:semicircle-log-potential}
For \(\left\lverty\right\rvert\le\sqrt2\),
\begin{equation}
 U(y)=\frac{y^2}{2}-\frac12-\frac12\log2.
\end{equation}
For \(\left\lverty\right\rvert>\sqrt2\),
\begin{equation}
 U'(y)=y-\operatorname{sgn}(y)\sqrt{y^2-2}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, relative to its quadratic continuation from the edge,
\begin{equation}
 U(y)=\frac{y^2}{2}-\frac12-\frac12\log2
 -\int_{\sqrt2}^{\left\lverty\right\rvert}\sqrt{z^2-2}\,\,\mathrm d z,
 \qquad \left\lverty\right\rvert>\sqrt2.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

