\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.365704+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c015c69c3c5900abc3b61e2621cef12d43bd000e08dbeabf72fa6caec1790d36","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn eq:-19 of arXiv:2411.13403, 'Path weighting method for spot sensitivities and its variance reduction'] We denote by $\hat{Z}$ the vector obtained by reflecting the first
element of $Z$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\hat{Z}=(-Z_{1},Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}),
\end{equation}
and by $\bar{Z}$ the vector obtained by replacing the first element
of $Z$ by zero, i.e.
\begin{equation}
\bar{Z}=(0,Z_{2},\dots,Z_{N}).
\end{equation}
Moreover, the process $\hat{S}_{t}$ and $\bar{S}_{t}$ are defined
to be generated by replacing $Z$ with $\hat{Z}$ and $\bar{Z}$ in
((eq:-1 of the paper)) respectively, i.e. 
\begin{align}
\hat{S}_{t_{i+1}} & =H_{i}(\hat{S}_{t_{i}},\sqrt{\Delta t_{i+1}}\hat{Z}_{i+1}),\;\hat{S}_{t_{0}}=s,\\
