{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:11.412887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c08e0de45fe9b0631ac6d2ba937f4c6dae6fbff16913e93a9cc2c404dded81c3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cons 7 of arXiv:2606.08115, '2606.08115'] Suppose for two vectors $\ov{x}, \ov{y} \in K$ that $\ov{x} \le \ov{y}$. Then we have
\begin{equation}

V\left(M\ov{x}\right) \le V\left(M\ov{y}\right).
\end{equation}
\begin{proof}
Note that the inequality $\ov{x} \le \ov{y}$ entails $m_n\left(\ov{x}\right) \le m_n\left(\ov{y}\right)$ for every natural number $n$, where the function $m_n$ is defined by (\ref{mn_def}). From this inequality and relation (\ref{e}), we get the desired inequality.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.709099+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c092c0ff70620728507759906433e76880f0350c8256e735af8d900322431e52","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:BESQdual of arXiv:2411.04773, 'Meeting of squared Bessel flow lines and application to the skew Brownian motion'] [aïdékon2023stochastic] 
Define the dual flow ${\mathcal S}^*$ by, for $r\le x$ and $b\ge 0$,
\begin{equation}
    {\mathcal S}^*_{r,x}(b) := \inf\{a\ge 0\,:\, {\mathcal S}_{-x,-r}(a)>b\}.
\end{equation}
Then ${\mathcal S}^*$ is a general ${\rm BESQ}^{2-\delta}$ flow driven by the white noise $-\mathcal W^*$, where $\mathcal W^*$ is the image of $\mathcal W$ under the map $(a,r)\mapsto(a,-r)$. In the case $\delta\in (0,2)$, ${\mathcal S}^*$ is killed if ${\mathcal S}$ is not killed, and it is not killed if ${\mathcal S}$ is killed. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.938238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c09eb814126403601ffa0a580dbefaa06adfe953b426ff78b62086c9709ad602","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ Assume that $f : \N \rightarrow ( 0, \infty)$ satisfies $\E_{\pi_{T}} \big( \frac{(Df)^{2}}{f} \big) < \infty.$ Then, equality holds in Theorem \ref{thm:FIsc}, namely
    \begin{displaymath}
        I( T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT}) = a I(X | \pi_{T} )
    \end{displaymath}
    for every $a \in (0,1)$ if and only if $f(k)=e^{\beta k + \gamma}$ for some $\beta ,\gamma \in \R$ (that is $X$ is Poisson distributed).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:00:46.823679+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c0a383c97e04ee0df79a04db507b6fee9b17a200dacfd59ee97dcedbe40dd255","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 25 of arXiv:2608.13208, 'Universality in dimension $1$ of kinetically constrained lattice gases'] For every $\omega\in\Omega_{0}$, the path given in Definition \ref{def:path}
satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\omega_{m}(x)=\omega(x)$ for all $m$ and all $x\notin[-1,l(\omega)]$.
\item $\omega_{m}(-1)=1,\ \omega_{m}(0)=1$ for $m\in\{0,\dots,n\}$, and
$\omega_{m}(-1)=0,\ \omega_{m}(0)=1$ for $m\in\{n+1,\dots,2n+1\}$.
