{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-09T07:38:10+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2d3ac9feaa09091756544ea5fd288f96f2484334d879e4c72930c5ea6b59984","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3.1 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] The process $({\bar X}^\varepsilon_t)_{t \ge 0}$ with state space $\{0,1\}$ and c\`adl\`ag  trajectories defined by 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\forall t \ge 0, \quad {\bar X}^\varepsilon_t = \sum_{k\ge 0} {\mathbf 1}_{ t \in [e_{2k+1}^{\varepsilon}, e_{2k+2}^\varepsilon )} 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
converges in law to the jump Markov process $({\bar X}_t)_{t \ge 0}$ defined in Section \ref{sec:imr}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.662689+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2da472232e2fff50254fbc38ad3f336d31346fa3a1b873cc5fc0ddafcffccd8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem ratio TSAW is ratio G of arXiv:2603.17858, 'Decay of correlations and zeros for the hard-core model'] Let $G=(V,E)$ be a graph, let $v$ be a vertex of $G$ and let $(\lambda_u)_{u\in V}$ be a vector of variables. 
    Then there exists a vector of variables $(\tilde \lambda_w)_{w\in V(T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)})}$ with $\tilde \lambda_w\in \{\lambda_u\mid u\in V\}$ for each vertex $w$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        R_{T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)},v}(\tilde\lambda_w)=R_{G,v}(\lambda_u).  
    \end{equation}
    Moreover, for any vertex $u$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$, the induced subtree $(T,u)$ of $T_{\mathrm{SAW}(G,v)}$, with those vertices $w$ such that $u$ is on the unique path from $w$ to $v$, is the self avoiding walk tree of an induced subgraph of $G$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.169329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2daf02a4503c3015c0bccd8b003dda9e8421c5730a0c05db5b8d3f516acc3ac","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D_SB of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] A graph $H$ is {\it strictly balanced} if 
\begin{equation}
\frac{e(F)-1}{v(F)-2} < \lambda(H),
\end{equation}
for every subgraph $F\subset H$ 
with $3\leqslant v(F)<v(H)$, 
where
\begin{equation}
\lambda(H)=\frac{e(H)-2}{v(H)-2}
\end{equation}
is its {\it $\lambda$-density} 
(extending \ (equation E_lambda of the paper) above).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:43:18.993083+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2e15a73679406db9d344f93e6773d01ac956d8e06e11baf6c666210c9b43644","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma HLP of arXiv:2609.02541, 'A note on the norm of the embedding of $\text{PW}^2$ into $\text{PW}^q$'] Assume that $f,g: [0, \infty) \xrightarrow{} \mathbb{R}^{+}$ are non-increasing functions with 
    \begin{equation}

    \int_0^t f(x) dx \leq \int_0^t g(x)dx
    \end{equation}
    for all $0<t< \infty$.
    Then 
    $$\int_0^{\infty} \Phi(f(x))dx \leq \int_0^{\infty} \Phi(g(x))dx$$
    holds for every increasing convex function $\Phi: \mathbb{R}^{+} \xrightarrow{} \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that $\Phi(0) = 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.706151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2e515e79f483d3434e4d2aa9ba801837fe64d5825e1120b065dd02a1fdec11e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop strong inclusion of arXiv:2609.00436, 'A counterexample to the stable forking conjecture'] Let $Q \subseteq Q'$ be division rings with $Q$ a subring of $Q'$, and $M \subseteq M'$ with $M$ a left $Q$-module and $M'$ a left $Q'$-module, such that $M$ is a submodule of $M'$ with the induced $Q$-module structure. Then the following are equivalent:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Every linearly independent set in $M$ over $Q$ remains linearly independent over $Q'$.

        \item Some basis for $M$ over $Q$ remains linearly independent over $Q'$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:17.977320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2f8f12fe017a3a826ffca6ba1b8a30f53b4bfd6990e9b440ae8918320950690","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (\cite{Kac,Pleijel}) of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] If $\partial D$ is sufficiently regular (e.g., smooth), then
\begin{align}

\msf T_0(t)=\tfrac{\msf{A}(D)}{2\pi}t^{-1}
-\tfrac{\msf{L}(\partial D)}{4\sqrt{2\pi}}t^{-1/2}+O(1)\qquad\text{as }t\to0,
\end{align}
where $\msf L(\partial D)$ is the length of the curve
$\partial D$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.520151+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c2fee1c24210eacc1cf1ed8b7ff3995323fc2c571452bc7ae35f2d6e62b7320e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.06646, 'Three-dimensional stochastic wave equation with non-Lipschitz coefficients'] Assume $u_0\in C^2\lr{\R^3}$. Suppose $u_0$, $\nabla u_0$, and $v_0$ are bounded and let $u_0$, $\Delta u_0$, and $v_0$ be H\"{o}lder continuous with exponents $\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$, and $\alpha_3$, respectively. We also assume Assumption \ref{A:CovAssumption} and that $b$ and $\sigma$ are locally Lipschitz with the following growth condition.
    \begin{equation}
        b(z)=O\lr{|z|\lr{\log |z|}^{\theta_1}}\quad\text{and}\quad \sigma(z)=O\lr{|z|\lr{\log |z|}^{\theta_2}}\quad\text{as $|z|\to \infty$}\,,
    \end{equation}
    with $0<\theta_1<  2$, $0<\theta_2<\frac{\bar{\nu}+1}{2}$ and $\bar{\nu}=\min\{\nu,\nu_1,\nu_2\}$, where $\nu,\nu_1,\nu_2$ are given by Assumption~\ref{A:CovAssumption}. Then there exists a unique global solution $u$ to~\eqref{E:SWE}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.332917+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c300df17ce7dceb0033d8335325eb88e5aba248d9194575621f1bdb17f80701e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2609.03959, 'The combinatorial Hopf algebra of signed graphs'] Given a connected signed graph $\Sigma = (V,E, \sigma)$ and $S\subseteq V$, define $I_\Sigma(S)$ to be the set of edges $e\in E$ satisfying one of the following conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\sigma(e)=+$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $S$
\item $\sigma(e) = +$ and the endpoints of $e$ are in $V\backslash S$ 
\item $\sigma(e) = -$ and $e$ has one endpoint in $S$ and the other one in $V\backslash S$.
\end{itemize}
Then define $$\zeta_x(\Sigma) := \sum_{A \subseteq V} x^{rk(I_\Sigma(A))}. $$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.764652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c3120cea891cae44da11c7a925b1dc30402a55a328748e872fdf70047ed77689","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 56 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] For every \(\eta>0\),
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{T\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_T\left(
    \int_R^{T-\sigma_T}
      e^{-(M_T-B_{\sigma_T+s})}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0,

\end{equation}
where the integral is zero when its lower endpoint exceeds its upper
endpoint.  Equivalently,
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{R\to\infty}\sup_{a\geq2}
  \widehat{\PP}_a\left(
    \int_R^{a-U_a}e^{-R_a(s)}\,\mathrm ds>\eta
  \right)=0.

\end{equation}
Under \(\mu_H\),
\begin{equation}
  \int_0^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds<\infty
  \quad\text{almost surely},
  \qquad
  \int_R^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds\longrightarrow0

\end{equation}
almost surely and in probability.
The equivalence of the two finite-horizon formulations is the
self-similar identification \eqref{eq:long-horizon-scaling}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:11.638038+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c31bf325542493849ea8e249d982e2c6209d84c0549d0e7af66890d720b85ced","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 29 of arXiv:2605.31300, 'Cone-Additive Functions for Random Walks on Free Products of Graphs'] Assume that $\mathscr{R}>1$. Let $W$ be a weight function as above such that (\ref{eq: ass of weights}) is satisfied.
