{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.570296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c349dcd94fd9b88f739f43b29337243d33533e315fce6a7ccc464ac75b584741","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary corollary_riccati_with_source of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] Consider a Riccati equation 
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{cases}
        \bm \dot{\bm{\psi}}_t + \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}}} + \frac{1}{2}\, \bm \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{11}}}} + \frac{1}{2}\, \bm \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}^{\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup} 2} + \bm{\psi}_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup}\bm{\gamma} + \bm f = 0,\quad t \in [0, T]\\
        \bm \bm{\psi}_T = \bm{p},
    \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
    where $\bm{p}, \bm{\gamma}, \bm f \in \TA[2]$. If the coefficient 
    $$
    \widetilde\bm{p} := \bm{p} + \left(\bm f - \dfrac{1}{2}\bm{\gamma}|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}} - \dfrac12\bm{\gamma}^{\mathrel{\sqcup \mkern -3mu \sqcup} 2}\right){\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}} + \gamma{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}} \in \mathcal{B},
    $$
    then there exists a solution $\bm{\psi}$ and $r > 0$ such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For any ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}} \in V\,$, the function $t \mapsto \bm \psi_t^{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}}}$ is $C^1$ on $[0\,, T]\,$.
        \item For any ${\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}} \in V\,$, $\bm \psi_t|_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{v}}}} \in \mathcal{A}_{\widetilde\bm{p}, r}$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.051292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c35de0814ee8e1e3e093999dd0a48d4729c900b3bfbeedc1695d7bce3e77d1e4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Let $\tau\in[0,T]$, $\beta\in\mathbb R$, and $\gamma\in(0,1]$. Then there exists a linear map
\[
\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}: \mathcal A^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta) \to C([0,\tau];B_\beta),
\]
obtained from a dyadic recursion on $\mathcal D^\tau$ and continuous extension to $[0,\tau]$, such that, for every $A\in \mathcal A^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)$, $\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)_0=0$. If we define the dyadic remainder $\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A):=A-\delta\mathcal I_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)$, then
$
\delta \mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)=\delta A.
$
Moreover, if $\gamma\in(0,1)$, then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\in C^{\gamma}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)\ \text{\, and \, }\ \|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{\gamma,\beta;\tau}\le C_\gamma\|\delta A\|_{\gamma,\beta;\tau};
\end{equation}
if $\gamma=1$, then
\begin{equation}

\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\in C_{\log}^{1}(\Delta_\tau^2;B_\beta)\ \text{\, and \, }\ \|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{1,\log;\tau,\beta}\le C_{T}\|\delta A\|_{1,\beta;\tau}.
\end{equation}
while, for every $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, $\|\mathcal R_\tau^{\mathrm d}(A)\|_{1-\varepsilon,\beta;\tau}\le C_{\varepsilon,T}\|\delta A\|_{1,\beta;\tau}$. Here the constants can be chosen independently of $\tau\in[0,T]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.789022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c360b000461ce55f8ed95236a2e68ef62ed70010dfe51c3ecbf6e3a6bd97c259","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem E.g., Lemma 2.8 of~\cite{MaTo} of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] Starting from a domain $\Lambda$ with boundary conditions $\tau$, if we change the boundary conditions on a subset $\Delta \subset \partial \Lambda$ of size $|\Delta|$ to get boundary conditions $\tau^{\Delta}$, then 
    \begin{align*}
        t_{\textsc{mix}}(\Lambda, \tau) \le  4\beta |E(\Lambda)|\log 2\cdot e^{4\beta |\Delta|}\cdot  t_{\textsc{mix}}(\Lambda, \tau^\Delta)
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.633551+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c362a2cb370a368e04b0a575c39cdcdc6f024eeddceaf28dbcff41b311e08179","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:lattice of arXiv:2412.09145, 'Asymptotic expansions for normal deviations of random walks conditioned to stay positive'] Assume that $\mathbf E|X|^{r+3}$ is finite for some integer $r \ge 1$.
Assume also that $S_n$ is integer valued with maximal span $1$. Then there exist polynomials $P_1$, $P_2$,\ldots, $P_{r+1}$ such that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf P(S_n=x,\tau>n) 
=
    e^{-\frac{x^2}{2\sigma^2n}}
    \sum_{\nu = 2}^{r+1}
        \frac{P_\nu(x/\sigma\sqrt{n})}{n^{\nu/2}} 
+
    O\left( \frac{1}{\min (n^{(r+2)/2}, x^{r+2})} \right).
\end{align*}
Every polynomial $P_\nu$ has degree $3\nu-5$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:43:45.380889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c38144c3322f8f9109cecca67b1116e1383aa51f0190ec3fc5d176db104eca60","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 22 of arXiv:2609.04033, 'From configuration spaces to graph complexes via $\mathbf{FA}$-modules'] Let $d>0$.  The $\mathbf{FS}$-module structure on $H^d(\Gamma_{2,\ast})$ lifts to an $\mathbf{FA}$-module given by:	

	\begin{itemize}
		\item  If $d\equiv 2 \text{ mod } 4$ then $H^d(\Gamma_{2,\ast}) = 0$.
		\item  If $d\equiv 0 \text{ mod } 4$ then $H^d(\Gamma_{2,\ast}) = 
		\mathsf{C}_{(2^{d/2})}$.
		\item If $d=q+1$ is odd then 

	$$H^{d}(\Gamma_{2,\ast}) = 
\ds\bigoplus_{0\leq i<q/2} w_{i,q}\mathsf{C}_{(2^i,1^{q-2i})}$$

where 
$$w_{i,q} = \begin{cases}
	 \text{dim}(\op{M}_{q-2i+2}) & \text{ if } i \text{ is odd,} \\ 	 
	 \text{dim}(\op{S}_{q-2i+2}) & \text{ if } i \text{ is even.} 
\end{cases}
$$
the dimensions of the space of modular (resp.\ modular cusp) forms of the given weight.
	\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.214262+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_c381dc129c5ed6c4ef6420c934f0cdf1bca785b7bc29da953fc57f411882007e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 15 of arXiv:2608.03956, 'From Triangular Array Progression\\ to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions'] Let $\tau^{(1)}$ be defined by $\tau^{(1)}_{n,j} \triangleq \T^{(1)}_{n + 3, j + 3}$ for every $n \geq 0$ and $1 \leq j \leq n +1$. Then, 
\begin{enumerate}
