\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.867654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd6a3883c0a0bec97de3e57b6b0467df295c3c4a99867444fdbcd4cb31020437","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 (Weak sub-solution) of arXiv:2608.26834, 'Small-time annealed large deviations principle for one-dimensional diffusions in a random '] A nonnegative function $u$ on $(0,S)\times D$ is called a bounded weak sub-solution  of the differential equation
		\begin{equation}
			\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}={\mathcal L}_W u
		\end{equation}
		if
		\[
		u\in L^\infty((0,S)\times D) \cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}((0,S);H^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)),
		\]
		and, for every nonnegative $\varphi\in C_c^1((0,S);H_0^1(D)\cap L^\infty(D))$, one has
		\begin{equation*}
			-\int_0^S\!\int_Du\,\partial_s\varphi\,\dd \mu_W\dd s
			+\int_0^S\mathcal E_W(u(s),\varphi(s))\,\dd s\le0.
		\end{equation*}
		If equality holds for all such $\varphi$, then $u$ is called a weak solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.315354+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd7bf153fdaaeb3ff2101886b165be72c514ed0f033753d9163e75a7643c6059","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:1530 of arXiv:2411.14921, 'A boundary Harnack principle and its application to analyticity of 3D Brownian intersectio'] Consider the triples $(x_i,y_i,D_i)$ with $x_i\in D_i$ and $y_i\in \partial D_i$ that belong to one of the following three cases
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-4}$ with $l=2,3,4$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{-1}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_2^\circ$.
        \item $x_i\in\partial\mathcal{B}_{2}$ and $D_i=\mathcal B_l^\circ\setminus \mathcal B_{-1}$ with $l=5,6,7$.
    \end{enumerate}
   Let $\eta_i$ be sampled according to $\mathbb{P}_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}$ and $\overline\eta:=\cup_{i=1}^k \eta_i$. Then, for each $\varepsilon>0$, there exists $0<C_1=C_1(\varepsilon,k)<\infty$ such that
    \begin{equation}
        \prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb P_{x_i\to y_i}^{D_i}\Big(K(p_{1/2,1}^{\overline\eta\cup\mathcal B_0})<C_1\Big)>1-\varepsilon.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-09T07:38:10+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd7f42fa6cce70728b6f738f882eb1c9265b6470f11903f06083059a131ed96a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4.5 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For any $\ka,\eps,t>0$, we let $\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t)$ denote the integral operator on $L^2(D)$ with kernel
