{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.962085+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4bc05ef354d88a5b8d7a51ea00c1fdad15c1d6069d318b8529f40c027924711","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 7 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] If Assumption \ref{asmp:baseline} and $T\leq \infty$ hold, then $V_T$ is upper semicontinuous, $\mathcal{C}_T$ is open, and $\mathcal{D}_T$ is closed. Furthermore, the stopping time
\begin{equation}
    \tau^*_T(t,x):=\inf\{0\le s<T-t:(t+s,X_s^x)\in\mathcal{D}_T\}\wedge(T-t)
\end{equation}
is the smallest optimal stopping time for $T<\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.143328+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4d7b483ce5825ecfb794897bc758799b7e4239ff49dfa75bf249af1a38266bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:tail-rate of arXiv:2607.04620, 'The Exact Ville Identity\\ \large From the Absorbing Case to the General Law\\ with an App'] Let $S_n$ be a centered martingale with $\mathbb{E}[S_n^2]=n$, and let $T$ be a first-passage time with $L=\mathbb{E}[S_T\mathbf{1}(T<\infty)]$ finite as in (thm:conservation of the paper). Then, from the moment inequalities of [klass2026beyond], for every $p>1$,
\begin{equation}
  |L|^{p/(p-1)}
  \le
  \liminf_{n\to\infty}\mathbb{P}(T>n)
  \left(\mathbb{E}[|S_n|^p\mathbf{1}(T>n)]\right)^{1/(p-1)}.

\end{equation}
The case $p=2$, after dropping the indicator inside the moment, gives the cruder $\liminf_n n\mathbb{P}(T>n)\ge L^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.464581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4e1c50007ab52eb8c847a0e778a7024c1729c18fb4edc65f8168ed7a23aaee3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma notation:LT of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] For any $\gamma\geq 0$, the Laplace transform of $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2$, under the Palm probability distribution $\mathbb{P}^0_{{\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}}$ of ${\mathcal A}_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$, is given by 

\begin{equation}
    {\mathcal L }^0_{H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}^2}(\gamma)= \left(1+\frac{\gamma}{\lambda\pi}\right)^{-1/2}, 
\end{equation}

that is, $H_{\scriptscriptstyle V}$  follows a Nakagami distribution with parameter $\left({\frac{1}{2}}, \frac{1}{2\lambda\pi}\right)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.385969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d4ead1d68abc39cb01f002b5b2fa7b462aa73e4c5cc5a132a07f91ee21ed2e12","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_hops of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let~$\gamma = (x_0,\ldots, x_n)$ be a path in~$\mathcal G^t$. Assume that~$x_0$ and~$x_n$ belong to~$\mathcal H$ (the infinite cluster of~$\mathcal G$), and~$x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1}$ do not belong to~$\mathcal H$ (so that each of them is either in~$t\Z^d$ or in some finite cluster of~$\mathcal G$). Let
	\[b:= \max\left\{\|y-x\|_\infty:\; \begin{array}{l} x, y \in V,\; x \text{ and } y \text{ are in the same finite} \\ \text{cluster of }\mathcal G, \text{ and this cluster is visited by } \gamma \end{array}\right\},\]
		that is,~$b$ is the maximum~$\ell_\infty$-diameter of all finite clusters of~$\mathcal G$ intersected by~$\gamma$. Then,
	\begin{equation*}
		\sum_{e \in \gamma} \tau^t_e \ge \frac{\K t}{2t+b}\cdot \|x_n -x_0\|_\infty.
	\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:06:07.090618+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d50c4627629a66377aee3aa83edd934a36260a1ae151d391c688be98e20ad27c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 27 of arXiv:2608.28148, 'Flows and homotopies of Banach Lie algebroids'] Let $(E,\pi,M)$ be a Banach bundle and $U$ be an open set of $M$. A \emph{linear local}  vector field on $E$ is a pair $(\mathcal{X},X)$ where $\mathcal{X}$ is a vector field on $E_{U}$ and $X$ a vector field on $U$ such that
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
E_U \arrow[r,"\mathcal{X}"] \arrow[d,"\pi"]& TE_U \arrow[d,"T\pi"] \\ U \arrow[r,"X"] & TU
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
is a bundle morphism. When $U=M$ the pair $(\mathcal{X},X)$ is simply called a \emph{linear vector field} on $E$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.376438+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d51060e7d37e6ed3b7dbb15137eac2e5e003d9cda09db376d3aaec27b7af7bf2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:e2 of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] For any $N \in \mathbb N$, we have that
\begin{align}
\lVert g^n \rVert_{C^2(\bar \Omega_N)} \leq K(N) \quad \text{for all $n \geq N+10$},
\end{align}
where $K(N)$ is a positive constant depending only on $N$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.800198+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d51983c4f63a77f17372197f2523848359aad2a1cf50390086c1259face99d30","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme 76 (Chernoff bound) of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\{X_i\}_{i=1}^{n-1}$ be a collection of $n-1$ random uniformly distributed iid variables on $\intff{0}{1}$. Then, for any  $\xi \geq 1$,
it holds

$$\mathbb{P}\bigg[ \sum_{j=1}^{n-1} \big|\ln X_i \big| \geq (n-1)\xi \bigg] \,  \leq  \, \big( \xi \mathrm{e}^{1- \xi} \big)^{n-1}\, .$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.970104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d51a291b1fe725be8d70ce2271b3261f952df92f7679fffc552cb42f4d82003d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 22 of arXiv:2607.22416, 'A multi-player optimal stopping game based on a spider Brownian bridge'] There exists a Nash equilibrium in the multi-player version of the optimal stopping game with linear payoffs, if for every $i\in\{1,...,N\}$ 
    \begin{equation}

        p_iQ_i^i+ \sum_{j\neq i,j=1}^N p_jQ_j^i\geq0.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.846183+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d51b9a0facced450fda634c23e49e0a40de97487f881bf8f571bbc8760e8c29a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma timeregularity-es of arXiv:2603.26307, '\LARGE The incompressible Navier--Stokes--Fourier system with thermal noise'] Let 
			$(u_{\epsilon} ,\psi_{\epsilon},q_\epsilon)$ be the weak martingale solutions to  (equation ep_approx of the paper)--(equation eqn354865865 of the paper) with initial data $(u_0,\psi_0)$ constructed in Proposition (prp-existence-ep of the paper). Then, for every $\alpha \in (0,1/2)$ and $l > 5/2$, there exists a constant \[C = C(\alpha,l, u_0,\psi_0, F_1,F_2, G_1,G_2,T) <\infty, \] such that
		\begin{align}
			\mathbb{E}\|u_{\epsilon}\|_{C^{\alpha}([0,T];H^{-l}(\mathbb{T}^3;\mathbb{R}^3))}+\mathbb{E}\|\psi_{\epsilon}\|_{W^{\alpha,2}([0,T];H^{-l}(\mathbb{T}^3))} \leq C,
		\end{align}
			for all $\epsilon\in (0,1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.121990+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d52e6289375e7a63e75272d8f8b8961d01873c973ebaf833b57dc0f68380af08","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Conj desirables of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Under the setting and notation of Theorem~(maintheorem2 of the paper), let us write $\mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n)$ the set of maximum size matchings in $G_n$, then:
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
                \lim_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{1}{|E(G_n)|} \sum_{e \in E(G_n)} \mathbbm{1}_{\forall M \in \mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n), e \in M}\right] = \mathbb{P}_{((i,Z),(i',Z'))\sim \zeta'\otimes\zeta'}\left( i+i'<k \right), \\
                \lim_{n \rightarrow +\infty} \mathbb{E}\left[ \frac{1}{|E(G_n)|} \sum_{e \in E(G_n)} \mathbbm{1}_{\forall M \in \mathcal{M}_{\max}(G_n), e \notin M}\right] = \mathbb{P}_{((i,Z),(i',Z'))\sim \zeta'\otimes\zeta'}\left( i+i'>k \right).
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation} (Stated as a conjecture by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.560798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d531d2a49a7cf4abea0b7ce23b398f81f8008980b3dff4b678c266d266ee3f5d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:class_B of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] We say that $\bm{p} \in \mathcal{B} \subset T(\mathbb{C}^2)$ if there exist three complex numbers
    $$
    \alpha, \beta \in \{z \in \mathbb{C}\colon \, \mathfrak{Re}(z) > 0\} \cup \{ 0\}, \quad \gamma \in \mathbb{C},
    $$
    two natural numbers $m, n \in \mathbb{N}^*$, and three coefficients $\bm{q}, \bm{r}, \bm s \in T(\mathbb{C}^2)$ satisfying
    \begin{align}
        \deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}(\bm{q}) < 2n,  \quad \deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}}}(\bm{q}) = 0, \quad \deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}(\bm{r}) < 2m, \quad \deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}}(\bm s) < 2m \land 2n,
    \end{align}
    such that 
    \begin{equation}
        \bm{p} = -\alpha({\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}^{\otimes 2n} + \bm{q}) - \beta({\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{1}}}^{\otimes 2m} + \bm{r}){\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}} + \alpha\beta\bm s + \gamma {\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{\varnothing}}}.
    \end{equation}
    {The time degrees $\deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}}}(\bm r)$ and $\deg_{{\color{NavyBlue}{\mathbf{0}}}}(\bm s)$ are only required to be finite.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:44:24.742549+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d55074263fbdec3aed4858036ffac6ce75cc4063ec27ba23fdd410b1619a7702","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ExpGSA of arXiv:2605.05193, 'Grokability in five inequalities'] Fix $n\ge 1$, $\rho>0$, and an integer $N\ge 1$.
Let $x_1,\dots,x_N$ be i.i.d.\ uniform on the unit sphere $S^n\subset\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ and set
\[
Q:=\bigcap_{j=1}^N\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n+1}:\ \langle x,x_j\rangle\le \rho\}.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\,\operatorname{GSA}(Q)
=
N\,\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-\rho^2/2}
\int_{\mathbb{R}^n}\varphi_n(y)\,\bigl(1-p(|y|)\bigr)^{N-1}\,dy,
\end{equation}
where $p(r)$ depends only on $r\ge 0$ and is given by
\begin{equation}
p(r)=
\Bigg(\int_{-\sqrt{r^2+\rho^2}}^{\sqrt{r^2+\rho^2}}
\Bigl(1-\frac{t^2}{r^2+\rho^2}\Bigr)^{\frac{n-2}{2}}\,dt\Bigg)^{-1}
\int_{\rho}^{\sqrt{r^2+\rho^2}}
\Bigl(1-\frac{t^2}{r^2+\rho^2}\Bigr)^{\frac{n-2}{2}}\,dt .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:31.000470+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d55e853f84b34fca6260c28c070e2e671edabfd72e841bec9db7987ebc9486e6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 47 (Square-root promotion) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] Suppose
\[
 0\le N_q\le MR^2\Id,
 \qquad
 E_q=R_q^*N_qR_q,
\]
and let $L$ be a top eigenspace of $E_q$, with eigenvalue $\lambda>0$.  Set
\begin{equation}
 V_q^{1/2}=\Ran(N_q^{1/2}R_qL).

\end{equation}
Then, for every unit $c\in L$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{P_{V_q^{1/2}}R_qc}^2
 \ge\frac{\lambda}{MR^2},
 \qquad
 \norm{N_q^{1/2}R_qc}^2=\lambda.

\end{equation}
Moreover
\begin{equation}
 \ip{N_q^{1/2}R_qc}{N_q^{1/2}R_qc'}
 =\ip{R_qc}{N_qR_qc'},

\end{equation}
which is a four-source Gram.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.668727+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5600f9cd826e737aaa6ff4acafe83a76f8a0c856aa6704d4379011a4ed8d1fd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Theorem \ref{DecoTheo3}) of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] Let $f : X \rightarrow Y$ be a topologically semi-small map such that $X$ is a closed topological manifolds, the fibered set $R$ is a closed stratified space, and $Y$ is a closed topological $n$-pseudomanifold. Set $B:=f^{-1}(R)$, let $R=\bigsqcup_{\beta}T_{\beta}$ be a suitable decomposition of the fibered set, and let $R_{\operatorname{rel}}$ be its relevant part. Furthermore, let the filtration $Y \supset Y_{n_{R}}(=R) \supset Y_{n_{R}-1} \supset \cdots$ be the refined stratification on $Y$ obtained by Proposition~\ref{RefinedStra}, where $n_{R}:= \dim(R)$. Set $S_{k}:= Y_{k} \setminus Y_{k-1}$, and assume the followings:
        \begin{enumerate}
        	\item for each $r \in R$ the fiber $f^{-1}(r)$ is a closed orientable topological manifold;
        	\item the normal bundle of the manifold $f^{-1}(S_{k})$ in $X$ exists, for each $0 \leq k \leq n_{R}$, and is orientable, and for each $S_{k'}\in R_{\operatorname{rel}}$ and each $r\in S_{k'}$ there is a small open neighborhood $U$ of $r$ such that the connecting homomorphism in the Gysin sequence of the normal bundle of $S_{k}$ restricted to $f^{-1}(U)$ vanishes in degree $\operatorname{codim}_{X}(B)-1$.
        	\end{enumerate}
        	Let $R(f|_{X \setminus B})_{*}\,\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X \setminus B}\simeq \underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y \setminus R}\oplus \mathcal{L}$, where $\mathcal{L}$ is the local system on $Y \setminus R$ associated to the covering map $f|_{X \setminus B}$. If each $\operatorname{codim}_{Y}(S_{k})$ is even and $\operatorname{codim}_{X}(B)\geq 2$, then
        \begin{align*}
        	Rf_{\ast}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{X}[n]\simeq \bigoplus_{S_{k} \in R_{\operatorname{rel}}}\iota_{k\ast}(IC_{\overline{S_{k}}} (R^{0}f\vert_{f^{-1}(S_{k})}\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{S_{k}})) \oplus IC_{Y}(\underline{\mathbb{Q}}_{Y \setminus R} \oplus \mathcal{L}),
        \end{align*}
        where $\iota_{k}:\overline{S_{k}} \hookrightarrow Y$ is the canonical closed inclusion, and $IC^{\overline{p}}_{Y}$ is Deligne's intersection complex with respect to the above refined stratification and the perversity $\overline{p}\in\{\overline{n},\overline{m}\}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.821310+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d562794fd7f0ce986eccae15a7eee51c78382f76d0f7204ea4c61c35240e8358","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:sharp-type-compression of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] For every $h\geq2$ and $k\geq3$,
\begin{equation}
 \max_{\mathsf T\in\mathfrak T^{\max}_{h,k}}\mu_h(\mathsf T)=h^{k-2}.
\end{equation}
Thus the maximal-rank boundary is sharp not only for individual sets: one
weak addition-table type itself forces the critical normalized diameter.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.162583+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5632b0db3e0eb0d45ffbf39f08fce1dc4719dd645c252975053261b738002af","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 13 (Theorem 2.2 of \cite{zhang2016central}) of arXiv:2604.17302, 'Elephant random walk with attributed steps and extractions of random sizes'] Suppose $\{Z_{n}\}$ converges to the constant $\theta^{*}$ almost surely, and that Assumption~\eqref{gen_assump_dist_2} is satisfied. Suppose $0<\rho<1/2$, that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\E\left[\Delta M_{i+1}\left(\Delta M_{i+1}\right)^{t}\Big|\mathcal{F}_{i}\right]=O(n) \text{ almost surely},
\end{equation}
and that there exists some $0<\epsilon<1-\rho$ such that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\delta_{i}=o\left(n^{1-\rho-\epsilon}\right) \text{ almost surely}.
\end{equation}
Then there exist complex random variables $W_{1},\ldots,W_{s}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\frac{n^{\rho}}{(\log n)^{\nu-1}}(Z_{n}-\theta^{*})-\sum_{j\in[s]:\text{Re}(-\lambda_{j})=\rho,\nu_{j}=\nu}\exp\left\{i\text{Im}(\lambda_{j})\log n\right\}W_{j}\left(T^{-1}\right)^{t}e_{j}\convas 0,
\end{equation}
where $i=\sqrt{-1}$, the notation $\text{Im}(\lambda)$ indicates the imaginary part of $\lambda$, the matrix $T$ is as defined via \eqref{similarity_eq_general}, and $e_{j}$ is the $d$-dimensional column vector such that, if its first $\nu_{1}$ coordinates make up its first block, the next $\nu_{2}$ coordinates make up its second block, and so on, then the $\nu_{j}$-th coordinate of its $j$-th block equals $1$, and all other coordinates equal $0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:46:25.315918+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d56bf28d80822730ee9cc9bc1f833deb4d96933b3b7c700ba108629442a62fe6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem ;lpre2 of arXiv:2604.12462, 'Stochastic analysis of Beckner's and related functional inequalities'] Let $\Phi \colon (0,\infty )\to \mathbb{R} $ be as above. Then, for every $0\le s\le t\le 1$, 
it holds that 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
 &\ex \!\left[ 
 \Phi ''(M_{t})|\dv _{t}|^{2}
 \right] 
 -\ex \!\left[ 
 \Phi ''(M_{s})|\dv _{s}|^{2}
 \right] \\
 &=\ex \!\left[ 
 \frac{1}{\Phi ''(M_{t})}\left| 
 \Phi ''(M_{t})\dv _{t}-\Phi ''(M_{s})\dv _{s}
 \right| ^{2}
 \right] \\
 &\quad -\frac{1}{2}\ex \!\left[ 
 \Phi ''(M_{s})^{2}|\dv _{s}|^{2}\int _{s}^{t}du\,\left( 
 \frac{1}{\Phi ''}
 \right) ''\!(M_{u})|\dv _{u}|^{2}
 \right] .
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.719246+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5739603beed91d80c63bb45ff4db1a19c2d116b406d704f2a5fe11f10bafb44","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2609.01166, 'Nonparametric inference for density-dependent McKean--Vlasov diffusions'] For any candidates $\xi,\zeta\in\cX_m$, the truncated invariant densities satisfy
\(
 \mathfrak d_M(\pi_\xi^M,\pi_\zeta^M) \le C_{\mathrm{st}}(1+M)\norm{\xi-\zeta}_{\infty,[0,U]}
\)
with some constant \(C_{\mathrm{st}}>0\) depending on \(V\), \(\kappa\) and \(K\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:57:07.196566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d57fc308d9764eec61a144ac0cb4ea71f7841ff960b37abf55b7363ee34b080f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:cigar-shape of arXiv:2509.04333, 'The limiting law of the Discrete Gaussian level lines'] Fix $A, B$ with angle denoted $\theta_{A, B}$ and $B_1 - A_1 = M_{A, B}$. Define the curves $\ensuremath{\mathcal C}^\pm_{A, B}$ by
		\begin{equation*}
			\ensuremath{\mathcal C}^\pm(t) = \tan(\theta_{A, B})(t-A_1) + \left(\frac{(t - A_1)(M_{A, B} - t + A_1)}{M_{A, B}}\right)^{1/2}(\log L)^2\,.
		\end{equation*}
		Define the cigar shape ${\ensuremath{\mathscr C}} = {\ensuremath{\mathscr C}}(\overline{AB})$ as the region in between the curves $\ensuremath{\mathcal C}^+_{A, B}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathcal C}^-_{A, B}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.303690+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d591c0437aafcd816c836b31a482f8d3f74fda52b3a8c68e0a55b10d9cb8b051","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 43 (Anti-concentration/heat kernel estimates) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Fix $\sigma>0$, $M>0$, $K > 0$. Let $F:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ be decreasing and of exponential decay at infinity. Let $\br_0 \in \Ekk$ be centered and $\delta$-repulsive. Then
    \begin{itemize} 
    \item Case $d\ge 2$: there exists $\epsilon=\epsilon(F,\sigma,M,\delta)>0$ and $C=C(F,\sigma,M,\delta,K)>0$ such that uniformly over $\x\in I^k$ and $\y\in \mathbb R^{kd}$ and integers $r\ge 0$ one has the bound $$
       \sup_{\substack{\br\in \Ekk\\d_{\mathrm{SRI}}(\br,\br_0) <\epsilon
       }}
       \; \Pbb ( |\mathbf R_r-r\mathbf s(\nu_{\br}) -\y|\leq 1) < C r^{-kd/2} \big( 1+ r^{-1/2} |\x-\y|\big)^{-K}. $$
       \item $d=1$. We have the weaker estimate just for the centered chain: $$\mathbf P_{\x}^{\br_0} ( |\mathbf R_r - \y|\le 1) \leq Cr^{-kd/2}  \big( 1+ r^{-1/2} |\x-\y|\big)^{-K}.$$ We also have the following time-averaged bound for non-centered chains when $q\ge 4$: there exists $\epsilon>0$ so that for all $\lambda>0$ one has 
        $$  \sup_{\substack{\br \in \Ek\\d(\br,\br_0)<\epsilon }} \sup_{\x\in I^k} \sup_{r\in\mathbb Z_{\ge 0}} \Ebb [ e^{ \lambda r^{-1/2} \sum_{s=0}^{r-1} \sum_{1\le i'<j'\le k} \ind_{\{|R^{i'}_s-R^{j'}_s|\le 1 \}} }]<\infty.$$ 
       \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.832128+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5a40937db4db779cf13ec9a32f448a425aeb4045b82ee32014b23b64ceda00c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:tailbound_interval of arXiv:2608.05089, 'Correlation decay in area-tilted line ensembles'] There exist constants $C, c > 0$ such that for all $S \geqslant 1$ and $y > 0$,
we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P} \mleft[ \max_{s \in [-S,S]} X^1(s) > C \log(S) +  y \mright]
    \leqslant C S e^{- c y^{3/2}}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.975769+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5a9dc115f9ff84300ecf507070ccc643fb11902597743b428bcf774702512ea","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2606.11271, '\LARGE The $K$-th nearest neighbor random walk on a Poisson point process gets trapped'] For any $\varepsilon >0$, $A>0$ and $n\in \N$, $X_n$ is called an $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer point if there exist some $\rho \in [0,A]$ and some $c\in \R^d$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item[$(i)$] $B(c,\varepsilon) \subset B(X_n, \rho) \cap (\R^d \setminus E_n)$;
\item[$(ii)$] $| (\chi \smallsetminus \{X_n\})\cap \left(   B(X_n, \rho) \cap E_n  \right) | < M$.
\end{itemize}
The set of $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer points is denoted by
$$
\V_p (\varepsilon, A) \coloneqq \{ x\in \chi \,:\, \exists n \in \N \,,\, X_n =x \textrm{ and $X_n$ is $(\varepsilon, A)$-pioneer}\} \,.
$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.163111+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5b6d425871747bb0eb091670c42fdd9d906c3661e779008ec3c2cdd442aba7c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2604.17302, 'Elephant random walk with attributed steps and extractions of random sizes'] Let $\mathcal{A}$, and $\mathcal{A}_{n}\subset \mathcal{A}$ for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, be compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}^{d}$. Let $Q$, taking values in $\mathcal{A}$, and $Q_{n}$, taking values in $\mathcal{A}_{n}$, for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, be random variables, such that $\{Q_{n}\}$ converges almost surely to $Q$. Let $f_{n}:\mathcal{A}_{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ for each $n$, and $f:\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ with $f\in\mathcal{C}^{(0)}(\mathcal{A})$. Let $\beta_{n}>0$ for each $n$ be such that 
\begin{equation}
\sup\left\{\left|f_{n}(x)-f(x)\right|:x\in \mathcal{A}_{n}\right\}\leqslant \beta_{n} \text{ for each } n\in\mathbb{N} \quad \text{and} \quad \beta_{n}\rightarrow 0 \text{ as } n\rightarrow\infty. 
\end{equation}
Then the Cesaro average
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f_{i}(Q_{i}) \convas f(Q) \text{ as }n\rightarrow\infty.
\end{equation} 

Alternatively, let $Q$ as well as $Q_{n}$, for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, take values in $\mathcal{A}$, and let $Q_{n}\convas Q$. Let $f_{n}:\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$ and $f_{n}\in\mathcal{C}^{(0)}(\mathcal{A})$ for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, and $f:\mathcal{A}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$, such that $\{f_{n}\}$ converges uniformly to $f$ on $\mathcal{A}$. Then, once again, the conclusion of \eqref{Cesaro_convergence} holds. A special case of this scenario is where $f_{n}\equiv f$ for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.239020+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5b9b13e397bd060049d09c8f9b8ff6217a8d787a266b1477db2b89c5e224852","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 47 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] The \textbf{asymmetric simple exclusion process (ASEP)} on $\Z$ with parameter $q\in [0,1)$ is a continuous-time Markov chain $(\eta_t)_{t \geq 0}$ on $\{0,1\}^{\Z}$ with generator
\begin{equation}
L_{\Z}f(\eta) = \sum_{x \in \Z} \big(\eta(x)(1-\eta(x+1)) + q \eta(x+1)(1-\eta(x))\big)\big[ f(\eta^{x,x+1})- f(\eta) \big] 
\end{equation} acting on the core of cylinder functions $f \colon \{0,1\}^{\Z} \rightarrow \R$. 
Here, $\eta^{x,x+1}$ denotes the configuration, where we swap the values of $x$ and $x+1$, and leave all other values in $\eta$ unchanged, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
    \eta^{x,x+1}(y) := \begin{cases}
        \eta(x+1) & \text{ if } y=x \\
        \eta(x) & \text{ if } y=x+1 \\
        \eta(y) & \text{ otherwise. }
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
 We define the \textbf{wrapped periodic asymmetric simple exclusion process (periodic ASEP)} as the continuous-time Markov chain $(\eta^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ on $\{0,1\}^{\Z/N\Z}$ with respect to the generator
\begin{equation}
L_{\per}f(\eta) = \sum_{x \in \Z/N\Z} \big(\eta(x)(1-\eta(x+1)) + q \eta(x+1)(1-\eta(x))\big)\big[ f(\eta^{x,x+1})- f(\eta) \big] 
\end{equation} for all cylinder functions $f \colon \{0,1\}^{\Z/N\Z} \rightarrow \R$. We extend the wrapped periodic ASEP $(\eta^{\per}_t)_{t \geq 0}$ to the \textbf{unwrapped periodic ASEP} $(\epb_t)_{t \geq 0}$ on $\{0,1\}^{\Z}$ by setting for all $t\geq 0$
\begin{equation}
    \epb_t(x) := \eta_t( x \textup{ mod } N) . 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

