{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.330194+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d6813cac745447caab46edf0e0ebef5b254223fb29db26d2b9dae2542a1b44a2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:results:uniqueRegime of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Suppose $(U, W)$ satisfy Assumption (as:results:fixedPoint of the paper) and Assumption (as:results:LSI of the paper). Then there exists a unique Cayley fixed point $\pi \in \tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{2, d}$ and we have $\mathbb H_2^* = \mathbb H_2(\pi)$. In other words, for $\mathcal{Z}^n$ as in (equation eq:results:gibbsPFN of the paper), we have
\begin{equation*}
    \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{2n+1} \log \mathcal{Z}^n = \mathbb H_2(\pi).
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, we have \begin{equation}
        \mathbb H_2(\nu) - \mathbb H_2(\pi) = \mathcal{H}(\nu|\pi) - \mathcal{H}(\bar{\nu}|\bar{\pi}), \quad \nu \in \mathcal{Q}_{2, d}.

    \end{equation}
Moreover, if there exists a solution to the 2-MLFE with potentials $(U, W)$ and initial distribution $\pi$, then $\pi$ is the unique stationary distribution of the 2-MLFE in the sense of Definition (def:results:stationary of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.757158+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d68c4bbf56767768f449d783032e54c8f2b3fae4315c9976b67ce7cd2a41ac01","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma5.7 of arXiv:2411.02362, 'Limit theorems for random Dirichlet series: boundary case'] Fix any $\gamma>0$ and put $\mathfrak{s}_n:=\exp(-\exp(n^{1+\gamma}))$ for $n\ge 1 
$. Let $N_1$ and $N_2$ be functions which take positive integer values, may depend on $\gamma$ and satisfy
$N_1(s)\geq 2$ for small positive $s$, $\lim_{s\to 0+}(\log\log N_1(s)/\log 1/s)=0$, $\lim_{s\to 0+}(\log\log N_2(s)/\log 1/s)=1$ and $\lim_{s\to 0+}s\log N_2(s)=0$. Then
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty}f(\mathfrak{s}_n) \sum_{k=2}^{N_1(\mathfrak{s}_n)}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}}{k^{1/2+\mathfrak{s}_n}}\widetilde{\eta}_{k,1}(\mathfrak{s}_n)=0\quad\text{\rm a.s.}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty}f(\mathfrak{s}_n) \sum_{k\ge N_2(\mathfrak{s}_n)+1}\frac{(\log k)^{-1/2}}{k^{1/2+\mathfrak{s}_n}}\widetilde{\eta}_{k,1}(\mathfrak{s}_n)=0\quad\text{\rm a.s.}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.218374+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d69d27f77e1bfa6cf22e29b58ad25539f17fc12054d1eb3fa9309e9ddcf1452e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:LIL of arXiv:2608.20537, 'Persistent anisotropy in multi-dimensional\\ branching Brownian motion'] Let $\beta \in \mathcal{C}^2\big([0, \infty); \mathbb{R}\big)$ satisfy $t \dot{\beta}_t \to 0$ as $t \to \infty$.
  If 
  \begin{equation}

    \int_1^\infty \frac{\mathrm{d} r}{r \exp \beta_r} = \infty,
  \end{equation}
  then almost surely, for every nonempty open $U \subset S^{d-1}$, there exist sequences $t_n \nearrow \infty$ and $p_n \in \mathcal{P}_{t_n}$ such that for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$,
  \begin{equation}

    R_{t_n}(p_n) = 2t_n + \frac{d-2}{2} \log t_n + \beta_{t_n} \quad \text{and} \quad \Theta_{t_n}(p_n) \in U.
