{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.053729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8b22c4ae1f6dfb95f0c9d2118508ba49694b89ad88842bb5ebf7016d5e78bca","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 44 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Fix an admissible scale-dependent selection rule $\mathsf s$ on a domain $\D^{\mathsf s}_{\gamma}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)$.  Let $p\in[1,\infty)$.
If $0<\gamma<1$ and $H\in L^p\big(\Omega; \D^{\mathsf s}_{\gamma}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)\big)$, then $R^{\mathsf s}:=\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,\gamma,\alpha}H$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

\mathbb E\left[\norm{R^{\mathsf s}}_{\gamma,\alpha}^p\right]\le C^p \mathbb E\left[\norm{H}_{\gamma,\alpha}^p\right]
\end{equation}
and $\hdd R^{\mathsf s}=H$ almost surely.  For a family of individual random defects not lying in one fixed linear domain, the same estimate holds with the right-hand side replaced by
\[
C^p\mathbb E\left[\bigl(\norm{H}_{\gamma,\alpha}+C_H(\omega)\bigr)^p\right].
\]
If $H\in L^p\big(\Omega;\D^{\mathsf s}_{1}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)\big)$, then $R^{\mathsf s}:=\hat\Lambda^{\mathsf s}_{S,1,\alpha}H$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

\mathbb E\left[\norm{R^{\mathsf s}}_{1,\log;\alpha}^p\right]\le C^p\mathbb E\left[\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}^p\right]
\end{equation}
and $\hdd R^{\mathsf s}=H$ almost surely.  Again, for individual defects one may replace $\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}$ by $\norm{H}_{1,\alpha}+C_H(\omega)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.782770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8b70b52b137c20014fde7e5bd973877be79f4db3a0070f75699e18e14eeb856","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:initialization-coupling of arXiv:2605.08052, 'Rapid phase ordering of Ising dynamics on $\mathbb Z^2$'] [Time-zero monotone coupling] Assign each vertex in $\mathbb Z^d$ at time $0$ two $\text{Unif}[0,1]$ random variables $(\mathcal U_{v,0}^\pi)_{v\in \mathbb Z^d}$ and $(\mathcal U^Q_{v,0})_{v\in \mathbb Z^d}$ independently. Start with an arbitrary enumeration $v_1,v_2,...$ of the vertices of $\mathbb Z^d$. 

    For any distribution $\pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta}$ where $\Lambda \subset \mathbb Z^d$ finite, and $\eta \in \{\pm 1\}^{\Lambda^c}$ is a boundary condition, draw a sample $\sigma \sim \pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta}$  and $Q\sim \bigotimes_{v\in \Lambda} \text{Rad}(p)$ as follows: iteratively, for $i\ge 1$, 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item let $\sigma_{v_i}$ be $+1$ if $$\mathcal U_{v_i,0}^\pi \le \pi_{\Lambda}^{\eta} ( \sigma_{v_i} =+1 \mid (\sigma_{v_j})_{j<i:v_j\in \Lambda})$$
        and $-1$ else. 
        \item let $Q_{v_i}$ be $+1$ if $\mathcal U_{v_i,0}^Q \le p$ and $-1$ else. . 
    \end{itemize}
    Once all vertices in $\Lambda$ have been processed, terminate.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.022168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8c8b98116336daa5c3ed584da27f9b4b69649f303dee7e508ab573d98980834","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2606.06193, 'Peng's Maximum Principle for McKean-Vlasov Stochastic Differential Equations with Common N'] Let $\varphi \in C_b^{2,1}(\R^d\times \mathcal{P}_2(\mathbb{R}^d),\R)$. Then, for any given $x\in\R^d$ and $X \in L^2(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^d)$ we have the following second-order expansion, for all $y\in\R^d$ and $Y\in L^2(\Omega,\R^d)$,
        \begin{equation*}
            \begin{aligned}
                &\varphi(y,\cL(Y))- \varphi(x,\cL(X)) \\
                = & \varphi_x(x,\cL(X))[y-x]
                +\E\left[\varphi_\mu(x,\cL(X))(X)[Y-X]\right]
                +\frac{1}{2}\varphi_{xx}(x,\cL(X))[y-x,y-x]\\
                &+\frac{1}{2} \E\left[\tilde{\E}\left[ \varphi_{\mu\mu}(x,\cL(X))(\tilde{X}, X)[ \tilde{Y}-\tilde{X},Y-X]\right]
                +\varphi_{y\mu}(x,\cL(X))(X)[Y-X,Y-X]\right]\\
                &+\E\left[\varphi_{x\mu}(x,\cL(X))(X)[Y-X,y-x]\right]+R(x,y,\cL(X), \cL(Y)).
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation*}
        Furthermore, for all $x,y\in \R^d$ and $X,Y \in L^2(\Omega, \mathbb{R}^d)$, the remainder satisfies the estimate
        \begin{equation*}
            \begin{aligned}
                |R(x,y,\cL(X), \cL(Y))| \leq C\left( |y-x|^3

                + \E[|Y-X|^3]\right),
            \end{aligned}
        \end{equation*}
        and the constant $C \in \mathbb{R}_{+}$ only depends on the Lipschitz constants of $\varphi_{xx}$, $\varphi_{x\mu}$, $\varphi_{\mu\mu}$ and $\varphi_{y\mu}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.356741+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8cb417ff8089f28fe4706fe11af66494df6901f3a60acc86887470309d5c090","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2506.18558, '2506.18558'] Suppose that Assumption {\rm\ref{A2}} holds. Then the following statements hold.
