{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.332067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8ebedffa877bb53bfa3cc2f683a119640ef25a19e97c87d75fcfb6b7450e39b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:wp:entropyEstimates of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda_0 \in \mathcal{P}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m)$. Suppose $b^1, b^2:[0, T] \times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m$ are measurable and satisfy a linear growth condition, that is there exists $C \in (0, \infty)$ such that
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{(t, x) \in [0, T] \times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d} |b^i(t, x)| \leq C(1 + |x|), \quad i = 1, 2.
\end{equation*}
For $i = 1, 2$, suppose $(\Omega^i, \mathcal{F}^i, \mathbb{F}^i, \mathbb{P}^i)$ is a filtered probability space supporting an $m$-dimensional Brownian motion $B^i$ and a continuous $m$-dimensional $\mathbb{F}^i$-adapted process $Z^i$ satisfying
\begin{equation*}
    dZ^i(t) = b^i(t, Z^i(t)) dt + \sqrt{2}dB^i(t),
\end{equation*}
with $\mathcal{L}(Z^1(0)) = \mathcal{L}(Z^2(0)) = \lambda_0$. Then the following identity holds: 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{H} \big( \mathcal{L}(Z^1[T])\,|\, \mathcal{L}(Z^2[T]) \big) = \frac{1}{2} \mathbb{E}^{\mathbb{P}^1} \Bigg[ \int_0^T |b^1(t, Z^1(t)) - b^2(t, Z^1(t))|^2 dt\Bigg]. 
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.778074+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8eccd393907807b1c1a975aa9d5f2d2da7af10017c992b33efc5c9da4e1b8e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2608.31171, '\bf Finite-energy spacetimes with torus symmetry. \\ \Large Cauchy stability of Einstein--'] The \textbf{particle-production Einstein--Navier--Stokes model} associated with a Navier--Stokes triple $(\mathscr F,\chi,\mathscr B)$ is the Einstein--matter system
\bel{eq:pot-production-system}
G^{\alpha\beta}=T_\chi^{\alpha\beta},
\qquad
\nabla_\alpha T_\chi^{\alpha\beta}=0,
\qquad
\nabla_\alpha A_\chi^{\alpha\beta\gamma}
=-\mathscr B^{\beta\gamma}.
\ee
The unknowns are the spacetime metric and the fields $(\beta,\zeta)$, representing the rescaled matter momentum and the relaxing field, respectively. The tensors $T_\chi$ and $A_\chi$ are defined from the potential according to~\eqref{eq:pot-currents}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.257243+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8ef97fdb7f2d98c254881f33c6308c84ab091e888914a8096112e2118fb34e7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 111 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Define $\widetilde{U}_j$ by shifting $\mathcal{U}_{a,b,c}^{j/N}$ (vertically) down by $\mathfrak{d}^{2/3} j_{\bar{z}}^{-1/3} N^{\delta/2-2/3}$ and (horizontally) to the right by $\mathfrak{d}^{-1/3} j_{\bar{z}}^{-1/3} N^{-2/3-\delta}$. Specifically, for $j\le k$ and $x\in[x^-,x^+]$, set 
	\begin{flalign*} 
		\widetilde U_j(x) = \mathcal{U}_{a,b,c}^{j/N} (x - \mathfrak{d}^{-1/3} j_{\bar{z}}^{-1/3} N^{-2/3-\delta}) - \mathfrak{d}^{2/3} j_{\bar{z}}^{-1/3} N^{\delta/2-2/3}.
	\end{flalign*} 

	\noindent Furthermore, for each $t \in [0, N^5]$, let $\mathtt{U}_t^{j/N} (x)$ denote the $jN^{-1}$-level line (as in Definition \ref{ttU}, recalling also \Cref{ftn:minorabuse}) of $H_t$, and set $\mathtt{y}_t^{\pm,j} = \mathtt{U}_t^{j/N} (x^{\pm})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.953094+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d8f1431dcde23fe61e61c5c9335af6482ebd7b21400ce035926bc2dabb0c39c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 18 ({\cite[Theorem~13) of arXiv:2607.21759, 'Marchenko--Pastur law for tensor powers of exchangeable unconditional vectors'] {Diaconis_Freedman_1980}}] 
    Let $\nu$ be an exchangeable distribution on $\R^n$. Then there exists a probability distribution $w$ on $\mathcal P(\R)$ such that for any $k \leq n$,\[
