{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.304295+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d90709fb6e670e47706c1075b77a5c11e71ffdc1deb6cd75aaf0ecdf73434dda","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 52 (Backwards-time propagation of test functions in $d=2$) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d=2$. Assume $\x_N=(x^1_N,x^2_N)$ is any sequence such that $N^{-1/2}x_N^1 \to x_1$ and $x^1_N-x^2_N$ remains bounded, and consider the same setting as Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}. 
      Consider smooth bounded functions $\phi_1, ..., \phi_m : \mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R$ with globally bounded and continuous first and second partial derivatives, and consider $f^1,..., f^m : I\to \mathbb R$ of exponential decay at infinity. Then \begin{equation} 
    \lim_{N\to\infty} \Ebbnn \bigg[ \frac1{(\log N)^m}\sum_{0\leq r_1 < \ldots < r_m \leq Nt} \prod_{\ell =1}^m f^{\ell}(   R^1_{r_{\ell}}-   R^2_{r_{\ell}})\phi_{\ell}(N^{-1/2} (R^1_{r_\ell}-   s_N r_\ell))\bigg] = \prod_{\ell = 1}^m \bigg( \phi_\ell (x_1) \int_I f^\ell \; \dr \pi^{\mathrm{inv}} \bigg)\end{equation}
    The claim remains true if we replace $(f^1,...,f^m)$ on the left side by some sequence $(f^1_N,...,f^m_N)$ converging uniformly on compact sets as $N\to\infty$ to some $(f^1,...,f^m)$, as long as $\sup_{N} |f^j_N|$ decays exponentially fast at infinity for $j=1,...,m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:06.325639+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d9188b48ee9323390ceb5f9da2ecbd664eab465d3c21323bd20c57fc6cf9671d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 5 of arXiv:2607.06242, 'Exponential mixing and Freidlin--Wentzell large deviation principle for Markov cocycles'] Under assumptions $(H2)$--$(H4)$, there exist constants $\a_0, v, \g_{*}>0$ depending only on $\varpi,b_1,\zeta,\k$ such that for every $\a\in(0,\a_0]$ and the premetric 
      \begin{align}
        \theta_\alpha(x, y):=p(x, y)^\alpha e^{\alpha v U(x)}, \quad x,y\in E,
      \end{align}
      the following conclusions hold:
      \begin{enumerate}
        \item[(1)] There exists $C_{\a}\geq 1$, such that for any $x,y\in E$,
        \[ \mathbf{E} \theta_\alpha\left(X_t^\sigma, Y_t^\sigma\right)\leq A_t(\s)\theta_\alpha(x, y),\]
        and 
      \[d_{TV}\left(\mathcal{L}(Y_t^{\s}), P_{t}^{\s}(y,\cdot)\right)\leq B_t(\s)\theta_\alpha(x, y),\]
        where 
        \begin{align}
          A_t(\s) = C_{\a}e^{-\a\chi_{t}(\s)}, \quad B_{t}(\s) = C_{\a}\left(\int_0^{t}b_3(\beta_s\s)e^{-\chi_s(\s)}ds\right)^{\a}
        \end{align}
        with 
        \[\chi_t(\sigma) = \zeta t- \frac{\k}{\varpi-\g_{*} b_1}\int_0^t\left(b(\beta_s\s)+\g_{*} b_2(\beta_s\s)\right)ds.\]
        \item[(2)] For any $B\subset E$ such that $U$ and $p$ are bounded on $B$ and $B\times B$, and any $t\geq0$, there is  $C_{\a,B}>0$ such that for any $x,y\in B$, one has 
        \[d_{TV}\left(\mathcal{L}(Y_t^{\s}), P_{t}^{\s}(y,\cdot)\right)\leq 1-\bar{\varepsilon}_{\a}(C_{\a,B}B_{t}(\s)),\]
        and 
        \[\mathbf{E} \theta_\alpha\left(X_t^\sigma, Y_t^\sigma\right) \leq C_{\alpha,B} A_{t}(\s),\]
        where $\bar{\varepsilon}_{\a}$ is the function from $(H4)$. 
      \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:48:35.821947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d91d53cd737f3b8b699b6d55d6beb242eca1a8ddb161694be9e3b926765b7665","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary lower phase transition of arXiv:2411.09614, 'Moment estimates for the stochastic heat equation on Cartan-Hadamard manifolds'] Suppose $\alpha>\frac{n-2}{4}$, $x\in M$, $R>0$ and $u_0\in L^\infty(M)$  satisfies $u_0\geq \varepsilon>0$ in some $B(x,R)$. If $u(t,x)$ solves (equation eq:PAM-mild form of the paper) starting from $u_0$, then for any $p\geq 2$, there exists $\beta_c(p)>0$ depending on $K_2$ such that $\beta>\beta_c(p)$ implies \begin{equation}
    \lim_{t\to+\infty}\frac{\ln\mathbb{E}[\abs{u(t,x)}^p]}{t}>0.
