{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.329947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de6ec7325cdc9be15196f2312207668ef1db250cf79c49ea1dcfe5a60ca341c8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition qshprod of arXiv:2411.06827, 'The Exponential Lie Series and a Chen--Strichartz Formula for L{\'e}vy Processes'] The commutative quasi-shuffle product $\star$ on ${\mathbb R}{\langle}{\mathbb A}{\rangle}$  is  inductively defined by
\begin{align}

\begin{aligned}
	u\,& \star\, {\mathbf 1}
	= {\mathbf 1}\,\star\, u
	=u, \\
	ua \star vb & 
	=(u \star vb)a + (ua \star v)b + (u \star v)[a,b],
\end{aligned}
\end{align}
where $u$ and $v$ are words and $a$ and $b$ are letters, and where
 `$ {\mathbf 1}$' is the empty word.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751819+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de790121f6778ac4964e66b6afc1ca1e33fa731e65dce0854e84bc9d5e92f6d1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:well_posed_lin of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$. We say that SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ enjoys
\begin{itemize}
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{weak} existence}, if there exists a solution to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\nu})$ for any $\nu\in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$;
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{weak} uniqueness}, if, for any $\nu\in \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, two solutions to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\nu})$ have the same law;
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{pathwise} existence}, if there exists a solution to SDE$(b,\sigma;s,\textcolor{black}{\xi, W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t})$ for any $(W, (\mathcal{F}_t)_t )$ Brownian motion  and any \textcolor{black}{$\xi\in m\mathcal{F}_s$};
\item[-] \emph{\textcolor{black}{pathwise} uniqueness}, if, for any pair of general solutions $(( \mathcal{F}_t)_{t},W, X)$, $( (\mathcal{F}_t)_{t},W, X')$ to SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ with $X_s = X'_s$ almost surely, $X = X'$ almost surely.
\end{itemize} 

We say that SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed if weak (or pathwise) existence and uniqueness hold.

Finally, we say that SDE$(b,\sigma)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed if SDE$(b,\sigma;s)$ is weakly (or pathwise) well-posed for any $s\in [0,T)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.405785+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de82231fe69e3b4d553f65efe9859ceb2ce5bf890051b7593d83f8ab4c71d9ad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2604.06516, 'From stochastic individual-based models to free-boundary Hamilton-Jacobi equations'] For all $t>0$ and $x\in \R$, the family of laws $(\mu^K_{x,t})_{K\geq 1}$ satisfies a large deviation principle on $\mathbb{D}[0,t]$ with rate $1/\log K$ and good rate function $I_{t,x}$: for any subset $A\subset \mathbb{D}[0,t]$,
\begin{multline}
    -\inf_{f\in \mathrm{int}(A)} I_{t,x}(f)\leq \liminf_{K\rightarrow +\infty} \frac{1}{\log K} \mathbb{P}_x\big((X^K_{s\log K})_{s\leq t} \in A\big)\\
    \leq \limsup_{K\rightarrow +\infty} \frac{1}{\log K} \mathbb{P}_x\big((X^K_{s\log K})_{s\leq t} \in A\big) \leq -\inf_{f\in \mathrm{cl}(A)} I_{t,x}(f),
\end{multline}where $\mathrm{int}(A)$ and $\mathrm{cl}(A)$ are the interior and closure of $A$ for the Skorohod topology. \\
    In addition, the family of measures $(\mu^K_{x,t})_{K\geq 1}$ satisfies the Laplace principle with rate $1/\log K$ and rate function $I_{t,x}$ uniformly on compact sets with respect to $x\in\R$, as recalled below. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:58:40.874408+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_de85a0e48761bd1eecd15d787342eddbd953d5083fcec69511975c582a0046e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition deftheta of arXiv:2605.10617, 'Logarithmic scaling of selective sweep curves:\\ from tents to houses'] We put
  \begin{equation}
    \vartheta_N
     := \inf\{t\ge 0 \mid Y_0^N(t) \le \log_N\lfloor\tfrac{\log N}{\varphi_N}\rfloor\}
  \end{equation}
  and
  $$
    y_0^N(t) := 
    \begin{cases}
      \log_N^{} \lfloor \tfrac {N}{\sqrt{\log N}}\rfloor  -at &\mbox{for } 0\le t< \vartheta_N,\\
      \widetilde Y_0^N(t) & \mbox{for } \vartheta_N \le t < t^{(a,b)}+\varepsilon.
    \end{cases}
