{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.273309+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2c32a5c157eb91f97fb14228c0abf8fe3d5817a50af8172af15ddca665c7936","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mydef b2.1 of arXiv:2411.08430, 'The Restricted Isometry Property of Block Diagonal Matrices Generated by $\varphi$-Sub-Gau'] A matrix \( \mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{C}^{M \times D} \) is said to have the group restricted isometry property (group-RIP) of order \( s \) if there exists a constant \( \delta \in (0, 1) \) such that, for all $\mathbf{x} \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{S}, s}$, the following inequality holds:

	\begin{equation}
		(1-\delta)\|\mathbf{x}\|_2^2 \leq\|\mathbf{B} \mathbf{x}\|_2^2 \leq(1+\delta)\|\mathbf{x}\|_2^2 \quad \forall \mathbf{x} \in \Sigma_{\mathcal{S}, s}
	\end{equation}

The smallest constant \( \delta_s \leq \delta \) satisfying this inequality is referred to as the group restricted isometry constant (group-RIC) of $\mathbf{B}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.112071+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2c4549ae2ce02ef8d52a717d68a3ad19ffc2b9c569d3a176cffd2ee2dec8bc8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary thm:complete-linear-estimates of arXiv:2608.16119, 'Fully nonlinear parabolic equations under a fixed reference diffusion: weighted \texorpdfs'] Let $\alpha\geq0$ and let $w$ be a regular weight of order $\alpha$.
Suppose that Assumption~(ass:reference-diffusion of the paper) holds and $C_w(X)<\infty$, and take
$F\in L_\alpha^2$ and $\psi\in H^1(\mu_T)$.  If $\psi$ is not
spatially constant, also assume Assumption~(ass:drift-multiplier of the paper).  Then
$u:=\mathcal U(F,\psi)=SF+v^\psi$ is the unique order-$\alpha$ occupation Sobolev solution with data
$(F,\psi)$.  Its Hessian is
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Hess}_gu=\mathsf T_wF+\operatorname{Hess}_gv^\psi, \qquad
 D_x^2u=D_x^2SF+D_x^2v^\psi.

\end{equation}
There exists a constant
$C=C(T,\lambda,\Lambda,M_1(a),\operatorname{osc}_\alpha(w),
C_w(X),\Lambda_b)>0$ such that, for every $\kappa\geq1$,
\begin{align}
 \left\lVert u\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
 &\leq e^{\operatorname{osc}_\alpha(w)/2}\kappa^{-1}
       \left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
       +CT^{\alpha/2}\norm\psi_{H^1(\mu_T)},\notag\\
 \left\lVert \mathsf Z u\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
 &\leq e^{\operatorname{osc}_\alpha(w)/2}\kappa^{-1/2}
       \left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
       +CT^{\alpha/2}\norm\psi_{H^1(\mu_T)},\notag\\
 \left\lVert \mathsf G u\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
 &\leq C_{w_\kappa}(X)\left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
 +\frac32\Lambda^2\lambda^{-5/2}M_1(a)
        \kappa^{-1/2}
\times e^{\operatorname{osc}_\alpha(w)/2}
        \left\lVert F\right\rVert_{w_\kappa}
 +CT^{\alpha/2}\norm\psi_{H^1(\mu_T)}.

\end{align}
The corresponding canonical estimate is
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{j=0}^2\left\lVert D_x^ju\right\rVert_\alpha
 \leq C\left(\left\lVert F\right\rVert_\alpha
       +T^{\alpha/2}\norm\psi_{H^1(\mu_T)}\right).

\end{equation}
If $\psi$ is spatially constant, $C$ may be chosen without dependence on
$\Lambda_b$.
For every $s\in(0,T)$, its composition admits a continuous version on
$[s,T]$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 u(t,X_t)=\mathbb E_t\left[\psi(X_T)+\int_t^TF(r,X_r)\,\mathrm d r\right],
 \qquad s\leq t\leq T.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e2ca1384bf052b60305f773b727a70e34cb29a3baa1445a45f29ff031bef6375","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] We say the strongly measurable function $ f(\cdot) : \Omega \rightarrow \mathcal{X} $ is Bochner integrable with respect to $ \mu $ if there exists a sequence of Bochner integrable simple  functions $ \{ f_{i}(\cdot) \}_{i=1}^{\infty}    $ converging to $ f(\cdot) $ in $ X $, $ \mu $-a.e., such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim _{i, j \rightarrow \infty} \int_{\Omega}\left|f_i(s)-f_j(s)\right|_{\mathcal{X}} d \mu=0
        .
    \end{align*}
    For  $ E \in \mathcal{F} $, the Bochner integral of $ f(\cdot) $ over $ E $ is defined by 
    \begin{align*}
        \int_E f(s) d \mu=\lim _{i \rightarrow \infty} \int_{\Omega} \chi_E(s) f_i(s) d \mu(s) \quad \text { in } \mathcal{X} \text {. }
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

