{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:38:37.415296+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e3735676d846be10e6c35cd1422ea13e749f3ffb230755280984c6fe9b289517","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2605.15931, '2605.15931'] The sequence of processes $(n^{1/2}(X_{\tilde{\tau}^n(X)\wedge.}-x))_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ converges in the sense of finite-dimensional distributions over $\{t_1,t_2,...,t_m\}\subset (0,\infty)$. In particular, for any $\{t_1,t_2,...,t_m\}\subset (0,\infty)$ and $\varphi:\mathbb{R}^{m\times d}\to \mathbb{R}$ continuous and bounded we have
		$$
		\begin{aligned}
		&|\mathbf{E}[\varphi(n^{1/2}(X_{\tilde{\tau}^n(X)\wedge t_1}-x),...,n^{1/2}(X_{\tilde{\tau}^n(X)\wedge t_m}-x))]\\
		&-\mathbf{E}[\varphi(\sigma(x)W_{\tau^1(\sigma(x)W)},...,\sigma(x)W_{\tau^1(\sigma(x)W)})]|\,{{\to}}\,0
		\end{aligned}
		$$
		{as $n\to \infty$.}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.422545+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e376c76203383df0d7db043a0c57371c26d3a0ec30045174c71d4200439feeef","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:Z-def of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[definition]{def:regularized-Z}
For a Borel measurable function \(V\) bounded below,
\(f\in\mathcal F_m\) or \(f=\ell_\eta\) with fixed \(\eta>0\), and
\(\varepsilon>0\), define
\begin{align}
 \mathcal Z_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)
 :=\omega_N\int_{\mathcal O_{N,L}}
 &\exp\biggl\{-NV\bigl(h_J(O)\bigr)
 +\sum_{a=1}^n\log\kappa_\varepsilon\bigl(g_{a,J}(O)\bigr)\notag\\
 &\hspace{36mm}+\operatorname{Tr} f\bigl(A_J(O)\bigr)\biggr\}\,\nu_N(\,\mathrm d O).

\end{align}
Its annealed pressure is
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f)
 :=\frac1N\log\mathbb E\mathcal Z_{N,L}^J(V,\varepsilon,f).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.368669+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e3778a280e5f9707b6261d4924e4f22a5278e728c8d5ede2e9258ddb74b878b2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:LSI:zergalinski of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda$ be a 1-MRF on $\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbb{T}_2^n}$. Recall the notation $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ defined in Definition (def:results:Tn2 of the paper). For $v \in \mathbb{T}_2^n$ and $\mathbf{x}^{(n)} \in (\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)^{V_n}$, let $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_v := \{x_u\}_{u \neq v} \in (\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)^{2n}$. Denote the corresponding family of specifications (i.e. marginal conditional measures) of $\lambda$ by $ \lambda_v(x_v | \hat{\mathbf{x}}_v)$. 

     Suppose the following two properties hold.
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Expressed in terms of the constant $C_{LS}$ from Definition (def:LSI of the paper), the specifications $\{\lambda_v\}_{v \in \mathbb{T}^n_2}$ satisfy the uniform log-Sobolev inequality,
        \begin{align*}
            \tilde{C}_{LS, n} = \sup_{v \in \mathbb{T}^n_2, \, \hat{\mathbf{x}}_v \in (\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)^{2n}} C_{LS}\Big(\lambda_v\big(\cdot \big| \hat{\mathbf{x}}_v\big)\Big) < \infty.
        \end{align*}
        \item There exist constants $c^n_{uv}(\lambda) \geq 0$ for every $u, v \in \mathbb{T}_2^n$, such that every smooth strictly positive function $f: \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^{\mathbb{T}_2^n} \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ satisfies the following for all $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_v \in (\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)^{2n}$ :
        \begin{equation}

            \bigg | \nabla_{x_u} \sqrt{ \mathbb E^{\lambda_v}[f^2|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_v]}\bigg| \leq \sqrt{\mathbb E^{\lambda_v}[|\nabla_{x_u}f|^2|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_v]} + c^n_{uv}(\lambda) \sqrt{\mathbb E^{\lambda_v}[|\nabla_{x_v}f|^2|\hat{\mathbf{x}}_v]},
        \end{equation}
        where there exists $\delta \in (0, 1)$ such that
        \begin{equation}
            \sup_{u \in \mathbb{T}_2^n} \max\bigg\{ \sum_{v \in \mathbb{T}_2^n} c^n_{uv}(\lambda), \sum_{v \in \mathbb{T}_2^n} c^n_{vu}(\lambda)\bigg\} \leq \delta < 1.

        \end{equation}
    \end{enumerate} 
    Then $\lambda$ satisfies an LSI with constant
        \begin{align*}
            C_{LS}(\lambda) \leq \frac{\tilde{C}_{LS, n}}{(1 - \delta)^2}.
        \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.561272+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e37b83cbf6c6ad327a54073cdd73dd7ab8d1768107e4c26c0e8de66f4382d8f2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2605.30560, 'Projected McKean--Vlasov Dynamics for Entropic Weak Optimal Transport'] Suppose $\mathcal{Y}$ is a Polish space. For $\rho,\sigma\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y})$, the \emph{relative entropy} is defined as 
\[
H(\rho\,\|\,\sigma)
\;:=\;
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\int \log\frac{d\rho}{d\sigma}\,d\rho & \text{if } \rho\ll\sigma,\\[4pt]
+\infty & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.212986+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38c79dee049eafad806e9b8220723734cd704699ee6a5389de1b55cf6b60e7f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 4 of arXiv:2608.03956, 'From Triangular Array Progression\\ to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions'] A sequence $[\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_{n+1}]$ for $n\geq 2$ is a \emph{near}-log-concave sequence of degree $r\in \Z_+$ for finite $0 \leq r \leq n-2$, if the following holds:
\begin{align}
\eta_{i-1}\eta_{i+1} \leq \eta^2_{i} \hspace{0.15in} \text{$\forall i\in\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)}$}, \notag
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)} \subseteq \mathcal{I}_{n} = \{2,\ldots,n\}$ such that 
\begin{align}
|\mathcal{I}_{n}^{(r)}| = n - r - 1.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:25:06.201045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38eca02684bc8691906bfc53b0d05f5a2e89c5f883fd26578cc5e53394a89f4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 22 (Error recursion bounds) of arXiv:2608.22636, 'Q-Learning with Stable Infinite-Dimensional \\Linear Function Approximation'] For $\alpha>1$ and integers $n_0,m_0>0$ such that
$m_0+n_0\ge16\alpha$, define
$$\alpha_k=\frac{\alpha}{k+n_0},\quad     \tau(j,m)=\sum_{k=j}^{m-1}\alpha_{k+1},\quad\text{and}\quad m_{r+1}:=\max\cW(m_r,T),\; r\ge 0$$ 
where $\cW(\cdot,T)$ is the window defined in
Lemma~\ref{lemma:step size_window}. We will consider $T=1/4$ or $T = 1/5$. 

Let $g > 1/\alpha$ be a fixed constant and set $\lambda:=\alpha g$. In addition, when $T=1/4$, assume $g\le1$; when $T=1/5$, assume $g\le4/5$. 

Suppose that for every $r\ge0$ and every $m\in\cW(m_r,T)$ the nonnegative sequence
$\set{\Delta_n:n\ge m_0}$ satisfies, 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
        \Delta_m
        &\le
        e^{-g\tau(m_r,m)}\Delta_{m_r}
        +
        K_1\sqrt{\frac{H_{m_r}}{m_r+n_0}}
        +
        \frac{K_2}{m_r+n_0}
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
for some non-negative and non-decreasing sequence $\set{H_n:n\ge m_0}$ and $K_1,K_2\ge0$. 
Then, for every $n\ge m_0$,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
        \Delta_n
        \le e^{1/16}\Delta_{m_0}
        \crbk{\frac{m_0+n_0}{n+n_0}}^\lambda +
        \frac{14\alpha  K_1}{\lambda-1/2}
        \sqrt{\frac{H_n}{n+n_0}}+
       \frac{17\alpha  K_2}{\lambda-1}
        \frac{1}{n+n_0}.
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.922119+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e38f5a883986688cd8420dfef13cb332affa85e0046718e706ce593bdecee0bf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T},$ and let $a \in [0,1].$ Assume that $f$ is bounded away from zero and infinity. Then $(\lambda_{t})_{0 \le t \le aT}$ defined in \eqref{eq:minimizer}, is the minimizing process for $T_{a}(X)$ with respect to the Poisson measure $\pi_{aT}.$ Therefore,
    \begin{displaymath}
        H(T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT} ) = \E \int_{0}^{aT} \phi (\lambda_{t} ) \dd t.
    \end{displaymath}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.292483+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e39bd705cb65e655148ee695d07f8a96b7880608e5ab9c92816feb67b70c56b0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:C_reg of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Let $\Omega$ be defined as in Theorem~(theo:main of the paper). Fix (small) $c_0,\varepsilon>0$. For any $f_1,f_2\in H_0^{2}(\Omega)$ and $x_j,x_j'\in [c_0,1]$, $j=1,2$, it holds that
