{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.745613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e65cc6bc1a113afcb9bae3d531fc257731716ddfd95171de5e1b308b7ff52bc1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 (Critical rescaled power variation) of arXiv:2606.11994, 'Sample Path Properties of the\\ Fractional Wiener--Weierstrass Bridge II'] Assume $H=K$.  Then, $\Pp$-a.s.~for every $t\in[0,1]$,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\uparrow\infty}
\frac1{n^{1/(2H)}}
\sum_{k=0}^{\floor{tb^n}-1}
\abs{Y((k+1)b^{-n})-Y(kb^{-n})}^{1/H}
=
\frac{2^{1/(2H)}\Gamma\big(\frac{H+1}{2H}\big)}{\sqrt\pi}\,t.
\end{equation}
In particular, almost all sample paths of $Y$ have infinite $b$-adic $(1/H)^{\text{th}}$ variation:
\begin{equation}
 V_n^{(1/H)}(Y,t)  \longrightarrow+\infty\qquad\text{for every $t\in(0,1]$ $\Pp$-a.s.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:18:19.440318+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e66242bc6838efec779b8bd2dd54982ad6fba760295b3711cedf8bbb514f6229","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:T=Gamma of arXiv:2508.17838, '\bf Edge Universality for Inhomogeneous Random Matrices'] With the same assumption and notation as in Proposition (prop:ribbon of the paper), 
let \begin{equation}
    b'_{n}=\begin{cases}
        -\frac{1}{2},&n=0,\\
        1,&n=2,\\
        0,&\text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
\end{equation} then  for any  non-negative integers $n_1,\dots ,n_s$ we have 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{E}\big[\prod_{j=1}^s \mathrm{Tr} (\widetilde{T}_{n_j}(X)+b'_{n_j}\mathbf{I})\big]=\sum_{\Gamma\in \mathscr{D}_{s;\beta}} \mathfrak{F}_{\Gamma}(\{n_j\}).
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.074799+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e66dbaec733926c3e790a1d0c1fd0cc5d534ad07c6bfcc3aba197debb716046f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2606.02036, 'Self-intersection local times for Volterra Gaussian processes in stochastic flows with int'] Let $\{u(t) \equiv (u_1(t),\ldots,u_d(t)):  t\in[0,1]\}$ be a centred Gaussian process in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with indepdendent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) components and $\rho:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R}$ be a weight function satisfying \eqref{eq:condition_on_weight_function}. Let $p\ge 2$.  If
 \begin{align}

 \int_{\Delta^p_k}\frac{1}{G(u_1(t_1),\ldots,u_1(t_{pk}))^{\frac{d}{2}}}\differential{t_1}\cdots\differential{t_{pk}}<\infty,
\end{align}
then there exists a random variable $T^{u}_k(\rho)$ such that 
the family  
$\{T^u_{\varepsilon,k}(\rho) : \ve >0 \}$ converges to $T^{u}_k(\rho)$ in $L^p(\Omega,\cF,\prob)$ as $\ve\to0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.668329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e67d6211542d5022d0f7fc0d50a33ddef0cb9349ce3bb4b5ed756962b1a7b69a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 (Almost Covering Maps) of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] A continuous proper surjection $ f: X \longrightarrow Y$ between path-connected, locally compact, Hausdorff spaces $X$ and $Y$ is called an \textbf{almost covering map}
    	if 
    	\begin{enumerate}
    	\item there exists a nowhere dense closed set $R \subset Y$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(Y \setminus R)} : f^{-1}(Y \setminus R) \longrightarrow
    	Y \setminus R$ is a finite covering map, and
    	\item there is a finite decomposition $R=\bigsqcup_{\beta}T_{\beta}$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(T_{\beta})}:f^{-1}(T_{\beta}) \longrightarrow T_{\beta}$ is a fiber bundle for each $\beta$.
        \end{enumerate}
        The set $Y \setminus R$ is called the regular set of the almost covering map $f$, and the set $R$ is referred to as a fibered set of the map $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.116101+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e697e6349af0e5dd913cd7da1a06be9d0ac3f34d02adc8b515a5feb84d322170","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor 135 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Let $\md,\me\lesssim1$ be positive numbers. There exists a constant
    $C>1$ such that the following holds. 
	Fix $(x', y') = (x', \gamma_{x'})$ on the arctic boundary $\mathfrak A_{q;a,b,c}$ with $x' \in [1/4, 5/4]$, and denote $f = f_{x'} (\gamma_{x'})$; $\gamma = \gamma_{x'}$; $\gamma' = \gamma_{x'}'$; and $\gamma'' = \gamma_{x'}''$. 
	 Let $z=(x,y)$ with 
	\begin{flalign}

		x = x' + (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2} \hat{x} - \gamma' \mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e} \hat{y}; \qquad y = \gamma_{x'} + \gamma' (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2} \hat{x} + \mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e} \hat{y}
	\end{flalign}
        and assume that
        \begin{equation}

       \frac{ \md}{|\gamma'|},\frac{\me_{z;q}}{\me},\frac{\md_{z}}\md\in   [C^{-1},C].
        \end{equation}

	\noindent Denoting  
	\begin{flalign}

		\hat{f}(\hat{x}, \hat{y}) = (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{-1/2} ( f(x,y) - f); \qquad \Gamma =  \gamma''- \gamma'(1 - \gamma') \log \mathfrak{q}, 
	\end{flalign}
	\noindent we have 
	\begin{flalign}

		\hat{f} (\hat{x}, \hat{y}) =  \displaystyle\frac{\mathrm{i} \Gamma^{1/2}}{(\gamma'-1)^2}   \big( 2 (\gamma'^2 + 1) \hat{y} - \gamma'' \hat{x}^2 \big)^{1/2}  - \displaystyle\frac{\gamma'' \hat{x}}{(\gamma'-1)^2} + \mathcal{O} ( (\mathfrak{d} \mathfrak{e})^{1/2}).
	\end{flalign} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:40:59.361580+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e69d75508d1a45fb7e5e1993fafd299a7904c97073f87a3240c9d4ee89edf70c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 (Global reciprocal bounds) of arXiv:2609.03958, '2609.03958'] Let $n\geq 2$ and $K\geq 0$. Suppose that $\lambda\in \Gamma_2$ satisfies $\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_n\geq -K$ and $\sigma_2(\lambda)=f$ with $f_0\leq f\leq f_1$ for two constants $f_1\geq f_0>0$.  Then there exists  $C >0$ depending only on $n$, $K$, $f_0$ and $f_1$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sigma_{1;i}(\lambda)}\leq C(\sigma_1(\lambda)+1),
 \qquad
 \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{1}{\sigma_1^2(\lambda)\sigma_{1;i}(\lambda)}\leq C.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.669122+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e69e5d9dae95fbd2444420c164e8ce8587697ce8f5208b2fca2829aa4f896b46","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] We define a \textbf{topologically stratified space} inductively on dimension. A 0-dimensional topologically stratified space $X$ is a countable set with the discrete topology. For $m > 0$ an \textbf{$m$-dimensional topologically stratified space} is a paracompact Hausdorff topological space $X$ equipped with a filtration
	\begin{align*}
		X=X_{m} \supseteq X_{m-1} \supseteq \dots \supseteq X_{1} \supseteq X_{0} \supseteq X_{-1}= \emptyset
	\end{align*}
	by closed subsets $X_{j}$ such that if $x \in X_{j}-X_{j-1}$ there exists a neighborhood $\mathcal{N}_{x}$ of $x$ in $X$, a compact $(m-j-1)$-dimensional topologically stratified space $L$ with filtration
	\begin{align*}
		L=L_{m-j-1} \supseteq \dots \supseteq L_{1} \supseteq  L_{0} \supseteq L_{-1} = \emptyset,
	\end{align*}  
	and a homeomorphism $\phi : \mathcal{N}_{x} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L),$
	where $\mathcal{C}(L)$ is the open cone on $\mathcal{L}$, such that $\phi$ takes $\mathcal{N}_{x} \cap X_{j+i+1}$ homeomorphically onto
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L_{i}) \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{j} \times \mathcal{C}(L)
	\end{align*}
	for $m-j-1 \geq i \geq 0$, and $\phi$ takes $\mathcal{N}_{x} \cap X_{j}$ homeomorphically onto
	\begin{align*}
		\mathbb{R}^{j} \times \{ \text{vertex of }\; \mathcal{C}(L) \}.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:48:17.115981+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a3474211e65169cc501abb794344876c32549a94d46c80f19724efc320643e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 134 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] {Let $C>1$ be a constant.}
		Letting $x \in [1/4, 5/4]$, the following two statements hold. 

		\begin{enumerate} 
			\item Recalling $\Gamma_{x;2}$ from \eqref{gammax12}, we have for $\gamma_x \le y$ that
		\begin{flalign}

			f_x (y) = f_x (\gamma_x) + \mathrm{i} ( f_x (\gamma_x) - 1 )^2 \cdot ( 2 \Gamma_{x;2} (y - \gamma_x) )^{1/2} + (y-\gamma_x) \cdot \varsigma_1 ( x, (y-\gamma_x)^{1/2}),  
		\end{flalign}

		\noindent where $\varsigma_1: [1/4,5/4] \times \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \rightarrow \mathbb{C}$ is a function dependent on $(a,b,c,q)$ that satisfies the following. For any derivative $\partial_{\ell}$ in $(x,z,a,b,c,q)$, there exists a constant $C_{\ell} > 1$ such that $|\partial_{\ell} \varsigma_1 (x,z)| \le C_{\ell}$, for all $(x,z) \in [1/4,5/4] \times [0, C]$; $q \in [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon]$; and $a,b,c \in [1-\varepsilon, 1+ \varepsilon]$. 

		\item  The asymptotics can be refined close to $p^{SW}_{q;a,b,c}$. That is, assume that $\gamma_x \le y \le C \mathfrak{d}^2$ and denote
		\begin{flalign}
			D' & = f_0' (0) = \displaystyle\frac{\log \mathfrak{q}}{\mathfrak{q}^x - 1}; \\ 
			A' & = f_0'' (0) + \displaystyle\frac{\mathfrak{q}^{x} + 1}{(\mathfrak{q}^{x} - 1)^2} (\log \mathfrak{q})^2; \\
			B' & = \displaystyle\frac{1}{3} \cdot \Bigg( f_0''' (0) + \displaystyle\frac{2 (\mathfrak{q}^{2x} + \mathfrak{q}^{x}+1)}{(\mathfrak{q}^{x}-1)^3} (\log \mathfrak{q})^3 +  3 f_0'' (0)   \cdot \displaystyle\frac{ (\mathfrak{q}^{x} + 1)}{\mathfrak{q}^{x} - 1} \log \mathfrak{q} \Bigg).
		\end{flalign}
		Then, we have 
		\begin{flalign}
			\begin{aligned} 
                  f_x (y) - f_x (\gamma_x)  & = \mathrm{i}  (f_x (\gamma_x) - 1)^2 \cdot ( 2 \Gamma_{x;2}  (y-\gamma_x) )^{1/2} \\ 
                 	& \qquad + \mathfrak{q}^x A'^{-1} D' (A'^{-1} B' D' - f_0'' (0)) \cdot (y - \gamma_x) + \mathfrak{d}^3 \cdot \varsigma_2 ( x, (y-\gamma_x)^{1/2}), 

                 \end{aligned} 
		\end{flalign}

		\noindent where $\varsigma_2: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \rightarrow \mathbb{C}$ is a function dependent on $(a,b,c,q)$ that satisfies the following. For any derivative $\partial_{\ell}$ in $(x,z,a,b,c,q)$, there exists a constant $C_{\ell} > 1$ such that $|\partial_{\ell} \varsigma_2 (x, z)| \le C_{\ell}$, for all $(x,z) \in [1/4,5/4] \times [0, C \mathfrak{d}]$; $q \in [-\varepsilon, \varepsilon]$; and $a,b,c \in [1-\varepsilon, 1+ \varepsilon]$. 

	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:57:28.725598+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6a3ec9a3634caf9f650bce1347e5f1b56138b6ebe7509f49a73eb28820ccde4","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 25 of arXiv:2609.01190, 'Rough differential equations on manifolds via natural bundles'] Let $\theta\in (0,1)$. There exist $\delta\in (0,1)$ and $L\in (0,\infty)$ such that for every partition $\Pcal=\{S=U_0<U_1<\ldots<U_N=T\}$ (with $N\in\Nsf$, $N\geq 2$) satisfying \ref{ass:sewing-p} the estimate
	\[
		|Z_{s,t}|\leq L\omega_{s,t}^\frac{3}{p}
	\]
holds for every $(s,t)\in \Delta\llbracket 0,N \rrbracket$ satisfying $\omega_{s,t}<\delta$. Both constants $\delta$ and $L$ depend only on $\theta$, $m$, $\|f\|_2$, $\mathrm{supp}(f)$, $p$, $\omega(S,T)$, and $\|X\|$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.313946+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6abb5d80e693f72773193c2d4c335b7c2426df2f9fa2e96a91ae0a48374dc87","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 11 of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] For all $\sigma, \alpha > 0$,
\begin{align*}
    \sigma \le c^{\SE}(\sigma, \alpha) \le \sigma \vee 2\alpha,
\end{align*}
and the constant $\sigma \vee 2\alpha$ is itself admissible: every $X \in \SE(\sigma^2,\alpha)$ satisfies $X \lecx (\sigma \vee 2\alpha)\,\Lap$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.826240+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6aeeb1523d6fdfcb30cd2c14be50ccfc725adc6b2beb629b8dc49726050c49e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:popularity of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $h\geq2$.  There are explicit positive rational constants
$\kappa_2,\ldots,\kappa_h$ such that, as $q\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h-M_{h-r}}(q)=\kappa_rq^3+O_h(q^2)
 \qquad(2\leq r\leq h),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,M_h}(q)=\binom q4-
 \left(\sum_{r=2}^h\kappa_r\right)q^3+O_h(q^2).
\end{equation}
For every
\[
 n\in\mathcal R(h,4)\setminus
 \left(\{M_h\}\cup\{M_h-M_{h-r}:2\leq r\leq h\}\right)
\]
there is an explicit positive rational constant $\lambda_{h,n}$ such that
\begin{equation}
 F_{h,n}(q)=\lambda_{h,n}q^2+O_h(q).
\end{equation}
Thus exactly $h$ cardinalities are popular: the maximum has frequency of
order $q^4$, the other $h-1$ displayed values have frequency of order
$q^3$, and every remaining attainable value has frequency of order $q^2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.653034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6b996a13cd9872a316d03ce8e449f23117bec9f023088cb04148a86778655b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Hold-and-jump product-form representation) of arXiv:2607.24533, 'Colored Markov-Modulated Brownian Motion for Preemptive Workload Stacks'] {Suppose that Assumptions~\ref{assump:intensity}, \ref{assump:stability}, and~\ref{assump:first-moment} hold. Then the hold-and-jump model admits a stationary probability law.} On the face $\Theta_{c_1,\dots,c_n}$, the stationary density row vector
of the hold-and-jump model is
\begin{equation}

\vect{\pi}^{\mathrm H}(\bm{x})
=
\vect{p}^{\mathrm H}_0
\Lop{0}{c_1}(x_{c_1})
\prod_{r=1}^{n-1}
\Lop{c_r}{c_{r+1}}(x_{c_{r+1}}).
\end{equation}
The boundary mass vector $\vect{p}^{\mathrm H}_0$ satisfies
\begin{equation}

\vect{p}^{\mathrm H}_0\widetilde{\vect T}^{(0)}
=
\vect{p}^{\mathrm H}_0
\left(
\vect{T}^{(0)}
+
\sum_{c=1}^C\Rop{0}{c}
\right)
=
\vect{0}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, with
\begin{align*}
\mathfrak{M}^{\mathrm H}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\vect{0} & \vect{M}^{(1,2)} & \cdots & \vect{M}^{(1,C)}\\
\vect{0} & \vect{0} & \cdots & \vect{M}^{(2,C)}\\
\vdots & \ddots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\vect{0} & \cdots & \vect{0} & \vect{0}
