=
1.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:41:28.173419+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6bab3d680eefadd134cff3f80c6dd3c1cf7d640ce98c94f504cd17a5c4bfae3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D_Kr_tree of arXiv:2510.26724, 'Sharp Fuss-Catalan thresholds in graph bootstrap percolation'] We call a graph $T$ a {\it $K_r$-tree}
(or a {\it clique tree})
of {\it order} $\vartheta$ if it is 
the union of $\vartheta$
many copies $H_1,\ldots,H_\vartheta$ of $K_r$, 
and that, 
for every $1<i\leqslant \vartheta$, $H_i$ shares exactly 
one edge with $H_1 \cup\cdots \cup H_{i-1}$.
We call an edge $e\in E(G)$ an {\it internal edge} 
of $T$ if it is contained in at least two of the copies of $K_r$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.577764+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c28f1ef61145861068066cd48f8d3e80ac31ec538534f9a936dd8886862515","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 40 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] A \textit{current} $\n$ on $G_\fg$ is a function defined on the edge set $E_\fg$ and taking its values in $\mathbb N=\lbrace 0,1,\ldots\rbrace$. We let $\Omega_{G_\fg}$ be the set of currents on $G_\fg$. The set of \emph{sources} of $\n$, denoted by $\sn$, is defined as
\begin{equation}
\sn:=\Big\{ x \in V_\fg \: : \: \sum_{y\in V_\fg:\: xy\in E_\fg}\n_{xy}\textup{ is odd}\Big\}.
\end{equation}
We also define, for every $\beta\geq 0$ and $\mathsf{h}\in (\mathbb R^+)^V$, 
\begin{equation}
w_{\beta,\mathsf{h}}^G(\n):=\prod_{\substack{xy\in E}}\dfrac{\beta^{\n_{xy}}}{\n_{xy}!}\prod_{x\in V}\frac{\mathsf{h}_x^{\n_{x\fg}}}{\n_{x\fg}!}.
\end{equation}
If $\mathsf{h}=0$, we simply write $w_{\beta}^G(\n)=w_{\beta,0}^G(\n)$.  Similarly, we define $\Omega_G$, the set of currents on $G$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.245684+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6c80137abc249b680d0242c63c3a6588bd26ad1a9a32accbae69a85da31f433","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 16 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Given $H_+,H_-:\mX_{a,b,c}\mapsto \mathbb R$, with ${H_-}\le {H_+}$ pointwise,
  let $\mathcal P^+[H_-,H_+]$ denote the collection of  probability distributions $\mu$ on $\Omega_{H_\pm}$ that are increasing with respect to  $\pi_{H_\pm}$: 
  \begin{eqnarray}

   \eta\ge \eta'\Rightarrow {\mu(\eta)}\ge \mu(\eta').
  \end{eqnarray}
  When $H_+$ and $H_-$ are the height functions of the maximal and minimal configurations in $\Omega^N_{a,b,c}$, respectively, we write $\mathcal P^+$ instead of $\mathcal P^+[H_-,H_+]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:17:04.313315+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6cacd5b53b2f418c651617470422421f3f42aaa056bb0341f37cf796972eab9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem WP_H^2 of arXiv:2606.00325, 'Ergodicity for a class of stochastic Hasegawa-Mima equations with dissipation'] Assume that $A$ satisfies (ass2 of the paper), $v\in C^\infty_c(\mathcal{D})$ and the noise coefficients $(f_k)_{k=1}^M \subset L^2(\mathcal{D})$. Then for every $\phi_0 \in H^2 \cap V$ let $\phi_t$ be the unique solution of (eq:main of the paper) in the sense of (def-sol-H1 of the paper). Then the process $\xi = \phi -\Delta \phi - v$ belongs to $C(0, T; L^2) \cap L^2(0, T; Y) $ almost surely and it verifies, for every $\psi \in Y$
    \begin{align}
        \left\langle\xi_t, \psi\right\rangle - \left\langle\xi_0, \psi\right\rangle = -\int_0^t \brak{B^{1/2 }\xi_s, B^{1/2} \psi}ds + \int_0^t\left\langle \xi_s, \nabla^\perp \phi \cdot \nabla \psi\right\rangleds + \int_0^t \left\langle\xi_s , dW_t\right\rangle.
    \end{align}
    Moreover it satisfies the following energy balance
    \begin{align}
    \mathbb{E}[|\xi(t)|^2] - \mathbb{E}[|\xi_0|^2]
     +2\int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\|\xi\|_{Y}^2\right]dt = 2 \int_0^t\mathbb{E}\left[\brak{B^{1/2} \xi, B^{1/2} v} \right]dt+ \sigma t,
\end{align}
    where $\sigma = \sum_{k=1}^M \sigma_k^2|f_j|^2$. Moreover, $\xi$ is a Markov process in $L^2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.414344+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6d73bb1ce7f15392fa7b9eb4412f5c31dff1545d43cd275a77d69d0a9503bde","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 79 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Suppose Assumptions~\ref{Assumptions_Theta}, \ref{Assumptions_basic} and \ref{Assumptions_offcrit} hold. For any $\zeta_2 \in \big(0,\frac{\mathfrak{b} - \mathfrak{a}}{2}\big)$, there exist $c>0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, and $\zeta_1 \in (0,\zeta_2)$ (which may depend on all the data) such that
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{x\in [\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_2, \mathfrak{b}]} \big(V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(x) -
V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1)\big) \leq c
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1)=\sup_{x\in [\mathfrak{a}+\zeta_1,\mathfrak{b}]}
V^{\textnormal{eff}}_h(x).
\end{equation}
Moreover, there exist $\zeta_1^\pm$ depending only on $\zeta_2$ and the constants in the
assumptions such that the choice of $\zeta_1$ can be made to obey $0<\zeta_1^-<\zeta_1<\zeta_1^+<\zeta_2$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731711+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e222684db5e5aac679a86aab3060841923ea86706f2ab12ddb0ecece1658e8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Fix $0<\kappa \le \kappa_0$ for $\kappa_0$ sufficiently\footnote{For example, $\kappa_0 = 1/100$ would do} small. We write $\mfT$ for the regularity structure generated by the noise types $\mathfrak{L}_- = \{\<b0>, \<p0>, \<0t>\}$ and kernel types $\mfL_+ = \{\<k>, \<bk>, \<pk>\}$ where the rule (in the sense of \cite[Definition 5.7]{BHZ}) is the one naturally associated to the system \eqref{eq:main_sys}. In particular, each $\mathcal{I}_{\diamond}$ for $\diamond \in \mfL_+$ is a degree $2$ integration operator and
\begin{equ}
	|\<b0>|_\fs = |\<p0>|_\fs = - 3/2 - \kappa, \qquad |\<0t>|_\fs = - 2- \kappa.
\end{equ}
Further, we write $\mcT$ for the canonical basis of the model space of $\mfT$, which is therefore henceforth denoted by $\scal{\mcT}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:07:15.754750+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6e59c6fdda898892b9b9126c98b37a234785edc006515a1cd1e4a22feb26c14","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:abstract-common-domain of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] The operator $\widehat A_{\tau,f}$ is associated in $L^2(\mu)$ with
\begin{equation}
 a_{\tau,f}(u,v)
 =\mathcal E(e^{-\kappa\tau f/2}u,e^{-\kappa\tau f/2}v),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{Dom} a_{\tau,f}=V_\mu.

\end{equation}
For every $T>0$, the form norms
\[
 \left\|u\right\|_{\tau,f}^2
 :=a_{\tau,f}(u,u)+\left\|u\right\|_{L^2(\mu)}^2
\]
are uniformly equivalent for $|\tau|\le T$: there are
$0<c_{T,f}\le C_{T,f}<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
 c_{T,f}\left\|u\right\|_{\mathcal E,\mu}
 \le \left\|u\right\|_{\tau,f}
 \le C_{T,f}\left\|u\right\|_{\mathcal E,\mu},
 \qquad |\tau|\le T.

\end{equation}
For $u,v\inV_\mu$,
$\tau\mapsto a_{\tau,f}(u,v)$ is real analytic and
\begin{equation}
 \dot{a}_{0,f}(u,v)
 =-\frac\kappa2\mathcal E(fu,v)-\frac\kappa2\mathcal E(u,fv).

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.033109+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e6f10263858cd320063162159f6feb6dd3a823331b2d550ab03d7a9fd383d2ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2605.03057, 'Uniform-in-time quantitative fluctuations of large scale interacting particle systems'] Assume that Assumptions \ref{ass:smooth},
\ref{ass:second-malliavin-uniform}, \ref{ass:flow-smothness} and \ref{ass:coeff-level-new} hold,  and let
$\varphi\in C_b^2\left(\R^d\right)$.
For $ t \ge 0 $, define
\begin{align*}
\Delta_{N,t}
&=
\iint_{A_t\times A_t}
\sqrt{
\E\left(
\left(
D^2F_t^N\left(\varphi\right)\otimes_1
D^2F_t^N\left(\varphi\right)
\right)\left(x,y\right)^2
\right)
}
\\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
\sqrt{
\E\left(
DF_t^N\left(\varphi\right)\left(x\right)^2
DF_t^N\left(\varphi\right)\left(y\right)^2
\right)
}
\lambda_t\left(dx\right)\lambda_t\left(dy\right),
\end{align*}
where
$A_t=\left[0,t\right]\times\left\{1,\dots,N\right\}\times\left\{1,\dots,m\right\}$,
and
\begin{equation*}
\lambda_t\left(ds,dj,d\alpha\right)=ds\sum_{j=1}^N\sum_{\alpha=1}^m
\delta_{\left(j,\alpha\right)}.
\end{equation*}
Then, there exists a constant $ C > 0 $, independent of $ t $ and $ N $, such that
\begin{equation}

\sup_{t \ge 0} \Delta_{N,t}\le \frac{C}{N}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:22:59.740127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e70ae8c5520e8d7c1baea6abfe9ee6d4938f0e6bb36e88b6bf023914f3cf56b0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem slconv of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] Suppose we have a sequence of $\Phi$-horizontal $C^0$-foliations $\mathcal{G}_k$, $k \geq 0$, on $\mathbb{T}^2$, converging to a $\Phi$-horizontal foliation $\mathcal{G}$ in the $C^0_\mathrm{Fol}$ topology. Furthermore, assume that each $\mathcal{G}_k$ is Reebless with slope $s_k \in \mathbb{R}$, and that $(s_k)_k$ converges to some slope $s_\infty \in \mathbb{R}$.
    Then $\mathcal{G}$ is admissible, and either
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\mathcal{G}$ is of $\aleph$ type, or
        \item $\mathcal{G}$ has no Reeb annuli and has slope $s = s_\infty$.
    \end{itemize}

    Moreover, in the second case, if $(s_k)_k$ is strictly increasing (resp.~decreasing), then $\fs(\mathcal{G}) \in \{s^\natural, s^\flat\}$ (resp.~$\fs(\mathcal{G}) \in \{s^\natural, s^\sharp\}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:47:07.705080+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e712b68dff38c72e07d4089fc509bbbe54357abbb2f930d44c4e62d381a49057","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2608.06038, 'Efron type identities\\ for stopping sets and Poisson hulls'] Let $B\in\cX_0$, $k\in\N$ and $l\in\N_0$. Then
  \begin{displaymath}
    \BE \eta_{[\eta]}(B)^{(l)}\partial\eta(B)^{(k)}
    =\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\binom{k}{j}(-1)^j
    \BE\int_{B^{k-j}} \lambda_B([\eta+\delta_{\bx}])^l
    \lambda_B\big([\eta+\delta_{\bx}]\setminus[\eta]\big)^j
    \,(\lambda_{[\eta]^c})^{k-j}(d\bx).
  \end{displaymath}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.244813+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e725db714098945970087a4bd89b31bf8e1d0a6217ee7c15fcff549b35342c49","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 64 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Recall that $\rho=k/N$, $\alpha=1-2\rho$, as well as $\varepsilon=\varepsilon(N,k)=N^{-3/2}\beta^{3/2}$ for $\beta=\beta(\alpha)$ from \eqref{def:betaASEP}. For all $i\in \lbr M \rbr$, $z\in \R$, $t \geq 0$, and periodic initial data $ h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$, we define the \textbf{rescaled patched ASEP height function} as 
\begin{align*}
\hfix^{\textup{patch},\delta,\varepsilon}( \hfix^{\per,\varepsilon}_0;z,t) := \sigma(\alpha)^{-1}\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{3}}&\Big(2\mu(\alpha)\varepsilon^{-1}t +\mu^{\prime}(\alpha)\beta(\alpha)z\varepsilon^{-\frac{2}{3}} \\
&- h^{\textup{patch},\delta}( h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0,0;2\alpha t \varepsilon^{-1} + \beta(\alpha)z\varepsilon^{-\frac{2}{3}},2(1-q)^{-1}\varepsilon^{-1}t) \Big) 
\end{align*} whenever the argument for $h^{\textup{patch},\delta}$ is an integer, and linear interpolation otherwise. Here, we obtain $\hfix^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$ from $h^{\per,\varepsilon}_0$ as in \eqref{def:RescaledPeriodicASEPheight}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.441230+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e72667d78fc3fc64cac662f8a893593743ee4b2ae3b1864aabaf9cdfe8427ae1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 40 of arXiv:2604.07370, 'Probabilistic Weyl law for twisted Toeplitz matrices with rough symbols'] Let $p=p(x,\xi)$ be of the form
    \begin{equation}
        p(x,\xi)=\sum_{k\in \Lambda}p_k(x)e^{2i\pi \ps{k}{\xi}}, \quad(x,\xi)\in [0,1]^d\times \T^d,
    \end{equation}
    where $\Lambda=\prod_{j=1}^d\disc{-n_j}{m_j}$, $n_j,m_j\geq 0$, and $p_k:[0,1]^d\to \C$, $k\in \Lambda$ is measurable. Let us denote 
    \begin{equation*}
        S:=\sum_{j=1}^d (n_j+m_j)>0\mm\m\m\text{and}\mm\m\m \Xi:=\prod_{j=1}^d(n_j+m_j+1).
    \end{equation*}
    We assume that: there exists $m>0$ such that for almost every $x\in [0,1]^d$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \sum_{\substack{k\in \Lambda\\k\neq 0}}^{N_+}\bb{p_k(x)}\geq m>0.
    \end{equation}
    Then, for all $z\in \C$, $t\geq 0$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathrm{L}\pr{\acc{(x,\xi)\in [0,1]^d\times \T^d\m;\m \bb{p(x,\xi)-z}^2\leq t}}\leq Ct^\kappa
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}
        \kappa=\frac{1}{2S}\mm\text{and}\mm C= 14d\pr{\frac{\sqrt{\Xi}}{m}}^{\frac{1}{S}}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:59:25.042060+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e729f463100923aa7c1ac739ff6bf79240e0b5916fbc901800a0a7c79d344652","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 20 of arXiv:2606.31596, 'The Invariant Measure of Multiscale Markov Chains via Fast Arborescence Factorization'] Assume reversibility $\eqref{eq:ydetail}$ of the rates $r_x$.
