the closed, densely defined Dirichlet form $(\mathcal E,V_x)$, where
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
  \mathcal E(u,v)&=\frac1{2\pi}\int_D\nabla u\cdot\nabla v\,dz,
  \qquad u,v\inV_x,\\
  V_x&=\{u\in H_0^1(D):\widetilde u\in L^2(M_x)\}.
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:10.806652+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea321e2fa2c72757dc17f05dd3b81c66e1708e0ecd648c8796d07666f815b39f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 5 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $t>0$ and any $0<a\le b\le1$, the probability distribution $P^\varepsilon_c( \cdot \, : \, t,a,b)$ over $\mathbb N_0$ converges in law to a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
f(0) \, t\,  \lambda_* ( [a,b]) = f(0)\,  t \, \int_a^b \cfrac{dx}{x^2} = f(0) \, t \,  (a^{-1}- b^{-1}) \  . 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, we have that, for any $n\ge 0$, the function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n^\varepsilon: r \in[0,\infty) \mapsto \mathbb P ( e_1^\varepsilon \ge r, N^\varepsilon_{(0,r]} ([a,b]) = n) \in [0,1]
\end{split}
\end{equation}
converges uniformly on any compact interval $[0,t]$, $t>0$, as $\varepsilon$ vanishes to 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n: r \in [0,\infty) \mapsto e^{-r f(0)} e^{-r \lambda_* ([a,b]} \frac{(r \lambda_* ([a,b]))^n}{n!} \in [0,1] \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859705+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea3f808c9b06a8dd680d0963729574b855d0d8830d94fbc52e2a1a6ce30c861f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem overtake-bound of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that $(X_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$, $(X'_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$ and $Y$ are mutually independent random variables, with $(X_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}} \sim (X'_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}}$. Suppose that, for $k \in \mathbb{N}_{0}$, there exists $\lambda > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation} 
    \prod_{i=k}^{\infty}\E{e^{\lambda  (X'_{i} - X_{i})}} < \infty.  
    \end{equation}
    Then, for any $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$
    \begin{equation} 
        \Prob{\exists j \in \mathbb{N}\colon Y + \sum_{i=1}^{k + j} X_{i} \leq \sum_{i=k}^{k+j} X'_{i}} \leq \left(\prod_{i=k}^{\infty}\E{e^{\lambda  (X'_{i} - X_{i})}}\right) \E{e^{- \lambda \left(Y + \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} X_{i}\right)}}.
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:44:17.167850+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea48a7454c3e54706543c28aa7d8ea5583d66df3a619f1e69b05c6855c697c05","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2604.17973, 'Schauder estimates and classical solutions of the Dirichlet problem for stochastic parabol'] Let $u$ be a quasi-classical solution of \eqref{eq:model-half-space}--\eqref{eq:model-half-space-bc} with $g\equiv0$. Assume that
\[
f\in C_x^{\alpha}(\bar Q_T^+;L_\omega^\gamma),
\qquad
f|_{\Sigma_T}\in C_{x,t}^{\alpha,\alpha/2}(\Sigma_T;L_\omega^\gamma),
\qquad
f(0,\cdot)|_{\partial\R_+^n}=0.
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
|D_x^2u|_{(\alpha,\alpha/2);Q_T^+}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
\le C\Bigl(
|u|_{0;Q_T^+}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
+|f|_{\alpha;Q_T^+}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
+|f|_{(\alpha,\alpha/2);\Sigma_T}^{L_\omega^\gamma}
\Bigr),
\end{equation}
where $C$ depends only on $n$, $\alpha$, $\gamma$, $\kappa$, and $K$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.572947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea495541c5f289b1bd4d949d6e93f2d4da80d1c6bcbc0eaac53681d75f00d709","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 21 (Domain Markov property) of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d\geq 2$ and $\beta\geq 0$. Let $G=(V,E),H=(V',E')$ be subgraphs of $\mathbb Z^d$ with $G\subset H$. Let $\xi\in \{0,1\}^{E'\setminus E}$. Then, for every event $\mathcal A\subset \{0,1\}^E$, one has
 \begin{equation}
 	\phi_{H,\beta}^0[\mathcal A \: |\: \omega_{|E'\setminus E}=\xi]=\phi_{G,\beta}^\xi[\mathcal A].
 \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:59:53.385951+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea4a87c8565655625edf776bba47275ee97891974cd25b43fdb28036ddb04342","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition appendix:zeros:integer_beta of arXiv:2608.18320, 'Asymptotics for Beta-Splitting Trees via Homogeneous Fragmentations and Meromorphic Potent'] [Zeros and poles for positive integer $\beta$]

        Let \(\beta>0\)  be an integer. Then 
        \begin{enumeratei} 
            \item the function \(f_\beta\) has exactly \(\beta+1\) simple zeros and \(\beta+1\) simple poles, the latter being exactly \(\{-\beta,-\beta+1,\dots, 0 \}\);

            \item if \(\beta\) is even, then \(f_\beta\) has a unique real zero at \(w=\beta+2\), and \(\beta\) nonreal zeros, which appear in conjugate pairs;

            \item if \(\beta\) is odd, then \(w=\beta+2\) and \(w=-2(\beta+1)\) are the real zeroes of \(f_\beta\), and its remaining \(\beta-1\) zeros are nonreal and appear in conjugate pairs;

            \item  every zero \(w_0\) of \(f_\beta\) satisfies 
             \[
                \operatorname{Re}(w_0)\in\left[-2(\beta+1),\beta+2\right],
                \qquad\text{and}\qquad
                \left|\operatorname{Im}(w_0)\right|<\left(3\beta+4\right)/{2}.
        \]    
        \end{enumeratei}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:28:34.963585+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ea5a237c6776530ead41f02cc6f42071aced0c926419d501bcacba7cf63979da","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Teo_Krylov of arXiv:2412.05950, 'Quantitative particle approximation of nonlinear stochastic Fokker-Planck equations with s'] Assume $(\mathbf{A}^c)$ and one of the two regimes:
				\begin{enumerate}
					\item $(\mathbf{A}^F)$ and $(\mathbf{A}^K)$.
					\item $(\mathbf{A}^I)$ and $(\mathbf{A}^K)$.
									\end{enumerate}
				Let $0 \le \rho_0 \in L^1 \cap H^{1-\frac{2}{q}}_q\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)$, with $\Arrowvert \rho_0 \Arrowvert_{1}=1$. For each $\kappa>0$, there exists a $T>0$ depending on $\kappa,\lambda, q, C_K, L, M$ and $d$ such that if $\Arrowvert \rho_0 \Arrowvert_{q} = \kappa$,  the SPDE ((SPDE_Ito of the paper)) admits a unique nonnegative solution in
				\begin{align*}
					\mathbb{M} \doteq L_{\mathcal{F}^B}^q\left([0, T]; H_q^{1}\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)\right) \cap S_{\mathcal{F}^B}^{\infty}\left([0, T] ; L^1 \cap L^q\left(\mathbb{T}^d\right)\right).
