{"assumptions":["M is a compact Riemannian manifold; f is a C^{1+a} diffeomorphism of M with a > 0 (Holder derivative)","mu is an f-invariant Borel probability measure","the conclusion is stated at mu-almost every point having at least one negative Lyapunov exponent","eps > 0 is fixed small relative to the largest negative exponent; C(x) and r(x) are measurable, r tempered","no claim is made at points with only non-negative exponents, in zero-exponent directions, or about absolute continuity of the lamination"],"created_at":"2026-09-12T19:09:43.732688+00:00","domain":"smooth ergodic theory, non-uniform hyperbolicity, Pesin theory","id":"claim_eabc8d2a11c5f96e9cd3d9d0ae8e4274abc626af17b4f59d7b9ce92e1510c03b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"Let M be a compact Riemannian manifold and let f be a C^{1+a} diffeomorphism of M for some a > 0 (that is, Df is Holder continuous with exponent a). Let mu be an f-invariant Borel probability measure, and for mu-a.e. x let lambda_1(x) > ... > lambda_{k(x)}(x) be the Lyapunov exponents with the Oseledets splitting. Write E^s(x) for the sum of the Oseledets subspaces with NEGATIVE exponent, d_s(x) = dim E^s(x), and lambda^s(x) = the largest negative exponent at x. Fix a small eps > 0.

Then for mu-a.e. x with d_s(x) > 0 there exist a measurable function r(x) > 0 and an embedded C^{1+a} disk W^s_loc(x) of dimension d_s(x), contained in the ball of radius r(x) around x and TANGENT TO E^s(x) at x, such that:
 (i) for all y in W^s_loc(x) and all n >= 0,  d(f^n x, f^n y) <= C(x) e^{(lambda^s(x) + eps) n} d(x, y), with C(x) measurable and finite; in particular limsup (1/n) log d(f^n x, f^n y) <= lambda^s(x) < 0;
 (ii) f( W^s_loc(x) ) is contained in W^s_loc(f x);
 (iii) x -> W^s_loc(x) is measurable, and the size is TEMPERED: r(f^n x) >= r(x) e^{-eps |n|} for all n, so the disks may shrink along the orbit but only subexponentially;
 (iv) on each Pesin block Lambda_l the constants C, r and the estimates are uniform and the disks vary continuously in the C^1 topology with the base point.
The global stable manifold W^s(x), defined as the increasing union over n >= 0 of f^{-n}( W^s_loc(f^n x) ), is then an injectively immersed C^{1+a} submanifold consisting of points whose forward orbit converges exponentially to that of x. The unstable statement follows by applying this to f^{-1}.

SHARP EDGES. (1) The Holder hypothesis on Df is NOT cosmetic: the theorem is false for merely C^1 diffeomorphisms -- Pugh constructed a C^1 diffeomorphism (in dimension 4) with non-zero exponents for which the conclusion fails. The Holder exponent is what makes the nonlinear remainder in Lyapunov charts small enough to run the graph transform against a chart size that shrinks subexponentially. (2) r(x) CANNOT be taken uniformly positive: it is only tempered, and any argument that needs a uniform size must work on a Pesin block and pay for the fact that the block is not invariant. (3) Nothing is asserted at points where all exponents are >= 0, or in directions with ZERO exponent. (4) The family of stable manifolds is measurable, not continuous, on the whole space; continuity holds only along Pesin blocks. (5) Absolute continuity of the stable lamination (needed for Pesin's entropy formula and for ergodic components) is a FURTHER theorem, also requiring C^{1+a}; it is not part of this statement and must be cited separately.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:29:21.916389+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eac6ce50c2011e959cc78c2e7a4ec64ae0037ca3e108d70f4c3f5625ab9553ef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 11 of arXiv:2608.01419, 'A model of opinion dynamics evolving via a preferential attachment mechanism involving mul'] Consider the stochastic approximation process in \eqref{sa_borkar_general}, with $\delta_{n}=\mathbf{0}$ for each $n$. In addition to Assumptions~\eqref{borkar_1}, \eqref{borkar_2} and \eqref{borkar_4}, we assume  
\begin{enumerate}[label=(B'\arabic*), ref=B'\arabic*]
\item  that $F$ is continuously differentiable, and its Jacobian $J_{F}$ is uniformly Lipschitz,
\item  that $\alpha:=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\{a_{n+1}^{-1}-a_{n}^{-1}\}$ exists and is finite,
\item  that $\sup_{n}\E[||Z_{n}||^{4}]<\infty$,
\item  that for some $K>0$, and some $Q:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}$ such that $Q(\mathbf{x})$ is a non-negative definite symmetric matrix for each $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\E\left[\Delta M_{n+1}\Delta M_{n+1}^{T}\big|\mathcal{F}_{n}\right]=Q(Z_{n}) \text{ and } \E\left[\left|\left|\Delta M_{n+1}\right|\right|^{4}\big|\mathcal{F}_{n}\right]\leqslant K\left(1+||Z_{n}||^{4}\right),\nonumber
\end{equation}
where $A^{T}$, for any matrix $A$ with real entries, indicates its transpose.
\end{enumerate}
Fix \emph{any} $T>0$. Writing $t_{0}=0$ and $t_{n}=\sum_{i\in[n]}a_{i}$ for $n\in\mathbb{N}$, we set $m_{n}=\min\{m\geqslant n: t_{m}\geqslant t_{n}+T\}$ for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$. For each fixed $n\in\mathbb{N}$, let $\{Z^{n}(t): t\geqslant t_{n}\}$ indicate the solution to
\begin{equation}
\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)=F(\mathbf{x}(t)) \text{ for all }t\geqslant t_{n}, \quad \text{with} \quad \mathbf{x}(t_{n})=Z_{n},\nonumber
\end{equation}
and let $W_{j}=a_{j+1}^{-1/2}\{Z_{j}-Z^{n}(t_{j})\}$ for each $j\geqslant n$. We now define the process $\{W^{n}(t):t\in [t_{n},t_{n}+T]\}$: 
\begin{equation}
W^{n}(t)=W_{j}+\frac{t-t_{j}}{t_{j+1}-t_{j}}(W_{j+1}-W_{j}) \text{ for }t\in[t_{j},t_{j+1}], \text{ for each }j\in\{n,n+1,\ldots,m_{n}-1\}.\nonumber  
\end{equation}  
Finally, we set $\tilde{W}^{n}(t)=W^{n}(t_{n}+t)$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Likewise, we define $\tilde{Z}^{n}(t)=Z^{n}(t_{n}+t)$ for all $t\in[0,T]$. Then the laws of the processes $\{(\tilde{Z}^{n}(\cdot),\tilde{W}^{n}(\cdot))\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ are relatively compact in the space $\mathcal{P}(C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^{d}))^{2}$, where $C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^{d})$ is the set of all continuous functions from $[0,T]$ to $\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and $\mathcal{P}(C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^{d}))^{2}$ is the space of all probability measures on $C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^{d})\times C([0,T];\mathbb{R}^{d})$.

If a subsequence of $\{(\tilde{Z}^{n}(\cdot),\tilde{W}^{n}(\cdot))\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converges in law to a limiting process $\{z^{*}(\cdot),w^{*}(\cdot)\}$, then $z^{*}(\cdot)$ is a solution to the ODE $\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)=F(\mathbf{x}(t))$ for $t\geqslant 0$, belonging to a compact, connected, internally chain transitive invariant set corresponding to this ODE, and each of
\begin{align}
&w^{*}(t)-\int_{0}^{t}\left(J_{F}\left(z^{*}(s)\right)+\frac{\alpha}{2}I_{d}\right)w^{*}(s)ds \quad \text{and}\nonumber\\
&\left[w^{*}(t)-\int_{0}^{t}\left(J_{F}\left(z^{*}(s)\right)+\frac{\alpha}{2}I_{d}\right)w^{*}(s)ds\right]\left[w^{*}(t)-\int_{0}^{t}\left(J_{F}\left(z^{*}(s)\right)+\frac{\alpha}{2}I_{d}\right)w^{*}(s)ds\right]^{T}-\int_{0}^{t}Q\left(z^{*}(s)\right)ds,\nonumber
\end{align}
is a martingale for $t\in[0,T]$, where $I_{d}$ indicates the $d$-dimensional identity matrix. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.133084+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_eacb74dc6a2fe7f592546727fe7d8c08ee31d4e60b51da40a5ae0fbd8e6416c7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor FDkSk of arXiv:2506.18207, 'Cartan's Path Development, the Logarithmic Signature and a Conjecture of Lyons-Sidorova'] Let $a_{1},\cdots,a_{m}\in\mathbb{C}$ be given
