{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:29:21.916113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebb121ee841d72e5b919ad7b7e0104855602c74f28c5ede1f9fa48204ac9a8bd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2608.01419, 'A model of opinion dynamics evolving via a preferential attachment mechanism involving mul'] Consider the stochastic approximation process $\{Z_{n}\}$, with $Z_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$, given by
\begin{equation}
Z_{n+1}=Z_{n}+a_{n+1}\left\{F(Z_{n})+\Delta M_{n+1}+\delta_{n}\right\} \text{ for }n\in\mathbb{N}_{0},
\end{equation}
with prescribed $Z_{0}$ and the following assumptions:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(B\arabic*), ref=B\arabic*]
\item  the map $F:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d}$ is Lipschitz,
\item  the step-size sequence $\{a_{n}\}$, of positive scalars, satisfies $\sum_{n}a_{n}=\infty$ and $\sum_{n}a_{n}^{2}<\infty$,
\item  the sequence $\{\Delta M_{n+1}\}$ is a martingale difference sequence with respect to the filtration $\{\mathcal{F}_{n+1}\}$, where $\mathcal{F}_{n}$ is the $\sigma$-field consisting of all information about the process up to and including epoch $n$, and $\{\delta_{n}\}$ is a sequence of additional error terms adapted to $\{\mathcal{F}_{n}\}$, such that $\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}a_{i+1}(\Delta M_{i+1}+\delta_{i})$ converges almost surely as $n\rightarrow\infty$, 
\item  and $\sup\{||Z_{n}||:n\in\mathbb{N}_{0}\}<\infty$ almost surely, where $||\cdot||$ indicates the usual Euclidean norm in $\mathbb{R}^{d}$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, almost surely, $\{Z_{n}\}$ converges to a (possibly sample path dependent) compact connected internally chain transitive invariant set corresponding to the autonomous ODE $\dot{x}(t)=F(x(t))$ for $t\geqslant 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:55:18.051120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebc132869546e1289399efa916caeccab026f40cf7088913c4e93bc886acf5f1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 14 of arXiv:2606.16164, 'A Low-Regularity Semigroup Sewing Lemma via Quotient Structures'] Let $h\in Z\hat C(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)$. Then the map
\[
\mathcal T_h:\{R\in C(\Delta_T^2;B_\alpha) \mid \hat\delta R=h\}\to \{\text{compatible frozen increment families over }h\}
\]
defined by $\mathcal T_h(R):=(\Theta_\tau R)_{\tau\in[0,T]}$ is a bijection. Its inverse is given by
\begin{equation}

(r^\tau)_{\tau\in[0,T]}\mapsto R, \quad R_{ts}:=r^t_{ts}.
\end{equation}
In addition, if $\gamma\in(0,1)$ and $h\in Z\hat C^{\gamma}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)$, then
$\mathcal T_h$ restricts to a bijection between $ \{R\in E^{\gamma}(\Delta_T^2; B_\alpha) \mid \hat\delta R=h\}$ and the compatible frozen increment families over $h$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

\sup_{\tau\in[0,T]}\|r^\tau\|_{\gamma-\varepsilon,\alpha+\varepsilon;\tau}<\infty, \quad \forall \varepsilon\in(0, \gamma).
\end{equation}
Likewise, if $h\in Z\hat C^{1}(\Delta_T^3;B_\alpha)$, then $\mathcal T_h$ restricts to a bijection between $\{R\in E_{\log}^{1}(\Delta_T^2; B_\alpha) \mid \hat\delta R=h\}$ and the compatible frozen increment families satisfying
\begin{equation}

\sup_{\tau\in[0,T]}\|r^\tau\|_{1,\log;\tau,\alpha}+\sup_{\tau\in[0,T]}\|r^\tau\|_{1-\varepsilon,\alpha+\varepsilon;\tau}<\infty, \quad \forall \varepsilon\in(0,1).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.580575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ebd9b320c1571e1cf97241e4ba1bbd45ecc121d0bdf5fd614028be6ab64c78e9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 1 (Center accuracy after local calibration) of arXiv:2609.01319, 'Matched Queries for Curvature and Density at Branching Junctions'] Fix smooth localized score tests around a bounded reference offset. If the resulting exact tangent-model least-squares matrix has full column rank, then using the finite-noise score from \cref{thm:expansion} returns a physical center of the form
