{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.726329+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec8f2ebeb8822374178f4f8112ba4962d4276eba908429fd76b8215ea4edf43c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] Let $L:C^{0,1}(\Omega_T)\to\mathbb{R}$ be any linear functional and recall 
    \begin{equation*}
    \mathfrak{M}(H):=\int_0^T\int_0^1\sigma(\rho_t)|\nabla H_t|^2\;dx\;dt+\int_0^T\Psi(t,H_t(0),H_t(1))\;dt.
\end{equation*}
Then 
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{C^{0,1}(\Omega_T)}\{L_0(H)-\mathfrak{M}(H)\}=S_1+S_2,
\end{equation*}
where
\begin{equation*}
    S_1=\sup_{C^{0,1}_0(\Omega_T)}\{L_0(G)-\int_0^T\int_0^1\sigma(\rho_t)|\nabla G_t|^2\;dx\;dt\}
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
    S_2=\sup_{h,g\in C([0,T])}\{L(g_t[1-\Xi_t])+L(h_t\Xi_t)-\int_0^T\zeta_t[h_t-g_t]^2\;dt-\int_0^T\Psi(t,g_t,h_t)\;dt\}.
\end{equation*}
In the above formula, $\zeta_t=1/\langle 1/\sigma(\rho_t) \rangle$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.975744+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ec9f30da070f3d729d52ce80122759752c693c4284d5322775376c03241c6af0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma def:local of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] We define for any smooth function $f:\R^{2}\rightarrow\R$ 
