{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.553090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed43e6dbab641d34f24da9fdb597048fbad7c07589be7e2458b478da5603d8a7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2608.27156, 'Traveling Waves in Equity Markets with\\Rank-Based Entry and Exit'] Assume $\lambda_b, \lambda_d, b \in C^1([0,1])$ and $\sigma^2 \in C^1([0,1])$ with $\min_{[0,1]} \sigma^2 > 0$, and let the initial positions be independent samples from a distribution on $(0,\infty)$ with continuous CDF $u_0$ and $\E|\log X^1_0| < \infty$. Set $m(t) := e^{f(1)t}$. Then for every $T > 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \sup_{t \leq T}\, \Big( \big|m^n_t - m(t)\big| + \sup_{x > 0}\, \big| F^n(t,x) - m(t)\, w(t,\log x) \big| \Big) \;\longrightarrow\; 0
    \quad \text{in probability as } n \to \infty,
\end{equation}
where $w$ is the unique CDF solution (in the sense of Lemma~\ref{lem:uniqueness} below), in the log-capitalization variable $y$, of the quasilinear reaction-diffusion equation
\begin{equation}
    \pp_t w \;=\; \pp^2_{yy} A(w) - \pp_y B(w) + \tilde f(w),
    \qquad w(0, y) = u_0(e^y),
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation*}
    A(q) \;=\; \int_0^q \frac{\sigma^2(r)}{2}\,\d r, \qquad
    B(q) \;=\; \int_0^q \tilde b(r)\,\d r, \qquad
    \tilde b \;=\; b - \frac{\sigma^2}{2}.
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.457208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed58dfa1e220a776b52046dc025851f2353cb67e78192335e67ffe5f6e6d05f2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 25 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] Let $\gamma \geq 1$, $0 \leq s \leq t$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}_+$. Then the expectation in \eqref{varxznear2order} is bounded from above by
	\begin{align}
		 \widehat{C} (t-s)^{2} \frac{\big[ \Theta(n) K_n \big]^{2} }{n^{4} } \Bigg\{1 + \int_1^{L_n} u^{2 - 2 \gamma} \; \dd u \Bigg\}. 
	\end{align} 
	Furthermore, if $H \in C_c^2(\mathbb{R})$, the role of $\widehat{C}$ is fulfilled by some constant $C(H)$ satisfying \eqref{Cinvshift}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.279247+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ed59eeb8a33c99e7d72e04a07414b09617a9a7f44d7f7ab0aa7d9291674322f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition pro: Quantile Coupling Lower Bound for SSQ of arXiv:2603.14163, '\LARGE \bf Tail Bounds for Queues with Abandonment: Constant, Moderate, Large Deviations, '] \textnormal{(Quantile Coupling Lower Bound for SSQ)} 
