Then $X_\infty=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty} \widetilde{X}_n$ and $X_\infty^+=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\widetilde{X}_n^+$ almost surely and in $L^p$ for every $p<3$.\footnote{It should be possible to prove $L^p$ convergence for all $p>0$, but we do not need it} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:14:37.643993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8ab3ccf772fb8fa97d779058606f5971d90b000669945415b1e20cb1b9d5b0f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.19179, 'A random walk on $p$-groups with a symmetric perfect pairing'] Suppose $1 \leq k \leq n$. If $M_{k,k}$ is non-singular, then there is a canonical isomorphism of groups equipped with a symmetric pairing:
\begin{equation}

V_k^{\perp} \Big/ V_k \cong \Ker(M_{k,k}) \times \Q_p^{n-k} 
\end{equation}
where the pairing on the right hand side is the sum of the pairing on $\Ker(M_{k,k})$, and some symmetric pairing on $\Q_p^{n-k}$. The pairing on the left hand side is the pairing induced by $M_{n,n}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:56:49.282666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f8af81b3a77c126757a172d93b0647ef0cc99cedfd1e3bcf4e1c75b41b775785","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 2 of arXiv:2606.22656, 'Fluctuations of Point Vortex Ensembles\\ at Small Negative Inverse Temperature'] For every $\beta_*<8\pi$ there exists $C_{\beta_*}<\infty$ such that, for all $0\leq \beta\leq \beta_*$ and $2\leq n\leq N$,
\begin{equation}
    \int_{\T^n}\exp\pa{\frac{\beta}{N}\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq n}|G(x_i-x_j)|} dx^n
    \leq C_{\beta_*}^{n}.
