{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.800286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fb6d241c94329d0e1a8fd2a3e98e9c8140686cb30be82635e08647e2fca81e96","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin 77 ($\epsilon$-cluster structure) of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] Let $\eps>0$ and $\bs{n}_K=(n_1, \dots, n_K)\in (\mathbb{N}^*)^K$ with $\ov{\bs{n}}_K=\sum_{k=1}^K n_k = N$. One says
that $\bs{x}_N \in \R^{N}_{<}$ has the $\epsilon$-cluster $\bs{n}_K$  if the following is true.
Define $i_1=1$ and $i_k = n_1 + \dots + n_{k-1}+1$ for $k\geq 2$, so that $x_{i_k}$ is the leftmost particle in the $k^{\e{th}}$ cluster.

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all $k = 1, \dots, K$,  $x_{i_k + j} - x_{i_k + j-1} \leq \epsilon$ for all $j = 1, \dots, n_k -1$.
\item For all $k = 2, \dots, K$,  $x_{i_{k}}  - x_{i_{k} -1} > \epsilon$.
\end{enumerate}
We let $\mc{E}_{\eps}( \bs{n}_K)$ be the set of $\bs{x}_N \in \R^{N}_{<}$ that have $\epsilon$-cluster $\bs{n}_K$.","status":"unverified"}

